/* The Kessler Court origin of the Snow War, shown by the Kessler Court Story mode.
 * Built by stories/build_kessler.py from Jill's doc "The Snow War: How It Started"
 * (English tab and Yoopanese tab). An invented story, not a real event. */
window.KESSLER_STORY = {"en": "<div class=\"ks-title\">The Snow War: How It Started</div>\n<p>The Snow War started on Kessler Court in Tulsa with a snowball nobody can prove was thrown, and reached its continental age in the Great Storm of February 2028: 34&quot; of thundersnow and 6&quot; of snowball snow, 74&quot; packable, Continental War (tier 208 of 327).</p>\n<p><em>An invented origin for the Snow War ladder, not a real event. Tier names and numbers come from the current 327-tier ladder.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-cast\">Cast</h2>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Age at start</th><th>House</th><th>Side</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tessa Oduya</td><td>10</td><td>#14</td><td>North</td><td>Drew the sketch; the machine&#39;s designer</td></tr><tr><td>Owen Brandt</td><td>10</td><td>#9, across the street</td><td>South</td><td>Threw, or didn&#39;t throw, the first snowball</td></tr><tr><td>Lila Brandt</td><td>13</td><td>#9</td><td>North</td><td>Owen&#39;s sister; keeper of the rules; fights against her brother</td></tr><tr><td>Marcus Oduya</td><td>14</td><td>#14</td><td>South</td><td>Tessa&#39;s brother; fights against his sister</td></tr><tr><td>Dev Rao</td><td>9</td><td>#11, the corner</td><td>North</td><td>The undecided third party; later the operator</td></tr><tr><td>Priya Rao</td><td>15</td><td>#11</td><td>Neutral</td><td>Dev&#39;s sister; &quot;doesn&#39;t care about any of this&quot;; judge; runs the printer</td></tr><tr><td>Anjali Rao</td><td>—</td><td>Juniper Lane</td><td>North</td><td>Rao cousin; first ammunition maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>The older siblings each fight against their own family. Both took the other side&#39;s account of November 24, and neither has ever changed it.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-part-one-before-snow\">Part One: Before Snow</h2>\n<p>The war&#39;s real cause is a throw on Tuesday, November 24, 2026 that may not have happened, packed with snow that may not have been anyone&#39;s to pack.</p>\n<p><strong>Saturday, September 12, 2026.</strong> The 2025–26 winter gave Tessa zero snow days. Tessa draws a machine on graph paper at four squares to the inch: a screw inside a trough, two wheels, a handle, and an arrow labelled &quot;snow goes here.&quot; One wheel is drawn larger than the other. The purpose, written in the corner and later lost: <em>to make it look like a snow day.</em> Snow on the ground: 0&quot;. <strong>(Sketch on Graph Paper)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>September 19 – November 14.</strong> The machine is printed on a borrowed printer and proven at the kitchen table:</p>\n<ul><li><p>acorns, September 27;</p></li><li><p>flour, October 3 (the kitchen is out of use for two days);</p></li><li><p>birdseed, October 10;</p></li><li><p>sand, October 17: 11 kg through the trough, and the wheels slip once in six pushes.</p></li></ul>\n<p>Owen watches the sand run from the curb and asks to push it. Tessa says no. Nobody records this. It is the first refusal in a war that will later be fought over spares.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuesday, November 24.</strong> A weak clipper overnight leaves <strong>0.2&quot;</strong> on car roofs and 0.1&quot; on grass at 31°F. It is gone by 10:40 am.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>7:08 am:</strong> Tessa scrapes two car roofs into the buffer and lays an 11-foot band down the #14 driveway, photographed from a low angle. <strong>(First Snow Contact)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>7:12 am:</strong> Owen crosses the street, scrapes <strong>Tessa&#39;s laid band</strong> bare-handed, and packs something about 1½&quot; across.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Owen:</strong> &quot;I threw it from 20 feet and hit Tessa&#39;s backpack.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>Tessa:</strong> &quot;It never hit. And it wasn&#39;t yours to throw. That was my snow.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>Witnesses:</strong> four at the bus stop. Marcus says it hit. Lila says it never left Owen&#39;s hand. The other two decline to say.</p></li><li><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> none. The backpack is dry, and by 7:14 there is no trace of the round.</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Recess, 10:15 am:</strong> Owen tries about 40 times to repeat it off the playground rails. Nothing holds. <strong>(Anecdotal → Rumored → Disputed)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<p>That morning leaves two questions no one on the block ever settles:</p>\n<ol><li><p><strong>Did the first snowball happen?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>If it did, whose snow was it: the sky&#39;s or the machine&#39;s?</strong></p></li></ol>\n<p>The siblings&#39; testimony sets the sides for good. Marcus, who says it hit, ends up on Owen&#39;s side. Lila, who says it never left Owen&#39;s hand, ends up on Tessa&#39;s.</p>\n<p><strong>November 25 – December 9.</strong> The question splits the bus stop, 5 kids to 4. Nothing is thrown, because there is nothing to throw. <strong>(No Ammo)</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-part-two-year-zero\">Part Two: Year Zero</h2>\n<p>The calendar starts at the first flake, 9:40 pm on Thursday, December 10, 2026. From that moment the North reaches every tier first, and the South follows. November 24 falls 16 days Before Snow, outside the reckoning.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-preparation-monday-december-7-thursday-december-10\">Preparation: Monday, December 7 – Thursday, December 10</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Monday, December 7.</strong> Models show a closed low swinging out of the Southwest. Dev, who has not picked a side, reads the forecast aloud at the bus stop to both of them.</p></li><li><p><strong>Tuesday, December 8.</strong> Tessa tests the machine in the Oduya garage on a bag of ice crushed with a hammer: a 6-ft band at 12 mm, even enough. She oils both wheels and the pinion.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wednesday, December 9.</strong> A Winter Storm Watch goes up for 2–4&quot;. Both sides recruit Dev all evening: Owen with his garage bat, Tessa with a turn pushing the machine. That night Lila carries her own gloves, hat and boots across the street to the Oduya mudroom, where they stay all winter.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, December 10.</strong> Dry gloves are counted on both sides of the street: the North has 9 pairs for 5 kids, the South 6 pairs for 6. Schools cancel Friday at 8:50 pm. At 9:40 pm, the first flake falls on the Kessler mailboxes, and the calendar starts.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> None yet. Every kid on the court goes to bed knowing exactly where they will stand at 7:00 am.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-friday-december-11-the-first-day-of-the-war\">Friday, December 11: the first day of the war</h3>\n<p>Schools are closed. The storm ends at 1:30 pm with <strong>2.5&quot;</strong> of snowball snow at 27–31°F.</p>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>North (Tessa)</th><th>South (Owen)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One Bad Snowball</td><td>7:20 am. Tessa throws a 2&quot; curb-grit round at Owen from 20 ft and misses. Owen: &quot;That&#39;s the <em>second</em> one.&quot;</td><td>7:24. Owen throws back and misses too</td></tr><tr><td>One Good Snowball</td><td>7:31. A clean 2¼&quot; round lands on Owen from 35 ft</td><td>7:50. Marcus lands one on Lila</td></tr><tr><td>Skirmish</td><td>7:40. Lila joins her brother&#39;s enemies; 12 rounds a side, the North fires first</td><td>Same exchange; Marcus joins his sister&#39;s enemies</td></tr><tr><td>Standoff</td><td>9:30. Forty feet apart, about 20 rounds a side, for 5 minutes. <strong>Dev commits to the North at 9:35</strong>, because Tessa lets Dev push the machine</td><td>Same standoff</td></tr><tr><td>Snowball Fight</td><td>10:30. 9 kids, cover behind the Brandts&#39; minivan</td><td>Same fight</td></tr><tr><td>Running Battle</td><td>12:10 pm. The North drives the fight across 3 yards and the #12 driveway, 400 ft</td><td>Pushed back the whole way</td></tr><tr><td>First Wall</td><td>1:45. 20&quot; high, 8&quot; thick, 10 ft long</td><td>2:05</td></tr><tr><td>Elbow Wall</td><td>2:10. A 5-ft return covers the open flank</td><td>2:40</td></tr><tr><td>Backpack Spreader</td><td>2:30. Tessa&#39;s machine resurfaces 60 ft of stripped yard behind the line</td><td>No machine yet</td></tr><tr><td>U-Fort</td><td>2:45. Three walls, 8 ft by 6 inside</td><td>3:05</td></tr><tr><td>Fort Building</td><td>4:05. Walls <strong>30&quot; high × 12&quot; thick × 16 ft</strong>, 250 rounds behind them</td><td>4:30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>At 3:00 the sides break for <strong>the porch negotiation</strong> at #9: wall height, throw range, resupply intervals, and whether Owen&#39;s garage bat and racquet may be swung. They may. The first exchange across finished walls is at 4:35: Tessa, Lila, Dev and two Rao cousins (5) against Owen, Marcus and four others (6). It is called for darkness at 5:25. Hits are 61 to 54, North, with 11 disputed.</p>\n<p><strong>Peak tier: Fort Building</strong> (2.5&quot; × 1.0 = 2.5&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 4.</strong> The porch rules cover wall height, range, resupply, and implements. Tessa insists on one more: <em>&quot;The matter of November 24 is reserved.&quot;</em> Owen agrees, taking &quot;reserved&quot; to mean he was right; Tessa agrees, taking it to mean he wasn&#39;t. Lila writes it in pencil on the back of a Christmas-card envelope, the war&#39;s first written document.</p>\n<p>The walls slump 6&quot; on Saturday (36°F), drop to 11&quot; on Sunday (45°F), and the last stub on the shaded north side of the South&#39;s wall is gone at 3:40 pm on Wednesday, December 16. <strong>(No Ammo)</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-the-machines-and-the-patent-dispute\">The machines and the Patent Dispute</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>December </strong><strong>2</strong><strong>6:</strong> Owen shows up with a machine built from Marcus&#39;s low-angle photos of the November 24 band. It has the right screw and trough and no clutch, because the clutch was never in the photos.</p></li><li><p><strong>December 28:</strong> Tessa prints a second machine from her own files. <strong>(Second Unit Built, North)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Saturday, January 2, 2027:</strong> the case is heard in the Brandt garage by Marcus (South), Lila (North), and Priya Rao, 15, declared neutral because she &quot;doesn&#39;t care about any of this.&quot; Priya rules in 4 minutes:</p><ul><li><p>The design is Tessa&#39;s.</p></li><li><p>The Brandts may print from her files if every trough carries a scratched <strong>T</strong> with one leg longer than the other.</p></li><li><p>Owen&#39;s clutchless machine may not be used against a person.</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Owen prints a licensed copy on the Rao printer, and the South has its first machine two weeks after the North&#39;s second.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-the-second-storm-friday-january-22-2027\">The second storm: Friday, January 22, 2027</h3>\n<p><strong>Preparation, Tuesday</strong><strong>,</strong><strong> January 19 – Thursday January 21.</strong> Models agree on an overrunning event ahead of an arctic front, and a Winter Storm Warning for 3–5&quot; follows on Wednesday. This time both sides have two days.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>The North.</strong> Eleven pairs of dry gloves go into rotation through the Oduya dryer. Dev, now the full-time operator, oils the wheels on both machines. Lila draws a star fort on graph paper, 12 ft a side with a 5-ft bastion at each corner, and spends Wednesday night arguing the angles with a protractor.</p></li><li><p><strong>The South.</strong> Three kids from Beacon Street join, where a stray December round started a separate argument. Owen writes the war&#39;s first siege plan on the back of a math worksheet: outworks first, then three assaults on the North&#39;s front wall.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, January 21.</strong> Schools cancel Friday at 4:30 pm. First flakes at 7:15 pm, 28°F; moderate snow from midnight to 6 am at ½&quot; an hour.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South&#39;s three Beacon recruits test their throwing arms on the North&#39;s mailbox at 5:00 pm Thursday, before a flake has fallen, with ice from a cooler. Lila logs it as a treaty violation. The Border Commission doesn&#39;t exist yet.</p>\n<p><strong>4.5&quot;</strong> of good packing snow ends at 11:40 am. Eighteen kids fight: 8 North, 10 South, counting three from Beacon Street on the South.</p>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>North</th><th>South</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fortified Position</td><td>8:30 am. Walls 40&quot; high × 18&quot; thick × 20 ft</td><td>9:10</td></tr><tr><td>Outworks</td><td>9:15. Two outworks 20 ft forward, 5 ft × 2 ft × 10&quot; each</td><td>10:00</td></tr><tr><td>Glacis Blanketing</td><td>10:15. An even coat laid across the open ground so every footprint shows</td><td>Never, that day; no machine on the field</td></tr><tr><td>Siege Begins</td><td>11:30. The South&#39;s 3 assaults fail short of the wall, and the North&#39;s sock slings lob over the South&#39;s wall from 80 ft</td><td>12:40. The South answers in kind</td></tr><tr><td>Star Fort</td><td>12:30 pm. Lila argues the bastion angles with a protractor; nobody rushes it twice</td><td>3:00</td></tr><tr><td>Fortress &amp; Siege</td><td>1:50. 6 manning, 3 in reserve, about 900 rounds a side, the middle ground empty for up to 40 minutes</td><td>2:15, once its own reserve is in place</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>It is called for dark at 5:25. Hits are 212 to 198, North, with 34 disputed. <strong>Peak tier: Fortress &amp; Siege</strong> (4.5&quot;).</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>January 23:</strong> the first <strong>Coverage Contest</strong>. Owen lays 1,900 sq ft and is disqualified for a 2 mm layer invisible from the street. Priya writes the rule: <em>a layer must be visible from the road at dusk.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday night, January 24:</strong> Tessa and Dev lay an even coat across the #9 front yard. At 7:25 Monday morning Lila sees the parallel tread marks from her own window and says nothing. Owen finds them himself at 7:45. <strong>(Deniable Snowfall)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>February:</strong> clutchless copies appear at school; 3 of 4 fail within a week. <strong>(Counterfeit Spreaders)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Tuesday, February 9:</strong> 0.1&quot; of freezing drizzle scores only a trace. Owen notices the glazed tent stakes won&#39;t pull out of the ground and writes <em>&quot;pour water on the wall?&quot;</em> on a math worksheet. He leaves it on the Brandt kitchen table, where his sister eats breakfast.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Wednesday, March 3, 2027.</strong> The last flakes of Year Zero: a trace, 0.01&quot; on car roofs at 6:50 am, 30°F, gone by 9:20.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>6:55 am:</strong> Tessa and Dev take N-2 out at the 4-mm detent and try to gather the whole court&#39;s trace into one band. Ten minutes of pushing lays a stripe 9 ft long that can be seen only from a low angle with a flashlight.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:05 am:</strong> Owen crosses the street, as he did on November 24, and tries to pack something from Tessa&#39;s stripe. Nothing holds. This time there are 6 witnesses, all of whom agree, which is a first.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:10 am:</strong> Lila enters it in the notebook as the last event of the season: <em>Imperceptible. Nothing thrown. Everyone agrees.</em> It is the only entry in Year Zero that both sides sign.</p></li></ul>\n<p>The season total at Kessler Court is 7.2&quot; of snow plus this trace, and 0.1&quot; of ice, slightly below Tulsa&#39;s normal.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-summer-2027-and-the-first-anniversary\">Summer 2027 and the first anniversary</h3>\n<p>Tessa writes everything down in <strong>22 hand-numbered pages</strong>. Four hand copies are made, and no two agree on the gate table. <strong>(The Spreader Manual)</strong> Lila copies the envelope into a notebook titled <em>Rules of Kessler Court</em>, 9 rules long. Rule 4 is unchanged.</p>\n<p><strong>Friday, December 10, 2027, 9:40 pm.</strong> It is clear and 34°F. Seven kids stand in the cul-de-sac for one minute at the exact time of the first flake, and Dev writes <strong>&quot;1 A.S.&quot;</strong> in the back of the manual.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-part-three-the-great-storm-1-as\">Part Three: The Great Storm (1 A.S.)</h2>\n<p>The Great Storm of January 31 – February 3, 2028 drops 40.0&quot; on Tulsa, 34.0&quot; of it thundersnow, and the snow lies for nearly six weeks. That is 74.0&quot; packable: the war climbs 104 tiers in 30 days and peaks at <strong>Continental War (tier 208) on Friday, March 3</strong>, after Hereditary Aristocracy (tier 205) and Client Kingdoms (tier 206) on February 25. Total Snow War (tier 182) is passed on day 9. The Great Storm is the last snow of the winter; everything after the peak is the slow melt.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-fall-2027-printed-in-a-basement\">Fall 2027: Printed in a Basement</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> After the first anniversary, the war has a manual, a calendar, three machines, and a waiting list: 31 kids on three streets have asked for a licensed copy. Only one printer will print them. It is in the Rao basement, and it belongs to Priya, who has said she doesn&#39;t care about any of this.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Saturday, December 11, 2027.</strong> Tessa, Lila, and Dev go down the Rao basement stairs with the files on a thumb drive and a bag of marshmallows. Priya&#39;s terms take 6 minutes: 1 machine per 11 hours of printing, 4 a week at most if nothing warps, filament paid for by the kid getting the machine, and cocoa every 2 hours while it runs. The South&#39;s request comes in at 4:00 the same afternoon and gets the same terms, second in line.</p></li><li><p><strong>December 13 – January 30.</strong> The printer runs unattended overnight, 11 hours a machine. Three prints warp in the cold basement and are scrapped. Every finished trough gets the scratched <strong>T</strong> before it leaves the basement. The North collects machines 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11 and 13; the South gets 4, 7, 10, 12 and 14, always one place behind.</p></li><li><p><strong>The bottleneck.</strong> By January the problem is not design but filament: a 1-kg spool makes about 2.5 machines. Priya, who has never thrown a snowball in the war, is now the most important person in it, and both sides know it. Her cocoa arrives on time, twice, from both houses. <strong>(Printed in a Basement)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> There is nothing to fight with, so the war is fought for printer time. The North gets Tuesday and Thursday nights by trading its Saturday slot for 40 marshmallows. Owen tries to bump the queue by printing a machine at a public library at 9:00 am on January 8, and the library&#39;s printer jams on the screw. That is the closest the war comes to a shot fired in December or January.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-saturday-december-18-2027-the-trace\">Saturday, December 18, 2027: the trace</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The forecast for Friday night says <em>flurries possible, no accumulation</em>. Nobody believes it, and both sides stage their machines on the porches anyway: the North&#39;s 5, the South&#39;s 2.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>6:40 am.</strong> A trace, 0.02&quot; on car roofs and the tops of the mailboxes at 29°F, gone by 9:30.</p></li><li><p><strong>6:52 am.</strong> Tessa and Dev take N-1 and N-2 out at the 4-mm detent and try to gather the court&#39;s trace into a band. After 15 minutes, it is a faint stripe on the #14 driveway that shows only in Lila&#39;s photograph from a low angle.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:10 am.</strong> Owen and Marcus come out with their two machines and get even less on the Brandt side.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:15 am.</strong> Dev scoops a gloveful off the stripe to see what it does. It whitens the dark glove for about two seconds and is gone; closed in a fist, it goes straight back to the air.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> None. Nothing can be packed, so nothing is thrown, and nobody argues about it. Lila logs it in the notebook as <strong>Imperceptible</strong>. It is the only snow on Kessler Court between March 3, 2027 and January 31, 2028.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-january-26-31-2028-the-week-before\">January 26 – 31, 2028: the week before</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> By January, Priya&#39;s printing has put <strong>14 licensed machines</strong> on three streets.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Wednesday, January 26.</strong> Long-range models show a deep upper low forming over the Four Corners by the following Monday, with arctic air already over Kansas. Dev reads the forecast discussion aloud at the bus stop, and one phrase gets repeated all week: <em>&quot;potentially historic.&quot;</em> That night the North starts a list on the Oduya fridge: gloves, shovels, recycling bins for block molds, sleds, marshmallows.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, January 27.</strong> Tessa redraws the December lines on graph paper at four squares to the inch, with every tent stake marked, because they will be under 3 ft of snow. The South buys 6 snow shovels at the hardware store at 4:30 pm, which leaves the store with none.</p></li><li><p><strong>Friday, January 28.</strong> A Winter Storm Watch goes up. The North dries 31 pairs of gloves in rotation through three dryers. Mr. Oduya brings 24 sled slats down from the attic &quot;just in case.&quot; Lila moves Owen&#39;s math worksheet from the kitchen drawer to the back of her own notebook, reads it, and puts it back.</p></li><li><p><strong>Saturday, January 29.</strong> Both sides hold a muster. The North&#39;s in the Oduya garage counts 9 kids, 9 machines and 6 shovels. The South&#39;s in the Brandt garage counts 10 kids, 5 machines and 10 shovels.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday, January 30.</strong> The Watch becomes a Warning for 10–16&quot;. Parents on the court agree the rules over coffee at #12: 45 minutes outside, 20 inside, and no tunnels any adult hasn&#39;t seen.</p></li><li><p><strong>Monday, January 31, 3:00 pm.</strong> It is upgraded to a <strong>Blizzard Warning</strong>, local amounts over 18&quot;, thundersnow possible. Schools close Tuesday, then Wednesday, before a flake falls. At 9:00 pm the North&#39;s 9 machines are folded and staged in the Oduya garage, wings in, gates at 12 mm.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> None. Both sides spend the week watching the sky and each other&#39;s garages.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-the-storm\">The storm</h3>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Temp</th><th>What falls</th><th>Rate</th><th>Running total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon Jan 31, 10:15 pm</td><td>26°F</td><td>First flakes, snowball snow</td><td>½&quot;/hr</td><td>0&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 1, 2:00 pm</td><td>24°F</td><td>Snowball snow ends</td><td>—</td><td><strong>6.0&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Tue 2:40 pm</td><td>22°F</td><td><strong>First thunder</strong>; thundersnow in the deformation band</td><td>1–2&quot;/hr</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tue 11 pm – Wed 1 am</td><td>19°F</td><td>Peak thundersnow, 47 strikes counted from the Oduya window</td><td>4&quot;/hr</td><td>17.5&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Feb 2</td><td>18–15°F</td><td>Thundersnow in bursts, gusts to 45 mph</td><td>½–2&quot;/hr</td><td>35.0&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Feb 3, 3:20 am</td><td>14°F</td><td>Last thunder, then the snow ends</td><td>—</td><td><strong>40.0&quot;</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Packable depth:</strong> 6.0&quot; × 1.0 + 34.0&quot; thundersnow × 2.0 = <strong>74.0&quot;</strong>, Continental War.</p></li><li><p><strong>Drifts:</strong> 11 ft against the north side of every house on Kessler Court.</p></li><li><p><strong>The slow melt:</strong> an arctic high parks over the Plains, and a second arctic front reinforces it on February 27. Lows run −8 to 19°F and no day reaches 32°F until Monday, March 6, three days after the peak. The pack settles from 39&quot; to 24&quot; but does not leave. Rain on March 7–8 floods every street the plows had narrowed, and the district gives up on the week: Tulsa schools stay closed through spring break and reopen <strong>Monday, March 20</strong>, after 28 school days closed.</p></li><li><p><strong>Realism:</strong> nothing like this is in Oklahoma&#39;s records. It sits where the ladder says it does: in the Upper Age, past anything even the snowbelt has seen.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-the-climb\">The climb</h3>\n<p>The North reaches every tier first. The South follows hours or a day behind, and copies most of what it sees.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-1-thursday-february-3-low-9f-high-14f-wind-chill-10f\">Day 1 — Thursday, February 3 (low 9°F, high 14°F, wind chill −10°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> At 6:30 am, three hours after the last thunder, the North musters in the Oduya garage. Parents have set one rule before anyone goes out: 45 minutes outside, 20 minutes in, no exceptions, checked against the microwave clock. The inventory is read aloud by Lila: 9 kids, 6 snow shovels, 4 recycling bins to use as block molds, 2 sleds, 9 machines with folded wings, 31 pairs of dry gloves rotating through three dryers. The plan was drawn on graph paper on Wednesday, at four squares to the inch: rebuild on the December tent stakes, which are now under 3 ft of snow and have to be found with a broom handle. The South musters at 8:15 in the Brandt garage with 10 kids, 4 shovels and 1 licensed machine, working from no plan at all.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Battlements (105).</strong> The North cuts blocks with the recycling bins, packs them 2 ft high in two staggered courses, and has walls 4–5 ft high and 20&quot; thick by 9:40 am. Dev cuts four firing notches per side, 10&quot; wide, with a dustpan. A tower goes up at the northeast corner, 2 ft above the wall, and the first lookout sees the South&#39;s door open from 100 ft. The South copies it by 4:30 pm.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 3, 9:40 am · South: Thu Feb 3, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Night Spreading (106).</strong> At 6:00 pm the North&#39;s 9 machines go out under the porch lights. The reflective strips on the wings, added for road safety and grumbled about since September, are the only way the crews find each other. The South&#39;s one machine goes out at 8:00.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 3, 6:00 pm · South: Thu Feb 3, 8:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Watch Rotation (107).</strong> Lila draws a shift chart on the back of the Wednesday plan: four to a fort, 2-hour shifts, first light to full dark. It starts at 7:10 am the next day; the South&#39;s starts Saturday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 4, 7:10 am · South: Sat Feb 5, 7:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Quinzhee (108).</strong> At 10:30 the North shovels a heap 6 ft high and 11 ft across beside the fort. Before lunch, 12 sticks a foot long, cut from a pruned redbud, are pushed in from outside as depth gauges. The heap sits 2 hours to sinter while everyone eats. Hollowing runs 1:15–2:30, and the diggers stop at each stick. It seats two, three if they are friends. The South&#39;s heap is shovelled at dusk and hollowed Friday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 3, 2:30 pm · South: Fri Feb 4, 10:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Depots (109).</strong> The Oduya garage becomes Depot 1: the 9 machines are numbered N-1 to N-9 in marker, with 30 spares from Priya&#39;s printer sorted into labelled shoeboxes (wings, tread bands, clutch springs).</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 3, 3:45 pm · South: Fri Feb 4, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Multi-Fort Campaign (110).</strong> At 1:00 pm, Beacon Street&#39;s 11 kids walk out of the South&#39;s fort over who gets the tower and build their own at the end of the court. That makes three strongholds, 400 ft of frontage and 30 kids. The North signs Beacon Street into an alliance at 3:10 for 40 marshmallows and the use of N-7. The South has no ally until Saturday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 3, 3:10 pm · South: Sat Feb 5, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Igloo (111), begun.</strong> Dev, the one person who has read about it, starts cutting blocks from the 10-ft drift on the north side of #14 with Mrs. Rao&#39;s bread knife: 18&quot; long, 10&quot; high, 6&quot; thick. Forty blocks are cut before dark.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The first exchange of the Great Storm is at 10:05 am, before either side has a roof. Owen leads 10 South kids across the court at the North&#39;s half-built wall, each carrying 8 rounds in a grocery bag. The North fires from the new notches, 9 throwers at about 18 rounds a minute, and the South&#39;s line stops 25 ft short. By the 10:50 break for the 45/20 rule, the hits are 140 to 96. The afternoon is a standoff across 60 ft of 3-ft snow nobody can run through. When Beacon Street walks out at 1:00, its 11 kids take their rounds with them, and the South&#39;s fire drops by half. Parents call everyone in at 6:15, 17 minutes after sunset.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-2-friday-february-4-low-6f-high-12f\">Day 2 — Friday, February 4 (low −6°F, high 12°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The cold is the story. Parents add a second rule: no tunnelling unless an adult has seen the tunnel. Mr. Oduya takes the day off work and brings 24 sled slats from the attic. The North&#39;s plan for the day is the trench line, drawn the night before with ranging marks every 25 ft.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Igloo (111), finished.</strong> The last of 140 blocks closes the spiral at 11:05 am. Inside: 7 ft across, 4½ ft at the crown, an entrance tunnel below floor level. With three inside it reads 30°F against 19°F outside. The South&#39;s igloo falls in twice and stands on Sunday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 4, 11:05 am · South: Sun Feb 6, 1:20 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Field Repair Kits (112).</strong> Two clutch springs, a spare pinion, four tread bands and a knife are rolled into a dish towel for each crew. Nine kits by 9:00 am.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 4, 9:00 am · South: Fri Feb 4, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Fieldworks (113).</strong> Eleven North kids cut firing steps, banked approaches and two sling lanes, 300 ft in all, between 9:00 and 3:30, working in shifts to the 45/20 rule.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 4, 3:30 pm · South: Sat Feb 5, noon</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Trench-Floor Spreading (114) and Trenches (115).</strong> The trench goes in 2 ft deep and 80 ft long along the ranging marks, and two narrow machines lay fresh footing on its churned floor. The South starts its trench at 2:00 and finishes it Saturday morning.</p><ul><li><p>Trench-Floor Spreading (114): North Fri Feb 4, 1:30 pm · South Sat Feb 5, 9:15 am</p></li><li><p>Trenches (115): North Fri Feb 4, 1:45 pm · South Sat Feb 5, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Communication Trench (116) and Spreader Sappers (117).</strong> A rear trench, 120 ft long and 3 ft deep, runs from the fort to the Depot 1 garage door. Crews lay cover over the spoil so the South can&#39;t see what&#39;s new.</p><ul><li><p>Communication Trench (116): North Fri Feb 4, 2:30 pm · South Sat Feb 5, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Sappers (117): North Fri Feb 4, 2:45 pm · South Sat Feb 5, 1:20 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Trench Warfare (118) and Camouflage Layer (119).</strong> By 3:00 nobody on the North stands up in the open. Lobbing planks, cut from a broken bookcase, go into service at 60 degrees.</p><ul><li><p>Trench Warfare (118): North Fri Feb 4, 2:00 pm · South Fri Feb 4, 2:20 pm</p></li><li><p>Camouflage Layer (119): North Fri Feb 4, 3:00 pm · South Sat Feb 5, 1:50 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Tunnel Networks (120) and Tunnel Spreaders (121).</strong> Three tunnels are cut into the 10-ft drift, 150 ft in all. Mr. Oduya crawls every foot of them before anyone else goes in, sets a sled slat every 6 ft, and marks four entrances, two of them concealed. N-3 and N-4 go underground with wings folded and the gate at 4 mm.</p><ul><li><p>Tunnel Networks (120): North Fri Feb 4, 4:20 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 9:00 am</p></li><li><p>Tunnel Spreaders (121): North Fri Feb 4, 4:45 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 9:45 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> At noon the South sends 4 kids to knock in the unfinished igloo. They get within 15 ft before Dev, inside it with 30 rounds, drives them off alone, and the igloo is closed 65 minutes later. From 2:00 pm it is trench warfare: lobbing planks on both sides at 60 degrees, hit rates down from one in four to one in eleven, and the front moves about 6 ft all afternoon. Hits for the day: 380 to 310.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-3-saturday-february-5-low-1f-high-18f\">Day 3 — Saturday, February 5 (low 1°F, high 18°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> At breakfast in the Brandt kitchen, Lila finds Owen&#39;s math worksheet from last February on the table. One line on it is circled: <em>&quot;pour water on the wall?&quot;</em> She photographs it, puts it back exactly where it was, and walks it across the street. The North spends the morning finding containers: 2 watering cans, 4 milk jugs and a mop bucket, 7 gallons a trip.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Staff Sling (122) and Undermining (123).</strong> Two staff slings are built from broom handles and dish towels. At 10:00 the North drives a 22-ft tunnel under the South&#39;s front wall in four hours, with two diggers in shifts and Mr. Oduya checking it twice. At 2:10 pm 8 ft of the South&#39;s wall drops in a second and a half.</p><ul><li><p>Staff Sling (122): North Sat Feb 5, 9:30 am · South Sat Feb 5, 3:40 pm</p></li><li><p>Undermining (123): North Sat Feb 5, 2:10 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>The Spreader Guild (124).</strong> The 9 North operators organise. Membership needs an even 5-mm run, the 9-second clutch check from memory, and the surface rule recited word for word.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 5, 11:00 am · South: Sun Feb 6, 10:15 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>The Keep (125) through Concentric Fort (129).</strong> The keep is a walled dome, 7 × 6 × 4 ft inside, shared by the North and Beacon Street. The inner bailey goes up behind it, 60 ft of circuit, 5 ft high and 30&quot; thick, and the second ring 30 ft out by dark. The South builds its keep Sunday.</p><ul><li><p>The Keep (125): North Sat Feb 5, 3:00 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>Keep Resurfacing (126): North Sat Feb 5, 3:20 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 11:45 am</p></li><li><p>Inner Bailey (127): North Sat Feb 5, 3:40 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Registry (128): North Sat Feb 5, 3:55 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 1:45 pm</p></li><li><p>Concentric Fort (129): North Sat Feb 5, 4:00 pm · South Sun Feb 6, 4:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ice-Armored Walls (130).</strong> At 4:10 pm, at 3°F, the North pours 7 gallons a side down the outer faces. It freezes within the hour, and the first South round to hit it shatters. Owen sees the glaze at 8:30, works out where the idea went, and has the South&#39;s walls poured by 8:50 pm. The worksheet is never left on the kitchen table again.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 5, 4:10 pm · South: Sat Feb 5, 8:50 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> When the South&#39;s wall drops at 2:10 pm, 14 North kids go through the 8-ft gap with 20 rounds each. They hold the inside of the South&#39;s fort for 25 minutes, until Marcus brings the South&#39;s reserve of 9 up through its own trench and pushes them back out. The gap is refilled by 3:30. At 4:10 the first South round to hit the new glaze shatters in front of 20 witnesses on both sides. Hits for the day: 410 to 260, the North&#39;s widest margin yet.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-4-sunday-february-6-low-4f-high-20f\">Day 4 — Sunday, February 6 (low 4°F, high 20°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Juniper Lane, Anjali&#39;s street, walks over at 8:00 am, 23 kids strong, and joins the North. That makes 43 on the North with Beacon Street, enough to staff jobs instead of forts. Lila writes the first job board on the Oduya garage door in dry-erase marker: glazing, slings, engines, depot, cocoa.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Armoured Spreaders (131).</strong> Curved shields cut from a plastic kiddie pool go over the troughs of N-1 to N-4, adding 4 kg to each.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 9:00 am · South: Mon Feb 7, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Glazing Crews (132).</strong> Three kids per fort haul 40 gallons a night from the Oduya basement tap, 60 ft in relays, 11 trips. Unglazed sections are marked on the map in red.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 9:15 am · South: Mon Feb 7, 9:40 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Doctrine (133).</strong> Tessa writes the three rules on an index card: resurface before the packers run dry, never under observation, never the same ground twice in a day.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 9:45 am · South: Mon Feb 7, 10:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Barbican (134), begun.</strong> Eleven kids push the main gate out into a 20-ft passage bent twice at right angles, 6 ft high, with a drop slot over the second bend. It takes until Monday afternoon.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 10:30 am · South: Mon Feb 7, noon</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Sling Corps (135).</strong> Thirty slingers drill in two ranks of 15, throwing on Lila&#39;s count. It takes 9 sessions over 3 days to get a usable volley, and a third of them never manage it.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 11:00 am · South: Mon Feb 7, 1:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Corps (136).</strong> Nine operators run a rolling line off the plow berm at the end of the court, 40 ft of fresh cover per pass.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 12:30 pm · South: Mon Feb 7, 2:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Siege Engines (137).</strong> The parts are gathered the night before: a plastic sled from the Rao garage, a fence post from the Nelsons&#39; blown-down section, and four bungee cords that Lila takes from her father&#39;s truck, on the South&#39;s side of the street, without asking. Five kids assemble it in 2 hours. At 2:40 pm it throws a 4&quot; round a measured 110 ft and comes apart on the second shot. Mr. Brandt misses the cords Monday morning, which is also when Owen works out how a catapult is made. The South&#39;s first engine flies Monday at 3:15.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 2:40 pm · South: Mon Feb 7, 3:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Liaison (138).</strong> Dev becomes the runner between the machines and the engine, and the only one allowed to stop a launch mid-load.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 3:00 pm · South: Mon Feb 7, 3:40 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ballista (139).</strong> Dev&#39;s bike gives up its inner tube; two fence uprights from the same blown-down section frame it. It puts one round through a South firing notch at 130 ft at 4:50 pm, with a 50-second reload.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 4:50 pm · South: Mon Feb 7, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Block Tower (140), begun.</strong> Six hundred blocks are needed. The first tower, built straight up, falls at 9 ft on Monday. The second, tapered from 9 ft square to 5, is finished Thursday and stands 19 days.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 6, 5:10 pm · South: Mon Feb 7, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The catapult&#39;s first and only shot, at 2:40 pm, lands on the South&#39;s tower and takes 8&quot; off its top. At 3:30 the South raids Depot 1 to get at the machines, 12 kids along the fence line. The sling corps&#39; first live volley, 30 rounds in 2 seconds, meets them at 40 ft, and the raid turns back with no machine taken. At 4:50 the ballista puts a round through a South firing notch at 130 ft; the South boards up three of its notches with a cut sled. Hits for the day: 690 to 300.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-5-monday-february-7-low-8f-high-22f\">Day 5 — Monday, February 7 (low 8°F, high 22°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Schools are still closed. Anjali spends Sunday night turning the Rao garage into a production line: 3 folding tables, 6 ice-cream scoops, a 3&quot; ring cut from a coffee can as the size gauge, and a tally sheet. Priya, who still doesn&#39;t care, agrees to keep the printer running if somebody brings her cocoa every 2 hours.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Counter-Battery (141) and Spread-and-Pack Cycle (142).</strong> The North&#39;s second engine is built to hit the South&#39;s first; it disables it at 11:20 am. Packers start working machine-laid beds within the hour while they still bind.</p><ul><li><p>Counter-Battery (141): North Mon Feb 7, 11:20 am · South Tue Feb 8, 9:00 am</p></li><li><p>Spread-and-Pack Cycle (142): North Mon Feb 7, 11:45 am · South Tue Feb 8, 9:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Trebuchet (143).</strong> A pit 2 ft deep is dug to seat the frame, and a laundry basket of packed snow becomes the counterweight. Nine hours of building, from 7:00 am to 4:00 pm. Its first shot goes 260 ft, past anything the South has.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 7, 4:15 pm · South: Tue Feb 8, 11:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ammunition Factory (144).</strong> Anjali is foreman, with 6 workers. Output runs 60–90 rounds an hour; every round is checked against the coffee-can ring and the engine cup before stacking. One in eight is rejected. Standing inventory passes 2,000 by Tuesday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 7, 8:00 am · South: Mon Feb 7, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Resurfacing Line, Engine Shops, Spreader Foundry (145–147).</strong> A second line lays snow back on stripped ground so the packers never run dry. Spare frames and arms are kept in the Rao shed. Priya tests two plastics in the family freezer overnight and picks the one that doesn&#39;t shatter; it is never revisited.</p><ul><li><p>Resurfacing Line (145): North Mon Feb 7, 1:00 pm · South Tue Feb 8, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Engine Shops (146): North Mon Feb 7, 2:00 pm · South Tue Feb 8, 2:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Foundry (147): North Tue Feb 8, 6:50 am · South Tue Feb 8, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Quarry Works (148).</strong> Four cutting sites open away from the fighting, each giving about 600 rounds a day. The best is the 9-ft plow pile at the church lot on Beacon Street, which changes hands three times by Friday.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 7, 2:30 pm · South: Tue Feb 8, 3:45 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Casemates, Ranging Stakes, Screw Standardization (149–151).</strong> The main front is thickened to 4 ft so firing rooms can be cut into it, 6 ft wide with an 18&quot; embrasure. Stakes go in every 25 ft out to 200 ft. Every screw on the North is brought to the 11-cm pattern.</p><ul><li><p>Casemates (149): North Mon Feb 7, 3:00 pm · South Tue Feb 8, 4:30 pm</p></li><li><p>Ranging Stakes (150): North Mon Feb 7, 3:30 pm · South Tue Feb 8, 5:15 pm</p></li><li><p>Screw Standardization (151): North Mon Feb 7, 5:00 pm · South Tue Feb 8, 7:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>The South spends Monday copying Sunday: its first engine, its first glazing crew, and a factory in the Brandt kitchen that Mrs. Brandt closes at 4:00 pm.</p>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The engine duel opens at 11:20 am, when the North&#39;s new counter-battery engine disables the South&#39;s first catapult on its third shot. The South&#39;s answer, at 3:15, is its own catapult, built from the bungee cords Mr. Brandt finally found missing that morning. The trebuchet&#39;s first shot, at 4:15, goes 260 ft over everything and lands in the Brandt back yard behind the South&#39;s last wall, where nobody was defending. Hits for the day: 1,120 to 640, with the factory&#39;s rounds now doing most of the work.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-6-tuesday-february-8-low-10f-high-25f\">Day 6 — Tuesday, February 8 (low 10°F, high 25°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The war has outgrown the court. At 7:30 am Tessa, Lila, Dev and the Beacon and Juniper captains walk four blocks with a 100-ft tape measure and a clipboard, marking the line with orange spray paint on the snow.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Front Lines (152).</strong> Four blocks, 2,000 ft of frontage. Each two-fort alliance gets one main wall, <strong>6 ft high, 3 ft thick and 220 ft long</strong>; three alliances wall 660 ft of it. Sixty-four kids, 5 engines, one per 400 ft.</p><ul><li><p>Front Lines (152): North Tue Feb 8, 8:00 am · South Wed Feb 9, 8:00 am</p></li><li><p>Spreader Reconnaissance (153): North Tue Feb 8, 9:00 am · South Wed Feb 9, 8:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ravelins and Salients (154–155).</strong> Four wedges go up in front of the wall, each 30 ft along its faces and 5 ft high, with a crew of 5 and 200 rounds.</p><ul><li><p>Ravelins (154): North Tue Feb 8, 10:30 am · South Wed Feb 9, 10:00 am</p></li><li><p>Salients (155): North Tue Feb 8, 11:15 am · South Wed Feb 9, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>Counter-Spreader Fire (156): North Tue Feb 8, noon · South Wed Feb 9, 12:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Bombardment (157).</strong> Nine engines fire 40 rounds an hour each at the South&#39;s walls. Glazed frontage that lasted a week now lasts two days.</p><ul><li><p>North: Tue Feb 8, 8:30 am · South: Wed Feb 9, 1:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Supply Lines through Depots (158–162).</strong> Sleds are requisitioned from four households that are not asked. A sled tramway runs to within 400 ft of the line. Twenty-two roofed blockhouses go up along the routes over the next four days. Six depots of 1,500 rounds each sit behind the sectors.</p><ul><li><p>Supply Lines (158): North Tue Feb 8, 1:30 pm · South Wed Feb 9, 2:30 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Convoys (159): North Tue Feb 8, 2:00 pm · South Wed Feb 9, 3:15 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Rail Head (160): North Tue Feb 8, 3:00 pm · South Wed Feb 9, 4:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Blockhouse Chain (161): North Tue Feb 8, 4:00 pm · South Wed Feb 9, 5:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Depots (162): North Tue Feb 8, 5:00 pm · South Wed Feb 9, 6:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Self-Propelled Spreaders, Mobile Batteries, Forward Works (163–165).</strong> Machines are towed on sled frames. Engines move nightly so no position is used twice. The works in front of the bailey are rebuilt to the new scale over Wednesday.</p><ul><li><p>Self-Propelled Spreaders (163): North Tue Feb 8, 6:30 pm · South Thu Feb 10, 7:30 am</p></li><li><p>Mobile Batteries (164): North Tue Feb 8, 9:00 pm · South Thu Feb 10, 8:30 am</p></li><li><p>Forward Works (165): North Wed Feb 9, 6:45 am · South Thu Feb 10, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>The South reaches Front Lines on Wednesday morning, with its line marked in Owen&#39;s mother&#39;s ketchup because the spray paint was on the North&#39;s side of the street.</p>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Front Lines is fought, for the first time, at a distance. Nine North engines bombard the South&#39;s walls from 8:30 am to 4:00 pm at 40 rounds an hour each. The South attacks the two eastern ravelins at 1:00 with 22 kids and takes one, crew of 5 and 200 rounds included. The ravelin is retaken at 2:45 when a North supply sled, arriving down the new tramway, turns out to be carrying 6 slingers instead of rounds. Hits for the day: 2,900 to 1,700.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-7-wednesday-february-9-low-12f-high-27f\">Day 7 — Wednesday, February 9 (low 12°F, high 27°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Tessa spends Tuesday night drawing the citadel at four squares to the inch: five rooms on a footprint of 34 by 22 ft, 40 ft on its longest axis, 6 ft of headroom at the center, walls 3 ft thick. The site is the side yard of #12, the only flat 40 ft on the court. At 7:00 am two parents walk the site with a yardstick and set the one rule that will never be broken: <strong>nobody inside while anyone is on top.</strong></p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Snow Citadel (166).</strong> Construction runs 7:30 am to 3:00 pm in crews of 8: 60 person-hours for the rooms and roof. The inner bailey is enlarged around it the same day, 160 ft of circuit at 7 ft high and 36&quot; thick, for another 90 person-hours. At 3:40 pm Tessa names it <strong>Nine-Forty</strong>, after the first flake, and paints the name over the entrance in food coloring. Garrison: 28 of the North&#39;s 88.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 3:40 pm · South: Thu Feb 10, 3:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Citadel Blanketing (167).</strong> Eleven crews resurface every walked-on surface within 72 hours. From the South&#39;s line the citadel looks permanently, impossibly fresh, and everyone inside understands this is a political statement.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 3:55 pm · South: Thu Feb 10, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Two-Story Fort (168).</strong> At 4:00 pm Dev and Tessa start a second floor over Nine-Forty&#39;s central room, the only one with walls under it on all four sides: 14 by 10 ft inside, 6 ft of headroom, walls 3 ft thick, 20 by 16 ft outside. Eleven kids carry 2-inch sled slats and fence boards out of the #14 garage, lay them across the walls at 6 ft, and pack 8 inches of snow over them for a floor 10 inches deep. The drawing says to leave it a night; at 12°F it gets three hours, through dinner. At 7:30 pm two parents stand on the slab together, 360 lb between them, and the citadel rule is amended to: nobody inside under a roof that nobody has stood on. The upper room is finished by porch light at 9:10 pm: 14 by 10 ft and 5 ft high inside walls 2 ft thick, under an 8-inch roof, so Nine-Forty now stands 12 ft 6 in at its center. Four notches, 10 inches wide and 8 high, sit 3½ ft above the upper floor and clear the ground floor&#39;s wall by a full story. Ten packed steps 3 ft wide, each about 8 inches high and 22 deep, climb 18 ft up the back to a hatch 2 by 3 ft. Nine kids hold it, and the 6 in the room below are told to stop throwing upward. On Saturday a crack opens over the ground-floor doorway, and the floor is re-slatted that night. The South follows on <strong>Friday, February 11</strong> at 2:15 pm, with a second floor over the middle room of its 3-room citadel in the Brandt backyard, 12 by 9 ft instead of 14 by 10 because the room underneath is smaller.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 9:10 pm · South: Fri Feb 11, 2:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Conscription (169).</strong> Lila draws up a roll across four blocks: one body per two children in each house, exemptions for injury, for anyone with a working stove, and for the engine crews. It comes to 140 names. Compliance runs about 70%.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 5:00 pm · South: Thu Feb 10, 7:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Heavy Trebuchets (170).</strong> Two engines with 400-lb counterweights of packed snow in a lawn cart, crews of 9, range 400 ft. One shot takes a foot of glazed wall off its face.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 5:30 pm · South: Fri Feb 11, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Standing Army (171).</strong> Ninety of the 140 report every day for 11 days running. Four ranks, scarves for uniforms, and every order routed through Lila.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 9, 6:00 pm · South: Fri Feb 11, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> At 10:15 am, the South tries to stop the citadel before it has a roof: 31 kids come across the court behind two sleds stacked with rounds. The North&#39;s sling corps puts 30 rounds into a 20-ft square in 2 seconds, three times. The attack stalls at the barbican&#39;s second bend, where 6 South kids stand under the drop slot for 40 seconds. Hits that hour run 214 to 77. The South reaches Snow Citadel on Thursday, with a 3-room citadel in the Brandt backyard.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-8-thursday-february-10-low-15f-high-29f\">Day 8 — Thursday, February 10 (low 15°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The treaties are drafted overnight on flattened appliance boxes, one delegation of six from each side, around the Rao kitchen table. Lila writes for the North and Marcus for the South. They agree on boundaries to the foot, a limit of 300 rounds a side per engagement, and a ceiling of 4 engines each, a number both sides already exceed. At 11:50 pm Tessa insists on one last article, word for word from the envelope: <em>the matter of November 24 is reserved.</em> Marcus agrees at once. Everyone at the table knows why.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Officer Corps and Spreader Clauses (174–175).</strong> Eleven officers across both sides, none of whom has thrown in 2 days. The clauses cap machines and ban resurfacing within sight of the other side&#39;s works. Both comply with the count; neither complies with the sight line.</p><ul><li><p>Spreader Conscription (172): North Thu Feb 10, 7:00 am · South Fri Feb 11, 3:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Pillboxes (173): North Thu Feb 10, 7:30 am · South Fri Feb 11, 4:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Officer Corps (174): North Thu Feb 10, 8:30 am · South Fri Feb 11, 5:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Clauses (175): North Thu Feb 10, 11:00 am · South Thu Feb 10, 11:05 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Treaties (176).</strong> Four documents are in force by noon, signed on cardboard. Two contradict each other on where the line crosses the #12 driveway.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, noon · South: Thu Feb 10, 12:05 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Three-Story Fort (177).</strong> The North wants a spotter higher than the Block Tower will be. A purpose-built fort goes up on the Juniper Lane corner from 6:00 am to 12:30 pm: six crews of 11, about 430 person-hours, the tier&#39;s 11 people for 5 days done in one morning. It stands 20 by 18 ft outside. The ground room is 12 by 10 ft and 5 ft 2 in high inside walls 4 ft thick; the second room, inside walls 3 ft thick, is 14 by 12 ft and 4 ft 2 in high; the floors over them are 10 inches of slat and packed snow, topping out at 6 ft and 11 ft. The third story is set back on the second&#39;s roof: 8 ft square and 4 ft high inside walls 2 ft thick, 12 ft square outside, under an 8-inch roof at 15 ft 8 in, 3 ft 8 in taller than the Block Tower. Its two notches, a foot wide and 10 inches high, take one spotter and one sling. Thirty-one steps 2 ft wide, each 4¼ inches high and a foot deep, are cut into the outer wall and climb 31 ft along the long side and around one corner; Anjali strings a rope beside them at 11:40 after Ada slips on step 19. The South builds its own on <strong>Saturday, February 12</strong>, finished at 8:45 am, before it passes Total Snow War that day.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, 12:30 pm · South: Sat Feb 12, 8:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Indirect Fire (178).</strong> The Block Tower is finished at 1:00 pm, and its spotter calls corrections down to the batteries. The first round on a target nobody can see lands at 2:20; hit rate settles near one in seven.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, 2:20 pm · South: Sat Feb 12, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Border Commission (179).</strong> Priya chairs 9 members, 4 and 4 and herself. It hears its first dispute at 3:30, the #12 driveway, and splits it down the middle with a garden hose laid on the snow.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, 3:30 pm · South: Thu Feb 10, 3:35 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Weather Falsification (180).</strong> One suburban district has plowed its bus lots clean and announces it will reopen Friday. That night 6 North crews, towing machines on sleds, lay a fresh 4&quot; coat across 9 square miles of its lots and routes. The district closes Friday. The instrument record says nothing fell. Every local account says 4&quot;.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, 9:30 pm · South: Fri Feb 11, 9:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>The Fortified Line (181), begun.</strong> The casemates, pillboxes and tunnels of the North start being joined into one continuous work. It reaches 2.5 miles by the end of February and is walked around at its eastern end, through a neighborhood whose parents refused permission to dig.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 10, 4:45 pm · South: Sat Feb 12, 7:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The treaty&#39;s 300-round limit lasts 4 hours. At 4:10 pm a South engine crew fires its 301st round at the Juniper Lane ravelin, the North answers with all 9 engines, and the commission&#39;s first full session is about that.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-9-friday-february-11-low-11f-high-31f-the-north-passes-total-snow-war\">Day 9 — Friday, February 11 (low 11°F, high 31°F): the North passes Total Snow War</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> At 8:30 am the shared timetable goes up on Anjali&#39;s factory whiteboard: every North neighborhood&#39;s offensive scheduled to the quarter hour, all seven of them. Kessler Court, Beacon Street, Juniper Lane, Halvorsen Place, Cherry Hill Circle, Pinecrest North and the Southwind apartments. Each gets a runner, a copy of the timetable and a cocoa ration.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Total Snow War (182).</strong> At 9:00 the North attacks on three fronts at once. Forty engines are in action, 21 trebuchets and 19 torsion onagers wound from bungee cord and rope; a third of all rounds are thrown by engine. By 6:10 pm, when parents call everyone in, the North has thrown 12,400 rounds, 4,100 of them by engine. Hits are 3,870 to 3,654, with 1,102 disputed.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 11, 9:00 am · South: Sat Feb 12, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> At 1:15 pm the South breaches Nine-Forty&#39;s outer bailey at the southeast corner and holds the gap 2 hours. At 3:30 the North comes up through the Juniper-side tunnel behind them and retakes it. Lunch is a truce nobody writes down and everyone keeps: 41 thermoses at 12:15. On the North&#39;s side this is just a Friday on the way up. The South reaches Total Snow War the next day, February 12, nine days after the snow stopped, and that is the day its chronicler writes the war&#39;s most famous wrong sentence.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-10-saturday-february-12-low-8f-high-26f\">Day 10 — Saturday, February 12 (low 8°F, high 26°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The engines are a mess of mismatched parts, so Dev and two shop-class eighth-graders spend Friday night drawing one engine: one frame, one arm length (8 ft), one cup (a cut-down mop bucket). Copies go out to every neighborhood by bike at 7:00 am. At the same hour, Lila starts calling every parent on the roll.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Pattern Engines (183).</strong> Ninety engines are built to the one drawing over the next three weeks, against 40 of mixed design still in service. A broken engine is back on the line in 4 hours instead of 2 days.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 12, 7:00 am · South: Sun Feb 13, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>General Mobilization (184).</strong> By Sunday night 1,100 kids have reported across the North&#39;s seven neighborhoods and three more that ask to join. Anjali&#39;s factory and its 9 copies shift entirely to ammunition at 4,000 rounds a day.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 12, 11:00 am · South: Sun Feb 13, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Divisions (185).</strong> The machines get their own command: four divisions, each 900 machines on the books and about 140 in fact. Spreader officers are saluted from this day on.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 12, 2:00 pm · South: Mon Feb 14, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South reaches Total Snow War at last: 7 neighborhoods of its own, 40 engines, a shared timetable copied from the North&#39;s whiteboard through the Rao kitchen window. Its chronicler, a Pinecrest sixth-grader, writes the day up that night. The North spends the day holding, not attacking, and loses 60 ft of the Juniper ravelin.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-11-sunday-february-13-low-6f-high-24f\">Day 11 — Sunday, February 13 (low 6°F, high 24°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The ravelin loss shows the forts are too slow. Tessa, Lila and the Beacon captain spend the morning in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room with a map on a cereal box, planning columns instead of walls: 11 kids each, 400 rounds, one light launcher on a sled.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Blitz Doctrine (186).</strong> At 1:30 pm six columns leave the North&#39;s forts at once. Each covers a quarter mile in under 6 minutes. Engagements that ran 50 minutes now run 9. The deepest column gets 600 ft past the South&#39;s line and the front moves a block.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 13, 1:30 pm · South: Tue Feb 15, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Blitz Spreading (187).</strong> Machines run ahead of the columns laying supply on ground not yet taken. Over the next week the fronts move 11 miles in all, mostly into parks and school fields. More than half the forward machines are lost and the doctrine is kept anyway.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sun Feb 13, 1:35 pm · South: Tue Feb 15, 10:05 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South&#39;s line breaks at 2:20 at the church lot on Beacon Street and is pushed back to the creek culvert by dark. Hits that afternoon run 2,140 to 610. The South copies the columns on Tuesday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-12-monday-february-14-low-10f-high-25f\">Day 12 — Monday, February 14 (low 10°F, high 25°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Four suburban districts try a half-day reopening for Valentine&#39;s Day; Tulsa stays closed. The North writes a sleepover permission slip, 1 page, with three conditions: sleeping bags rated to −20°F, a parent within 30 ft all night, and everyone inside by 10:00 pm. Fifty-two families sign it.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Deep Raiding (188).</strong> Parties of four with 60 rounds each go out up to 2 miles behind the South&#39;s line. Of 9 targets in the next month, 4 are destroyed: two depots, a quarry and an engine park.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 14, 9:00 am · South: Tue Feb 15, 1:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Precision Batteries and Deep Bunker (189–190).</strong> A battery clerk works out range, arm and counterweight on paper before the first shot. Four engines put rounds inside a 9-ft circle at 300 ft. Command moves into a bunker under the Oduya back yard, 20 ft of snow and tunnel above it; nobody on its staff can draw a plan of it from memory.</p><ul><li><p>Precision Batteries (189): North Mon Feb 14, 10:30 am · South Tue Feb 15, 3:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Deep Bunker (190): North Mon Feb 14, 2:00 pm · South Tue Feb 15, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Fortress Cities (191).</strong> The citadel is enlarged to house its garrison overnight: 41 rooms on a footprint of 120 by 90 ft, 8 ft of interior height behind 4-ft walls, 900 ft of mapped tunnel. Fifty-two kids sleep in it that night with 6 parents in the Oduya garage next door. The inside reads 28°F all night against −2°F outside.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 14, 7:30 pm · South: Wed Feb 16, 7:30 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Attendance in the four suburban districts drops under half, they close again at noon, and nobody records it. Valentines are exchanged across the line at the treaty gate at 4:00 pm, 340 of them, 22 containing a round.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-13-tuesday-february-15-low-12f-high-28f\">Day 13 — Tuesday, February 15 (low 12°F, high 28°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The North now has more kids than it can train on the job. Lila opens three schools in the Oduya basement and the Rao living room, each with a sign-up sheet and a whistle.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>City-Scale Spreaders (192).</strong> Two machines are scaled up to 4-m troughs on wagon wheels and towed by a lawn tractor. Each covers 11 blocks in a shift.</p><ul><li><p>North: Tue Feb 15, 9:00 am · South: Wed Feb 16, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Staff College, War Academy, Spreader Academy (193–195).</strong> The staff college takes 22 candidates and teaches supply, frontage and the war&#39;s 9 campaigns so far. The war academy teaches packing (four compressions, three seconds) to 40 students per intake, 80% graduating. The spreader academy&#39;s entrance exam is a single unbroken 100-ft run judged by eye, and its first lesson is the graph-paper sketch, one wheel larger than the other.</p><ul><li><p>Staff College (193): North Tue Feb 15, 10:00 am · South Wed Feb 16, 10:00 am</p></li><li><p>War Academy (194): North Tue Feb 15, 11:00 am · South Wed Feb 16, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>The Spreader Academy (195): North Tue Feb 15, 1:00 pm · South Wed Feb 16, 1:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South&#39;s first blitz columns go out at 10:00 am. Two of six get lost in Pinecrest and one is captured whole by a North deep-raiding party, with its sled launcher. The South reaches Fortress Cities on Wednesday, and its sleepover permission slip is copied from the North&#39;s word for word.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-14-wednesday-february-16-low-14f-high-29f\">Day 14 — Wednesday, February 16 (low 14°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The North is now too big to run from one garage. Tessa cuts a city map into 11 pieces with a ruler on the Oduya dining table, one per region, each with at least one quarry site, one depot and one engine park. The 22 staff-college candidates are paired up and sent out by bike to run them.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Provinces (196).</strong> Eleven provinces with a roll of about 200 kids each. An order from Nine-Forty reaches the furthest province in a day and a half, by bike and walkie-talkie relay. Three provinces produce more than they throw and are taxed a fifth of output, about 900 rounds a week each.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 16, 9:30 am · South: Thu Feb 17, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South attacks the Halvorsen province at dawn with 4 blitz columns to test the new borders. The Battle of the Culvert runs 7:10–10:40 am at the creek crossing. The North holds the culvert mouth and loses the playground behind it. Hits are 1,880 to 1,640, the closest day of the war so far. The South divides into provinces on Thursday, nine of them.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-15-thursday-february-17-low-15f-high-30f\">Day 15 — Thursday, February 17 (low 15°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Tessa and the staff college spend Wednesday night writing one decree for every work in the North. The numbers are set above the largest fort anyone has: main wall 550 ft long, 14 ft high and 8 ft thick, notches 14&quot; at the throat, six to a section; trench 6 ft deep; tunnel 14 ft down; keep 200 by 150 ft. Surveyors go out at 8:00 am with a broom handle painted in 1-ft bands and a composition notebook.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Continental Spreader Works (197).</strong> Forty garages and two school libraries print machines in the cold-tough plastic Priya picked. The standing park passes 1,100 machines.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 17, 8:00 am · South: Fri Feb 18, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Imperial Gauge (198).</strong> By 5:00 pm the surveyors report that not one work in the North meets the gauge, which is the point.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 17, 5:00 pm · South: Fri Feb 18, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> A South party of 12 ambushes the survey team at the Cherry Hill ravelin at 11:30 and captures the broom handle. The North makes a second one by 1:00 pm. That night the two sides sign the <strong>Recut Truce</strong>: no throwing while the walls are being rebuilt, through Monday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-16-friday-february-18-low-13f-high-30f\">Day 16 — Friday, February 18 (low 13°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Each of the North&#39;s 22 settlements gets a work order: one 550-ft wall, 14 ft high and 8 ft thick, which takes about 1.3 acres of stripped yard. Every family whose yard will be stripped signs a permission line in the province notebook. The 12 that don&#39;t are mapped and routed around.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>The Great Recut (199), begun.</strong> Sixty thousand hands start at 8:00 am: walls raised and thickened, every keep extended, trenches dug wider and deeper. Rounds packed in one province now fit the notches in every other.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 18, 8:00 am · South: Sat Feb 19, 8:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> None. The truce holds all day, the longest the war has gone without a throw since the snow fell.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-17-saturday-february-19-low-12f-high-29f\">Day 17 — Saturday, February 19 (low 12°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The capital&#39;s wall is laid out with string and stakes around the whole of Kessler Court and halfway down the street: 1,650 ft, 21 ft high and 12 ft thick, the gauge and half again. Its crews come from all 11 provinces in rotation, 600 at a time.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Snow Empire (200).</strong> By dusk the North&#39;s works run 2.5 miles across its settlements: 21 walls of 550 ft and the capital&#39;s 1,650. Twenty-two settlements are enrolled. Two are in arrears on their tribute of 2,000 rounds a week and keep their garrison support regardless. Four more are under negotiation.</p><ul><li><p>North: Sat Feb 19, 6:00 pm · South: Sun Feb 20, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The truce holds, barely. A South engine crew &quot;tests&quot; its trebuchet into an empty field at 3:00 pm, 6 shots, and the Border Commission rules it legal by 5 votes to 4.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-18-sunday-february-20-low-14f-high-31f\">Day 18 — Sunday, February 20 (low 14°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The South, a day behind on its own recut, declares its own empire anyway, with Kessler Court as its capital too: the Brandt side, 60 ft across the street from the North&#39;s.</p>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The Recut Truce breaks at 2:00 pm, a day early, when a North spreader crew lays fresh cover within sight of the South&#39;s capital wall. The South says it is a violation of the spreader clauses; the North says it is maintenance. In the 3 hours that follow, the two capitals exchange 6,200 rounds across a street 40 ft wide.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-19-monday-february-21-presidents-day\">Day 19 — Monday, February 21: Presidents&#39; Day</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Saturday&#39;s survey teams have walked a line across the city from the river on the west to the creek on the east: 19 miles. One milecastle per mile with a garrison of 32, two turrets between each pair, 9 gates. The line is drawn to run straight down the middle of Kessler Court, between #14 and #9, because that is where the war is.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>The Frontier Wall (201).</strong> Forty thousand hands build it to the gauge, 14 ft high and 8 ft thick. The North builds its half on Monday. The South builds the other face on Tuesday, and the two halves meet at the mailboxes, where a gate is cut 6 ft wide for the mail carrier.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 21, 5:30 pm · South: Tue Feb 22, 4:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Trade crosses at the 9 gates and nowhere else, which is true about half the time. The first fight at the Kessler gate is at 4:15 pm, over whose side the gate&#39;s latch is on.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-20-tuesday-february-22-low-16f-high-31f\">Day 20 — Tuesday, February 22 (low 16°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The city wants sidewalks, bus stops and driveways clear before schools reopen, and the war&#39;s machines are the only snow movers on most of the streets. Somebody has to decide which ground gets resurfaced and which gets cleared. Dev drafts a schedule on Monday night, and Priya, who still doesn&#39;t care, agrees to run the office if it has a printer.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>The Spreader Ministry (202).</strong> Redistribution becomes a department with 4,000 staff: every licensed operator, their crews and their record-keepers. It answers to no one who has pushed a machine since February 3. Its first published act puts fortifications below sidewalks, bus stops and driveways on the resurfacing list. That causes a constitutional crisis at Nine-Forty, and stands, because every parent in the empire agrees with it.</p><ul><li><p>North: Tue Feb 22, 8:00 am · South: Wed Feb 23, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The day&#39;s engagement runs 9:00 am–4:30 pm along the Frontier Wall. Both sides lose more ground to the ministry&#39;s list than to each other: 3 forts on driveways are taken down by their own crews.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-21-wednesday-february-23-low-18f-high-30f\">Day 21 — Wednesday, February 23 (low 18°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Decisions stop being made on the wall and start being made in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room, by flashlight, in the evenings. Nine advisors sit around Tessa there; none holds a position on the line.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Court Politics (203).</strong> Four of the last six senior appointments go to kids who have never built a wall, including two who have only ever run the cocoa line. Two province commands change hands in a week for reasons never written in the notebook.</p><ul><li><p>North: Wed Feb 23, 7:30 pm · South: Thu Feb 24, 7:30 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South raids the Pinecrest North province while its commander is at Nine-Forty for a meeting about the meeting schedule. They take 3 engines and the province&#39;s cocoa urn. The North gets the engines back on Thursday and never the urn.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-22-thursday-february-24-low-17f-high-30f\">Day 22 — Thursday, February 24 (low 17°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The court has outgrown the citadel. Since Tuesday, 900 kids in shifts have been building on the elementary school field. Lila spends Thursday night at the Oduya dining table drawing up the <strong>lines of succession</strong> for the North&#39;s 11 founding families, by birth order, youngest last.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Palace-Fortress (204).</strong> The palace is built mainly to keep the North&#39;s own court in order: 7 wards, one for each rank, each walled off from the next and entered only through the ward below it. The throne hall sits at the center under a dome 60 ft across. Nine hundred people live and work inside it by day, and every one knows how far in they may go. The outer wall is the weakest in the empire, and nobody ever tests it.</p><ul><li><p>North: Thu Feb 24, 2:00 pm · South: Sat Feb 26, 8:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> At 4:30 pm the South&#39;s whole engine park, 61 engines, fires on the palace&#39;s outer wall for an hour as a test. Nothing hits it. They were aimed at the citadel on Kessler Court, which is still the North&#39;s capital on every map the South has. The South starts its own palace Friday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-23-friday-february-25-low-18f-high-30f-the-court-crowns-itself\">Day 23 — Friday, February 25 (low 18°F, high 30°F): the court crowns itself</h4>\n<p>Packable depth is 74.0&quot;. The North has about 17,400 kids on its rolls and 214 engines; the South about 12,600 and 161. Schools are closed for the 18th school day in a row.</p>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Lila&#39;s lines of succession, finished at 11:40 pm Thursday, run 3 pages: 11 founding families, every child listed by birth order, youngest last. Her own name is on the Brandt line, on the South&#39;s side of the street, and is crossed out in her own hand. At 6:45 am the palace&#39;s throne hall is swept, and a dining chair from #14 is carried in as the throne.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>7:15 am, Hereditary Aristocracy (205).</strong> Command of the North passes by birth order to the youngest child of each of the <strong>11 founding lines</strong>. Tessa, 11, hands the North to her sister <strong>Ada, 6</strong>, in front of 140 kids in the throne hall. Older siblings are skipped entirely. Ada&#39;s first two orders, dictated to Tessa: cocoa for everyone, and nobody throws at the dog.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 25, 7:15 am · South: Sat Feb 26, 9:15 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>7:31 am.</strong> The school bus doesn&#39;t come, for the 18th school day running. The aristocracy goes to war.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:00 am, Client Kingdoms (206).</strong> Nine of the 12 yards that refused the Great Recut sign on as client kingdoms of the North. They sit inside the North&#39;s own territory, too small to conquer and too proud to enroll, and the empire has been flowing around them for a week: the Nelson side yard behind the blown-down fence (3 kids), the gap between the two pines at #3 (2), the Halvorsen culvert mouth (4), the corner of the Cherry Hill mailbox bank (2), a willow on Pinecrest North (5), two duplex porches at Southwind (3 and 4), the back corner of the church lot (6), and a sandbox on Juniper Lane (2). Each has one fort with a wall you can see over, and nothing bigger than a garage bat. Each keeps its own flag, its own calendar and its own rules, and hands every dealing with the South to Nine-Forty. In return each gets protection, 4,000 rounds a season and 6 pattern engines it may not export, and each owes the North 300 bodies on demand. The nine together have 31 kids and nobody who can crew an engine. Ada signs all nine treaties in purple marker by 11:40. The other 3 yards stay outside everything.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Feb 25, 11:00 am · South: Sat Feb 26, 1:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>3:40 pm.</strong> Ada holds court for 20 minutes in the throne hall and confirms all 9 advisors, 4 of whom she has never met.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The South, still governed by merit, mounts its biggest attack of the war so far while the North is busy with a coronation. At 3:50 pm, 61 engines open on the Frontier Wall&#39;s Kessler gate and 9 blitz columns go for milecastles 6, 7 and 8. Column 4, led by Owen himself, breaks through at milecastle 7 at 4:35 and holds the gap for 40 minutes. Ada&#39;s only battlefield order of the day, relayed by Tessa from the palace, is &quot;make them go back.&quot; The North&#39;s sling corps and 30 engines from the Halvorsen province close the gap at 5:15. At 6:05 the parents call everyone in. Rounds thrown that afternoon: about 48,000. Hits: 11,240 to 10,980, with 3,400 disputed. <strong>Tiers reached: Hereditary Aristocracy (#205) and Client Kingdoms (#206).</strong></p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-24-saturday-february-26-low-15f-high-31f\">Day 24 — Saturday, February 26 (low 15°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Lila&#39;s copy of the succession list goes across the street overnight; Marcus finds it under the Brandt door at 7:00 am and uses it as the template for the South&#39;s 9 lines. In the Rao basement, Priya spends the morning on a problem nobody else has noticed: the Continental Spreader Works&#39; 40 garages and 2 school libraries are all printing from her files, with 40 different tolerances, and spare parts from one garage no longer fit machines from another.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:15 am.</strong> The South reaches Hereditary Aristocracy. Owen hands command to his brother <strong>Sam, 8</strong>; Marcus, as an older sibling, is skipped.</p></li><li><p><strong>1:30 pm.</strong> The South reaches Client Kingdoms. Sam signs nine yards along Beacon Street and the creek in pencil, on the back of Lila&#39;s succession list. Two of them signed with the North the day before, and from now on they fly both flags.</p></li><li><p><strong>2:15 pm.</strong> Thirty-one hours after taking the throne, Ada hands the North back to <strong>Tessa</strong> and goes to a birthday party. Ada is the youngest Oduya, so there is nobody further down the line to pass it to, and command goes back up to the founder who gave it away. That is how the succession works from now on: the youngest rules about 31 hours, then the founder takes it back.</p></li><li><p><strong>6:30 pm.</strong> Priya sends one text to the owners of the 4 biggest print shops in the empire: &quot;Basement. 10 am tomorrow. Bring your moulds.&quot;</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Sam&#39;s first order is the siege of the Juniper ravelin, run from 8:30 am to 4:30 pm by the South&#39;s province captains, who are older siblings and therefore actually in charge. The ravelin falls at 4:00 and the North retakes it by 5:10 with 12 engines and the sling corps. Hits: 6,310 to 6,220.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-25-sunday-february-27-low-9f-high-22f-gusts-to-38-mph-the-second-front\">Day 25 — Sunday, February 27 (low 9°F, high 22°F, gusts to 38 mph): the second front</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> A second arctic front comes through at 3:10 am behind a line of snow showers that leave nothing measurable. The temperature drops 14 degrees in two hours, and every forecast that had shown a thaw this week now shows highs in the low 20s through Thursday. The glazing crews, who had planned to stop, are told to keep pouring.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>10:00 am, the Rao basement.</strong> Four print shops meet around Priya&#39;s printer: <strong>House Rao</strong> (Kessler Court, the original files, 1 printer), <strong>House Whitaker</strong> (a two-car garage on Cherry Hill, 9 printers), <strong>House Delgado</strong> (two Southwind duplex basements, 6 printers) and <strong>House Kim</strong> (the Beacon Street church basement, 11 printers). Between them they hold every screw mould and trough mould in the North. The meeting runs 2 hours and 40 minutes, with cocoa at 11:00 and 12:00, and ends with a single price list and a single pattern book, both to be published Monday.</p></li><li><p><strong>4:15 pm.</strong> Sam&#39;s 31 hours are up. Sam is the youngest Brandt, with nobody below him in the line, so command of the South goes back to <strong>Owen</strong> in the middle of the afternoon&#39;s fighting.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The Gust Battle at the Kessler gate runs 12:30–4:30 pm. At 38 mph a hand-thrown round drifts 15 ft over 60 ft of flight, and slings are useless, so the day belongs to the engines: 26 on the North, 19 on the South, all firing downwind or not at all. The North holds the upwind side of the court because the Frontier Wall runs north–south, and the South&#39;s rounds land short all afternoon. 4,100 rounds, 1,020 hits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-26-monday-february-28-low-2f-high-17f\">Day 26 — Monday, February 28 (low −2°F, high 17°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The coldest morning since February 4. Schools are closed for the 20th school day. The four houses&#39; price list is printed on House Rao&#39;s printer overnight, 400 copies, and taped up at all 9 Frontier Wall gates and every depot by 8:00 am.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>11:00 am, The Spreader Cartels (207).</strong> Manufacture of every machine in the North now runs through <strong>four houses holding the moulds between them</strong>: Rao, Whitaker, Delgado and Kim. They set the price, the pattern and the delivery day for every machine in the alliance. A screw costs <strong>44 marshmallows</strong>, eleven times the 4 it cost at the Continental Spreader Works in February. Deliveries go out on Thursdays only. The pattern book fixes one screw, one trough, one wing and one clutch spring, and nothing outside it can be ordered. The new screws fail at exactly the old rate: about 1 in 9 cracks in its first week.</p><ul><li><p>North: Mon Feb 28, 11:00 am · South: Tue Feb 29, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>2:00 pm.</strong> Two of the North&#39;s client kingdoms, the sandbox on Juniper Lane and the Nelson side yard, try to mould their own screws instead: one in a gelatin mould from a kitchen drawer, the other by packing modeling clay around a cartel screw. The cartels cut both off from spares at 2:40. The sandbox&#39;s only machine is down by dark with a cracked clutch spring it can no longer replace.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:30 pm.</strong> Owen, back in command for 27 hours, learns that every South machine was printed from the same files and will need the same parts. The South&#39;s 3 machine shops and the Brandt garage spend the evening counting what they have left.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> At 3:10 pm a South deep-raiding party of 4 gets into House Kim&#39;s church basement through the coat room and comes out with 2 trough moulds and a bag of clutch springs. The moulds turn out to be from a rejected batch with the wrong pitch, which is why they were in the coat room. Along the Frontier Wall the cold keeps the day short: 1:00 to 4:00 pm, with 20-minute indoor breaks every 30 minutes under a revised parents&#39; rule. 2,800 rounds, 640 hits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-27-tuesday-february-29-low-3f-high-21f\">Day 27 — Tuesday, February 29 (low 3°F, high 21°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The South&#39;s Pinecrest commander, an older brother with no line of his own, spends Monday night writing a two-page claim on notebook paper: command can only pass down a line, never back up it, so Owen&#39;s return on Sunday is void.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>10:00 am.</strong> The South reaches The Spreader Cartels. Its 3 machine shops and the Brandt garage become 4 houses of their own and publish a price list, 52 marshmallows a screw, on the back of the North&#39;s.</p></li><li><p><strong>The first contested claim, four days in.</strong> The Border Commission hears the Pinecrest claim at lunch and rules 5–4 that command may go back to the founder. The Pinecrest commander appeals because the ruling was made on February 29, a day that doesn&#39;t exist three years in four. The appeal is still pending in May.</p></li><li><p><strong>3:30 pm.</strong> The sandbox and the Nelson side yard capitulate to the cartels, three days after trying to leave them. They hand over the gelatin mould and the clay, and get their spares back at the old price plus 6 marshmallows for the trouble.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> That afternoon the South splits over the ruling. Two Pinecrest blitz columns attack the South&#39;s own Kessler capital wall for 40 minutes before anyone stops them. The North watches from the Frontier Wall and does not intervene, which the treaty does not technically require. 3,900 rounds are thrown on the court that day, 1,700 of them by South kids at other South kids.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-28-wednesday-march-1-low-8f-high-26f-contact\">Day 28 — Wednesday, March 1 (low 8°F, high 26°F): contact</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> By March 1 every heir in all 11 North lines and 9 South lines has had their 31 hours and, being the youngest in their line with nobody below them, handed command back up. The founders run the war again. The youngest of each line keeps the title, a chair in the throne hall, and the right to give one order a week, which is usually about cocoa.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:20 am.</strong> The Frontier Wall&#39;s east end, milecastle 19, reaches the creek. On the far bank, behind a wall of their own 9 ft high, are 40 kids from Broken Arrow who have been fighting their own Great Storm war since February 3. They call themselves the <strong>Lynn Lane Confederacy</strong>, they have reached Fortress Cities on their own, and they have never heard of Kessler Court.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:00 am.</strong> Lila and Priya cross the footbridge under a white pillowcase with a copy of the four treaties. The Confederacy reads them at a picnic table and asks one question: what is the matter of November 24? Nobody answers.</p></li><li><p><strong>By 8:00 pm</strong> the North has made contact with 4 more wars on its edges: Sand Springs across the river bridge, Owasso at the turnpike overpass, Jenks–Bixby at the levee, and Bartlesville, by a cousin. Each has its own storm war, its own forts and its own count of tiers, all lower than Kessler&#39;s.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The first fight between two cities&#39; wars is at the creek footbridge at 2:15 pm, over whose side the bridge belongs to. The Confederacy holds it until 3:40, when 9 North engines are walked down Milecastle Road and set up on the bank. Rounds: 3,300. Hits: 880 to 610. The footbridge is declared neutral at 4:30 by a Border Commission sitting on the bridge itself, with 2 Confederacy members added to make 11.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-day-29-thursday-march-2-low-12f-high-28f\">Day 29 — Thursday, March 2 (low 12°F, high 28°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Contact now spreads faster than anyone can walk. It runs through three channels at once: the cartels&#39; Thursday deliveries, which go out in parents&#39; trucks to relatives in other towns; a group chat Dev starts at 7:00 am called <strong>Turnpike Accord</strong>, which has 212 members by noon; and cousins.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:00 am.</strong> A cartel delivery of 12 machines reaches a cousin in Edmond, 105 miles away, whose own storm war has stalled at Trench Warfare for lack of anything to resurface with. By noon Edmond is allied with the North.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:30 am.</strong> Muskogee&#39;s war joins the Turnpike Accord; Stillwater&#39;s joins at 1:10. Oklahoma City&#39;s and Norman&#39;s wars sign with the South through Marcus&#39;s soccer teammates.</p></li><li><p><strong>3:00 pm.</strong> The chat reaches Wichita, 175 miles northwest, and Joplin, 110 miles northeast. Wichita&#39;s war, which has its own frontier wall along a drainage canal, declares on Edmond by mistake and then on Oklahoma City on purpose.</p></li><li><p><strong>9:40 pm.</strong> Priya counts <strong>14 belligerents</strong> in the chat and on the map taped to the Rao basement wall, and draws the front in red marker from Wichita to Muskogee: 220 miles.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Most of it is still local: the creek footbridge (neutral, fought over anyway at 2:00), the river bridge to Sand Springs, and the Frontier Wall, where the North and South exchange about 6,000 rounds between 10:00 am and 4:00 pm. The war&#39;s first long-range action is a cartel embargo: House Whitaker refuses to ship spares to any war allied with the South, and Norman&#39;s machines start failing by Friday.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-day-30-friday-march-3-the-peak\">Day 30 — Friday, March 3: the peak</h3>\n<p>Packable depth is 74.0&quot; and the pack on the ground is still 24&quot; settled. The North has about 19,800 kids on its rolls and 240 engines; the South about 14,100 and 175. Schools are closed for the 23rd school day. It is 10°F at dawn and 29°F by afternoon, the 30th day in a row below freezing.</p>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Priya is up at 5:30 taping 9 printed sheets of road map together on the Rao basement wall: Kansas, Oklahoma, Missouri and Arkansas, with every belligerent in the Turnpike Accord marked in red. At 7:00 am Ada uses her one order of the week from the throne hall: cocoa at every milecastle at 10:00. Tessa and Lila spend the morning at the Oduya dining table writing letters of alliance to wars they have never seen, copied out by hand 6 times each, Rule 4 at the bottom of every one.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>8:15 am.</strong> Fort Smith&#39;s war, allied to the South through Owen&#39;s cousin, attacks Fayetteville&#39;s across the Arkansas line over a plow pile at a church both towns&#39; kids use.</p></li><li><p><strong>9:40 am.</strong> Fayetteville asks the Turnpike Accord for help. The North answers in 11 minutes. Springfield, Missouri, joins with Fayetteville at 10:05.</p></li><li><p><strong>10:30 am, Continental War (208).</strong> Little Rock&#39;s war, the <strong>Arkansas River League</strong>, comes in on Fayetteville&#39;s side and the North&#39;s at 10:30, and the chain closes: one conflict from Wichita, Kansas to Little Rock, Arkansas. Priya runs a string along the red line through every belligerent and measures it against the map&#39;s scale bar: <strong>400 miles</strong>.</p><ul><li><p>North: Fri Mar 3, 10:30 am · South: Sat Mar 4, 9:40 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Noon.</strong> Priya&#39;s count of belligerents reaches <strong>19</strong>: Kessler North; the Kessler South coalition; the Pinecrest columns, counted as their own belligerent since Tuesday and still counted with the South; the Lynn Lane Confederacy; Sand Springs; Owasso; Jenks–Bixby; Bartlesville; Edmond; Muskogee; Stillwater; Oklahoma City; Norman; Wichita; Joplin; Fort Smith; Fayetteville; Springfield; and Little Rock. Nobody can name all 19 from memory, including Priya, and the count is still rising.</p></li><li><p><strong>4:00 pm.</strong> Each belligerent&#39;s chronicler phones or texts in a head count for the day. The total, counting only kids who threw at least once, is <strong>about 400,000</strong>. Kessler&#39;s own rolls account for only 33,900 of them. Oklahoma City alone reports 96,000, a number no other belligerent believes and nobody in Oklahoma City can explain.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The war is fought on four fronts at once. On the Frontier Wall, the North and South exchange about 52,000 rounds between 9:00 am and 6:00 pm, the most of any day of the war; the South&#39;s engines open on the Kessler gate at 9:00 and its blitz columns go for milecastles 3 and 4 at 1:15, and are held at both. At the creek footbridge the Confederacy, now allied with the North, holds the bridge against Sand Springs, which has joined the South, for 4 hours. At the Arkansas line, Fort Smith takes the church plow pile at 11:20 and loses it at 2:50. And by cartel embargo: House Whitaker&#39;s refusal to ship to the South&#39;s allies leaves Norman with 3 working machines out of 19. Hits on the Frontier Wall: 13,050 to 12,480, with 4,100 disputed. <strong>Peak tier: Continental War (#208).</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-en-the-slow-melt-and-aftermath\">The slow melt and aftermath</h3>\n<p>No more snow falls. The Great Storm is the last event of the winter, and everything after March 3 is fought on snow already on the ground and slowly leaving: 24&quot; settled on March 3, 9&quot; in open yards by March 17, bare yards on March 24, and the last plow pile gone on April 14. The war comes down tier by tier as the pack shrinks, starting at its edges: the other cities&#39; wars, on thinner snow, melt out first.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-saturday-march-4-low-14f-high-31f\">Saturday, March 4 (low 14°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Fort Smith signs a formal alliance with the South at 8:00 am, by text, copied onto cardboard by Marcus. Two of the North&#39;s clients try to renegotiate while the North is busy with three other states: the Halvorsen culvert and the church-lot corner.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:40 am.</strong> The South reaches Continental War, a day behind the North, the moment Fort Smith&#39;s signature is taped to the Brandt garage door.</p></li><li><p><strong>The renegotiations.</strong> The culvert succeeds. Its 6 engines, which have stood uncrewed in a row since February 25, are swapped for a standing delivery of cocoa at 3:00 every afternoon. The church lot asks for a seventh engine and is turned down.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The North&#39;s deep raiders take the South&#39;s main depot at the Brandt garage, 1,100 rounds, while the South is attacking the Halvorsen province. Along the Wall, about 21,000 rounds. In Wichita, the canal wall is overrun by Edmond&#39;s ally from the north side of town, and Wichita leaves the war at 5:00 pm, its first belligerent to go.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-sunday-march-5-low-19f-high-31f\">Sunday, March 5 (low 19°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The forecast finally shows a high above freezing for Monday, and 45°F rain for Tuesday and Wednesday. The glazing crews pour a last coat on every south-facing wall Saturday night, 40 gallons per fort. The imperial walls stand at the full gauge, 14 ft, on every face, and the Frontier Wall runs unbroken for all 19 miles.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Noon.</strong> Priya&#39;s map shows 17 belligerents: Wichita and Joplin are out. Two new ones, Claremore and Sapulpa, have joined, both on the North&#39;s side, so the count is still rising at the middle and falling at the ends.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> All day, mostly raids on depots and quarries, which will melt faster than the walls because they&#39;re piles. The Beacon church plow pile, now 9 ft, changes hands twice. About 14,000 rounds.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-monday-march-6-low-24f-high-38f-the-first-thaw\">Monday, March 6 (low 24°F, high 38°F): the first thaw</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> For the first time in 32 days a day reaches 32°F, at 11:40 am. At 6:00 am the district announces that schools stay closed this week because of the rain coming Tuesday, and that spring break the week after means classes resume Monday, March 20.</p>\n<ul><li><p>The walls sweat by noon but stand. Rounds packed from the sweating faces come out half slush and twice as heavy. Both sides call it cheating, and both do it.</p></li><li><p>Oklahoma City&#39;s and Norman&#39;s wars, on 14&quot; of pack, are gone by dark. Priya takes them off the map with a pencil eraser.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The Slush Battle at the Kessler gate runs 1:00–4:30 pm. Slush rounds don&#39;t shatter on glaze, they stick, and the South uses them to blind 4 of the North&#39;s firing notches. The North answers by moving its slingers into the casemates. 3,400 rounds, 960 hits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-tuesday-march-7-friday-march-10-the-rain\">Tuesday, March 7 – Friday, March 10: the rain</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> The Spreader Ministry calls an emergency meeting in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room on Monday night: the machines can&#39;t lay snow that isn&#39;t there, and rain will strip the glaze in a day.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>March 7–8.</strong> 0.9&quot; of rain over two days. The glaze goes first, then the outer foot of every wall. The palace dome on the school field slumps 8 ft at the crown and is closed at 4:00 pm Wednesday with nobody inside, following the citadel rule. Every street the plows narrowed floods curb to curb.</p></li><li><p><strong>March 9.</strong> Little Rock, Fayetteville, Fort Smith and Springfield melt out on the same day. The Arkansas River League sends a last text to the Turnpike Accord: a photo of a bare yard.</p></li><li><p><strong>March 10.</strong> The ministry declares the resurfacing season over. The 4,000 staff go back to being kids with machines, and the machines go back into garages, numbered. Priya&#39;s map is down to 6 belligerents, all within 30 miles of Kessler Court.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> Almost none on the rain days: the Border Commission rules that rain is a truce. On Thursday and Friday the fighting is in the snow that&#39;s left: the shaded north faces of the walls, where it is still 5 ft.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-monday-march-13-friday-march-17-spring-break\">Monday, March 13 – Friday, March 17: spring break</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Both sides know this is the last full-day fighting the winter will give them. The North spends Sunday consolidating everything that&#39;s left into one fortress on the shaded side of Kessler Court, rebuilding the Frontier Wall&#39;s last 300 ft from the slumped sections. The South does the same 60 ft away.</p>\n<ul><li><p>The last engines are moved into the court: 14 on the North, 11 on the South.</p></li><li><p>The Lynn Lane Confederacy, the last outside belligerent, melts out on Wednesday, March 15, and sends 6 kids to fight for the North on Thursday.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> The Siege of Kessler Court runs all five days, 9:00 am to 5:30 pm, with a cocoa truce at noon. It is the war at its beginning: two forts, one street, the same December lines, but with 25 engines and 400 kids. The last engine shot of the war is fired on <strong>Thursday, March 16, at 4:44 pm</strong>: a North trebuchet at the South&#39;s capital wall, 260 ft, a hit. Friday is fought by hand.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-monday-march-20-school\">Monday, March 20: school</h4>\n<p><strong>Preparation.</strong> Schools reopen after 28 school days closed: February 1 through March 10, then spring break. The bus comes at 7:31 for the first time since January 31, and 6 kids on Kessler Court are standing on the snow wall stub by the mailboxes to catch it.</p>\n<p><strong>The fighting.</strong> After school only, from 3:25 to 5:00 pm, on 9&quot; of gray snow in the shade. About 600 rounds. Nobody keeps count of hits, for the first time since February 3.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-march-24-april-14-the-last-of-it\">March 24 – April 14: the last of it</h4>\n<ul><li><p><strong>March 24.</strong> The yards are bare. The last 9 ft of the Frontier Wall stands in the shade between #14 and #9, and both sides still claim it.</p></li><li><p><strong>April 1.</strong> The wall stub is 3 ft. Owen and Tessa, each alone, are seen checking on it from their own porches at 7:00 am.</p></li><li><p><strong>April 14, 3:20 pm.</strong> The last plow pile in the empire, at the Kessler gate, is gone. <strong>(No Ammo)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<h4 id=\"ks-en-the-aftermath\">The aftermath</h4>\n<ul><li><p><strong>April – May.</strong> The Border Commission keeps meeting at a lunch table until the last week of May. It hears 140 disputes over the winter and has 113 of its rulings accepted. The 27 rejected are all about February 25.</p></li><li><p><strong>May 19.</strong> The South&#39;s chronicle is finished: 41 pages, 3 maps, and its famous first line about the storm that started it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Summer 2028.</strong> Lila copies all four treaties, the Imperial Gauge and the lines of succession into a three-ring binder. Rule 4 is the last article of every treaty in it, in the same words. Priya keeps the printer running all summer and prints nothing but spare clutch springs.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday, December 10, 2028, 9:40 pm.</strong> The second anniversary, <strong>2 A.S.</strong> Two hundred and twelve kids stand for one minute at the Kessler mailboxes, where the gate was. Ada is 7 and doesn&#39;t remember any of it. Sam is 9 and remembers all of it, differently.</p></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-how-the-record-goes-wrong\">How the record goes wrong</h2>\n<p>By February 25, roughly 30,000 kids are in the war, and fewer than 20 of them were on Kessler Court on November 24, 2026. To everyone else, <strong>the storm</strong> means the Great Storm.</p>\n<p>The South coalition&#39;s chronicler, a Pinecrest sixth-grader, opens the spring write-up with a line from Saturday, February 12, the day the South reached Total Snow War, nine days after the snow stopped:</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>The war has outlasted the storm that started it by nine days.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>That sentence is the Total Snow War tier. It was written by someone who wasn&#39;t there, about the wrong storm, at a step the North had passed the day before. From then on the record has two origins: Dev&#39;s calendar counts from December 10, 2026, and the chronicle counts from February 2028. Neither mentions November 24.</p>\n<p><strong>Where the ladder picks it up:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>No Ammo:</strong> the older siblings&#39; accounts &quot;place it in a different year.&quot; Marcus says the throw hit; Lila says it never left Owen&#39;s hand.</p></li><li><p><strong>Founding Myths</strong> and <strong>Mythic:</strong> eight versions of the first throw, and &quot;no record of the first throw survives,&quot; because the real one is dated before the calendar begins.</p></li><li><p><strong>Hereditary Aristocracy:</strong> command passes down by birth order, so for 31 hours the war is run by the people least able to remember November 24. Ada was 4 at the time.</p></li><li><p><strong>Settlement Law:</strong> Rule 4 is copied forward into 412 articles and becomes the last one. The law is later bound as volume nine, and volume nine is lost.</p></li><li><p><strong>The Inherited Grievance:</strong> the cause survives only as &quot;volume nine, article four hundred and twelve,&quot; and four reconstructions disagree on which side was wronged. The original question was never who was wronged. It was <strong>whether the snowball happened, and whose snow it was.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>The Spreader Schism:</strong> the war splits over whether laid snow is real snow or a forgery. That is Tessa&#39;s objection on November 24, argued by people who have never heard of November 24.</p></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ks-en-ledger\">Ledger</h2>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Year Zero (2026–27)</th><th>Year One (2027–28)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Key dates</td><td>Sketch Sep 12 · disputed throw Nov 24 · first flake Dec 10, 9:40 pm</td><td>Storm Jan 31 – Feb 3 · court crowned Fri Feb 25 · peak Fri Mar 3 · schools reopen Mon Mar 20 · last snow Apr 14</td></tr><tr><td>Storms</td><td>Dec 10–11 (2.5&quot;) · Jan 21–22 (4.5&quot;) · ice Feb 9 (0.1&quot;) · trace Mar 3, 2027</td><td><strong>40.0&quot; (6.0&quot; snowball + 34.0&quot; thundersnow)</strong>, after a trace on Dec 18, 2027; the last snow of the winter</td></tr><tr><td>Packable depth at peak</td><td>2.5&quot; · 4.5&quot;</td><td><strong>74.0&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Peak tiers</td><td>Fort Building (#84, Fri Dec 11) · Fortress &amp; Siege (#102, Fri Jan 22)</td><td><strong>Continental War (#208, Fri Mar 3)</strong>, after Hereditary Aristocracy (#205) and Client Kingdoms (#206) on Fri Feb 25 and The Spreader Cartels (#207) on Mon Feb 28</td></tr><tr><td>Total Snow War (#182)</td><td>—</td><td>Passed: North Fri Feb 11, South Sat Feb 12</td></tr><tr><td>Participants</td><td>11 → 18</td><td>30 → 300+ → 1,100 → about 2,200 in provinces → 60,000 hands at the Great Recut → 19 belligerents and about 400,000 counted throwers on Mar 3</td></tr><tr><td>Main wall</td><td>30&quot; × 12&quot; × 16 ft → 40&quot; × 18&quot; × 20 ft</td><td>220 ft per alliance → <strong>550 × 14 × 8 ft</strong> per settlement → 1,650 ft at the capital</td></tr><tr><td>Longest work</td><td>—</td><td>The Frontier Wall, 19 miles, through the middle of Kessler Court; the front, 400 miles from Wichita to Little Rock</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>1 → 3 licensed + 4 counterfeit</td><td>14 licensed by January, all printed in the Rao basement → 1,100 at the Continental Spreader Works → 4 cartel houses at 44 marshmallows a screw</td></tr><tr><td>Documents</td><td>Envelope (4 rules) → Manual (22 pp) → Rules of Kessler Court (9 rules)</td><td>4 treaties · Imperial Gauge · cartel price list · letters of alliance to 17 wars · South chronicle</td></tr><tr><td>Rule 4</td><td>Reserved</td><td>Last article of every treaty and every letter of alliance; still reserved</td></tr><tr><td>Who commands</td><td>Tessa · Owen</td><td>Ada, 6 and Sam, 8, for 31 hours each, then Tessa and Owen again</td></tr><tr><td>School days closed</td><td>—</td><td>28 (Feb 1 – Mar 10), then spring break</td></tr><tr><td>First to every tier</td><td>North</td><td>North</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p class=\"ks-note\">The whole story, same facts and numbers, said the way a Yooper would: press 🗣️ Yoopanese at the top of the page.</p>\n", "yooper": "<div class=\"ks-title\">Da Snow War: How She Got Started</div>\n<p>Da Snow War started on Kessler Court in Tulsa wit&#39; a snowball nobody can prove got thrown, an&#39; it hit its continental age in da Great Storm of February 2028: 34&quot; of thundersnow an&#39; 6&quot; of snowball snow, 74&quot; packable, War Across Da Whole Continent (tier 208 of 327). Holy wah.</p>\n<p><em>A made-up origin for da Snow War ladder, an&#39; it ain&#39;t a real event. Tier names an&#39; numbers come from da current 327-tier ladder.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-whos-who\">Who&#39;s who</h2>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Name</th><th>Age at start</th><th>House</th><th>Side</th><th>What dey do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tessa Oduya</td><td>10</td><td>#14</td><td>North</td><td>Drew da sketch; da machine&#39;s designer</td></tr><tr><td>Owen Brandt</td><td>10</td><td>#9, right across da street</td><td>South</td><td>T&#39;rew, or didn&#39;t t&#39;row, da first snowball</td></tr><tr><td>Lila Brandt</td><td>13</td><td>#9</td><td>North</td><td>Owen&#39;s sister; keeps da rules; fights against her own brother</td></tr><tr><td>Marcus Oduya</td><td>14</td><td>#14</td><td>South</td><td>Tessa&#39;s brother; fights against his own sister</td></tr><tr><td>Dev Rao</td><td>9</td><td>#11, da corner</td><td>North</td><td>Da undecided t&#39;ird kid; later da operator</td></tr><tr><td>Priya Rao</td><td>15</td><td>#11</td><td>Neutral</td><td>Dev&#39;s sister; &quot;don&#39;t care about any of dis&quot;; judge; runs da printer</td></tr><tr><td>Anjali Rao</td><td>—</td><td>Juniper Lane</td><td>North</td><td>Rao cousin; first ammunition maker</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Da older ones each fight against dere own family. Both took da other side&#39;s story about November 24, an&#39; neither one&#39;s ever changed it, ya know.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-part-one-before-snow\">Part One: Before Snow</h2>\n<p>Da war&#39;s real cause is a t&#39;row on Tuesday, November 24, 2026 dat maybe never happened, packed from snow dat maybe wasn&#39;t anybody&#39;s to pack.</p>\n<p><strong>Saturday, September 12, 2026.</strong> Da 2025–26 winter gave Tessa zero snow days. So Tessa draws a machine on graph paper at four squares to da inch: a screw inside a trough, two wheels, a handle, an&#39; an arrow dat says &quot;snow goes here.&quot; One wheel&#39;s drawn bigger dan da other. What it&#39;s for, written in da corner an&#39; lost later: <em>to make it look like a snow day.</em> Snow on da ground: 0&quot;. <strong>(Sketch on Da Graph Paper)</strong></p>\n<p><strong>September 19 – November 14.</strong> Da machine gets printed on a borrowed printer an&#39; proven at da kitchen table:</p>\n<ul><li><p>acorns, September 27;</p></li><li><p>flour, October 3 (da kitchen&#39;s outta commission for two days, holy wah);</p></li><li><p>birdseed, October 10;</p></li><li><p>sand, October 17: 11 kg t&#39;rough da trough, an&#39; da wheels slip once every six pushes.</p></li></ul>\n<p>Owen watches da sand run from da curb an&#39; asks to push it. Tessa says no. Nobody writes it down. It&#39;s da first refusal in a war dat&#39;s gonna get fought over spares later on.</p>\n<p><strong>Tuesday, November 24.</strong> A weak clipper overnight leaves <strong>0.2&quot;</strong> on da car roofs an&#39; 0.1&quot; on da grass at 31°F. By 10:40 am it&#39;s gone.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>7:08 am:</strong> Tessa scrapes two car roofs into da buffer an&#39; lays an 11-foot band down da #14 driveway, pictures taken from down low. <strong>(First Real Snow, Eh)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>7:12 am:</strong> Owen crosses da street, scrapes up <strong>Tessa&#39;s laid band</strong> bare-handed, an&#39; packs somethin&#39; about 1½&quot; across.</p><ul><li><p><strong>Owen:</strong> &quot;I t&#39;rew it from 20 feet an&#39; hit Tessa&#39;s backpack.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>Tessa:</strong> &quot;It never hit. An&#39; it wasn&#39;t yers to t&#39;row. Dat was my snow.&quot;</p></li><li><p><strong>Witnesses:</strong> four at da bus stop. Marcus says it hit. Lila says it never left Owen&#39;s hand. Da other two won&#39;t say.</p></li><li><p><strong>Evidence:</strong> nuttin&#39;. Da backpack&#39;s dry, an&#39; by 7:14 dere&#39;s no trace of it.</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Recess, 10:15 am:</strong> Owen tries about 40 times to do it again off da playground rails. Nuttin&#39; holds. <strong>(A Guy Says → Heard It Down at Da Bar → Dat&#39;s Not How It Went)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<p>Dat mornin&#39; leaves two questions nobody on da block ever settles:</p>\n<ol><li><p><strong>Did da first snowball happen?</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>If it did, whose snow was it: da sky&#39;s or da machine&#39;s?</strong></p></li></ol>\n<p>What da older ones said decides da sides for good. Marcus, who says it hit, ends up on Owen&#39;s side. Lila, who says it never left Owen&#39;s hand, ends up on Tessa&#39;s.</p>\n<p><strong>November 25 – December 9.</strong> Da question splits da bus stop, 5 kids to 4. Nuttin&#39; gets t&#39;rown, &#39;cause dere&#39;s nuttin&#39; to t&#39;row. <strong>(No Ammo, Eh)</strong></p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-part-two-year-zero\">Part Two: Year Zero</h2>\n<p>Da calendar starts at da first flake, 9:40 pm on Thursday, December 10, 2026. From dat minute on da North gets to every tier first, an&#39; da South follows. November 24 falls 16 days Before Snow, outside da reckonin&#39;.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-gettin-ready-monday-december-7-thursday-december-10\">Gettin&#39; ready: Monday, December 7 – Thursday, December 10</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Monday, December 7.</strong> Da models show a closed low swingin&#39; out of da Southwest. Dev, who still ain&#39;t picked a side, reads da forecast out loud at da bus stop to da both of &#39;em.</p></li><li><p><strong>Tuesday, December 8.</strong> Tessa tests da machine in da Oduya garage on a bag of ice smashed up wit&#39; a hammer: a 6-ft band at 12 mm, even enough. She oils both wheels an&#39; da pinion.</p></li><li><p><strong>Wednesday, December 9.</strong> A Winter Storm Watch goes up for 2–4&quot;. Both sides work on Dev all evenin&#39;: Owen wit&#39; his garage bat, Tessa wit&#39; a turn pushin&#39; da machine. Dat night Lila carries her own choppers, chook an&#39; boots across da street to da Oduya mudroom, where dey stay all winter.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, December 10.</strong> Dry gloves get counted on both sides of da street: da North&#39;s got 9 pairs for 5 kids, da South 6 pairs for 6. Schools cancel Friday at 8:50 pm. At 9:40 pm da first flake lands on da Kessler mailboxes, an&#39; da calendar starts.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> None yet. Every kid on da court goes to bed knowin&#39; exactly where dey&#39;ll be standin&#39; at 7:00 am.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-friday-december-11-da-first-day-of-da-war\">Friday, December 11: da first day of da war</h3>\n<p>Schools are closed. Da storm quits at 1:30 pm wit&#39; <strong>2.5&quot;</strong> of snowball snow at 27–31°F.</p>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>North (Tessa)</th><th>South (Owen)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One Crummy Snowball</td><td>7:20 am. Tessa t&#39;rows a 2&quot; curb-grit round at Owen from 20 ft an&#39; misses. Owen: &quot;Dat&#39;s da <em>second</em> one.&quot;</td><td>7:24. Owen t&#39;rows back an&#39; misses too</td></tr><tr><td>One Good Snowball</td><td>7:31. A clean 2¼&quot; round lands on Owen from 35 ft</td><td>7:50. Marcus lands one on Lila</td></tr><tr><td>Little Skirmish</td><td>7:40. Lila joins her brother&#39;s enemies; 12 rounds a side, da North t&#39;rows first</td><td>Same exchange; Marcus joins his sister&#39;s enemies</td></tr><tr><td>Standoff, Eh</td><td>9:30. Forty feet apart, about 20 rounds a side, 5 minutes. <strong>Dev picks da North at 9:35</strong>, &#39;cause Tessa lets Dev push da machine</td><td>Same standoff</td></tr><tr><td>Snowball Fight, Eh</td><td>10:30. 9 kids, coverin&#39; behind da Brandts&#39; minivan</td><td>Same fight</td></tr><tr><td>Runnin&#39; Battle</td><td>12:10 pm. Da North drives da fight across 3 yards an&#39; da #12 driveway, 400 ft</td><td>Pushed back da whole way</td></tr><tr><td>First Wall</td><td>1:45. 20&quot; high, 8&quot; thick, 10 ft long</td><td>2:05</td></tr><tr><td>Da Elbow Wall</td><td>2:10. A 5-ft return covers da open side</td><td>2:40</td></tr><tr><td>Da Backpack Spreader</td><td>2:30. Tessa&#39;s machine resurfaces 60 ft of stripped yard behind da line</td><td>No machine yet</td></tr><tr><td>Da U-Fort</td><td>2:45. T&#39;ree walls, 8 ft by 6 inside</td><td>3:05</td></tr><tr><td>Buildin&#39; Forts</td><td>4:05. Walls <strong>30&quot; high × 12&quot; thick × 16 ft</strong>, 250 rounds stacked behind</td><td>4:30</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>At 3:00 da sides break for <strong>da porch negotiation</strong> at #9: wall height, t&#39;row range, resupply, an&#39; whether Owen&#39;s garage bat an&#39; racquet can be swung. Dey can. Da first exchange across finished walls is at 4:35: Tessa, Lila, Dev an&#39; two Rao cousins (5) against Owen, Marcus an&#39; four others (6). It gets called for dark at 5:25. Hits are 61 to 54, North, wit&#39; 11 disputed.</p>\n<p><strong>Peak tier: Buildin&#39; Forts</strong> (2.5&quot; × 1.0 = 2.5&quot;).</p>\n<p><strong>Rule 4.</strong> Da porch rules cover wall height, range, resupply an&#39; implements. Tessa insists on one more: <em>&quot;Da matter of November 24 is reserved.&quot;</em> Owen agrees, figurin&#39; &quot;reserved&quot; means he was right; Tessa agrees, figurin&#39; it means he wasn&#39;t. Lila writes it in pencil on da back of a Christmas-card envelope, da war&#39;s first written document.</p>\n<p>Da walls slump 6&quot; on Saturday (36°F), drop to 11&quot; on Sunday (45°F), an&#39; da last stub, in da shade on da north side of da South&#39;s wall, is gone at 3:40 pm on Wednesday, December 16. <strong>(No Ammo, Eh)</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-da-machines-an-da-patent-dispute\">Da machines an&#39; da Patent Dispute</h3>\n<ul><li><p><strong>December 26:</strong> Owen shows up wit&#39; a machine built off Marcus&#39;s low-angle pictures of da November 24 band. It&#39;s got da right screw an&#39; trough an&#39; no clutch, &#39;cause da clutch was never in da pictures.</p></li><li><p><strong>December 28:</strong> Tessa prints a second machine from her own files. <strong>(Built Anudder One, North)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Saturday, January 2, 2027:</strong> da case gets heard in da Brandt garage by Marcus (South), Lila (North), an&#39; Priya Rao, 15, called neutral &#39;cause she &quot;don&#39;t care about any of dis.&quot; Priya rules in 4 minutes:</p><ul><li><p>Da design is Tessa&#39;s.</p></li><li><p>Da Brandts can print from her files if every trough carries a scratched <strong>T</strong> wit&#39; one leg longer dan da other.</p></li><li><p>Owen&#39;s clutchless machine can&#39;t be used on a person.</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Owen prints a licensed copy on da Rao printer, an&#39; da South gets its first machine two weeks after da North&#39;s second.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-da-second-storm-friday-january-22-2027\">Da second storm: Friday, January 22, 2027</h3>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready, Tuesday January 19 – Thursday January 21.</strong> Da models agree on an overrunnin&#39; event ahead of an arctic front, an&#39; a Winter Storm Warning for 3–5&quot; follows on Wednesday. Dis time both sides get two days.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da North.</strong> Eleven pairs of dry gloves go into rotation t&#39;rough da Oduya dryer. Dev, da full-time operator now, oils da wheels on both machines. Lila draws a star fort on graph paper, 12 ft a side wit&#39; a 5-ft bastion at every corner, an&#39; spends Wednesday night arguin&#39; da angles wit&#39; a protractor.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da South.</strong> T&#39;ree kids from Beacon Street join up, where a stray December round started a whole separate argument. Owen writes da war&#39;s first siege plan on da back of a math worksheet: outworks first, den t&#39;ree assaults on da North&#39;s front wall.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, January 21.</strong> Schools cancel Friday at 4:30 pm. First flakes at 7:15 pm, 28°F; moderate snow from midnight to 6 am at ½&quot; an hour.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South&#39;s t&#39;ree Beacon recruits test dere arms on da North&#39;s mailbox at 5:00 pm Thursday, before a flake&#39;s fallen, wit&#39; ice outta a cooler. Lila logs it as a treaty violation. Da Border Commission don&#39;t exist yet.</p>\n<p><strong>4.5&quot;</strong> of good packin&#39; snow quits at 11:40 am. Eighteen kids fight: 8 North, 10 South, countin&#39; t&#39;ree from Beacon Street on da South.</p>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Tier</th><th>North</th><th>South</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fortified Position</td><td>8:30 am. Walls 40&quot; high × 18&quot; thick × 20 ft</td><td>9:10</td></tr><tr><td>Outworks</td><td>9:15. Two outworks 20 ft out front, 5 ft × 2 ft × 10&quot; each</td><td>10:00</td></tr><tr><td>Blanketin&#39; Da Glacis</td><td>10:15. An even coat laid across da open ground so every footprint shows</td><td>Never, dat day; no machine on da field</td></tr><tr><td>Siege Starts</td><td>11:30. Da South&#39;s 3 assaults fall short of da wall, an&#39; da North&#39;s sock slings lob over da South&#39;s wall from 80 ft</td><td>12:40. Da South answers back da same way</td></tr><tr><td>Da Star Fort</td><td>12:30 pm. Lila argues da bastion angles wit&#39; a protractor; nobody rushes it twice</td><td>3:00</td></tr><tr><td>Fortress an&#39; Siege</td><td>1:50. 6 in da fort, 3 in reserve, about 900 rounds a side, da middle ground empty up to 40 minutes at a stretch</td><td>2:15, once its own reserve is in place</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>It gets called for dark at 5:25. Hits are 212 to 198, North, wit&#39; 34 disputed. <strong>Peak tier: Fortress an&#39; Siege</strong> (4.5&quot;).</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>January 23:</strong> da first <strong>Coverage Contest</strong>. Owen lays 1,900 sq ft an&#39; gets disqualified for a 2 mm layer ya can&#39;t see from da street. Priya writes da rule: <em>a layer&#39;s gotta be visible from da road at dusk.</em></p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday night, January 24:</strong> Tessa an&#39; Dev lay an even coat across da #9 front yard. At 7:25 Monday mornin&#39; Lila sees da parallel tread marks from her own window an&#39; says nuttin&#39;. Owen finds &#39;em himself at 7:45. <strong>(Snow Dat Maybe Fell)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>February:</strong> clutchless copies show up at school; 3 of 4 quit inside a week. <strong>(Knockoff Spreaders)</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Tuesday, February 9:</strong> 0.1&quot; of freezin&#39; drizzle scores only a trace. Owen notices da glazed tent stakes won&#39;t pull outta da ground an&#39; writes <em>&quot;pour water on da wall?&quot;</em> on a math worksheet. He leaves it on da Brandt kitchen table, where his sister eats breakfast.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Wednesday, March 3, 2027.</strong> Da last flakes of Year Zero: a trace, 0.01&quot; on car roofs at 6:50 am, 30°F, gone by 9:20.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>6:55 am:</strong> Tessa an&#39; Dev take N-2 out at da 4-mm detent an&#39; try to gather da whole court&#39;s trace into one band. Ten minutes of pushin&#39; lays a stripe 9 ft long dat ya can only see from down low wit&#39; a flashlight.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:05 am:</strong> Owen crosses da street, like he did on November 24, an&#39; tries to pack somethin&#39; off Tessa&#39;s stripe. Nuttin&#39; holds. Dis time dere&#39;s 6 witnesses, an&#39; all of &#39;em agree, which is a first.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:10 am:</strong> Lila writes it in da notebook as da last event of da season: <em>Can&#39;t Hardly Tell. Nothing thrown. Everyone agrees.</em> It&#39;s da only entry in Year Zero both sides sign.</p></li></ul>\n<p>Da season total at Kessler Court is 7.2&quot; of snow plus dis trace, an&#39; 0.1&quot; of ice, a little under Tulsa&#39;s normal. Not much by Houghton standards, but it was enough.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-summer-2027-an-da-first-anniversary\">Summer 2027 an&#39; da first anniversary</h3>\n<p>Tessa writes it all down in <strong>22 hand-numbered pages</strong>. Four hand copies get made, an&#39; no two agree on da gate table. <strong>(Da Spreader Manual)</strong> Lila copies da envelope into a notebook titled <em>Rules of Kessler Court</em>, 9 rules long. Rule 4 ain&#39;t changed.</p>\n<p><strong>Friday, December 10, 2027, 9:40 pm.</strong> It&#39;s clear an&#39; 34°F. Seven kids stand in da cul-de-sac for one minute at da exact time of da first flake, an&#39; Dev writes <strong>&quot;1 A.S.&quot;</strong> in da back of da manual.</p>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-part-tree-da-great-storm-1-as\">Part T&#39;ree: Da Great Storm (1 A.S.)</h2>\n<p>Da Great Storm of January 31 – February 3, 2028 drops 40.0&quot; on Tulsa, 34.0&quot; of it thundersnow, an&#39; da snow sits dere almost six weeks. Dat&#39;s 74.0&quot; packable: da war climbs 104 tiers in 30 days an&#39; peaks at <strong>War Across Da Whole Continent (tier 208) on Friday, March 3</strong>, after da Hereditary Aristocracy (tier 205) an&#39; da Client Kingdoms (tier 206) on February 25. All-Out Snow War (tier 182) goes by on day 9. Da Great Storm&#39;s da last snow of da winter; everyting after da peak is da slow melt.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-fall-2027-printed-in-a-basement\">Fall 2027: Printed Down in Da Basement</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> After da first anniversary da war&#39;s got a manual, a calendar an&#39; t&#39;ree machines, an&#39; a waitin&#39; list: 31 kids on t&#39;ree streets want a licensed copy. Only one printer&#39;s gonna print &#39;em. It&#39;s in da Rao basement, an&#39; it belongs to Priya, who&#39;s said she don&#39;t care about any of dis.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Saturday, December 11, 2027.</strong> Tessa, Lila an&#39; Dev go down da Rao basement stairs wit&#39; da files on a thumb drive an&#39; a bag of marshmallows. Priya&#39;s terms take 6 minutes: 1 machine per 11 hours of printin&#39;, 4 a week at most if nuttin&#39; warps, filament paid for by da kid gettin&#39; da machine, an&#39; cocoa every 2 hours while it runs. Da South&#39;s request comes in at 4:00 dat same afternoon an&#39; gets da same terms, second in line.</p></li><li><p><strong>December 13 – January 30.</strong> Da printer runs overnight wit&#39; nobody watchin&#39;, 11 hours a machine. T&#39;ree prints warp in da cold basement an&#39; get scrapped. Every finished trough gets da scratched <strong>T</strong> before it leaves da basement. Da North collects machines 1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 11 an&#39; 13; da South gets 4, 7, 10, 12 an&#39; 14, always one spot behind.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da bottleneck.</strong> By January da problem ain&#39;t da design, it&#39;s filament: a 1-kg spool makes about 2.5 machines. Priya, who&#39;s never t&#39;rown a snowball in da war, is now da most important person in it, an&#39; both sides know it. Her cocoa shows up on time, twice, from both houses. <strong>(Printed Down in Da Basement)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Dere&#39;s nuttin&#39; to fight wit&#39;, so da war gets fought over printer time. Da North gets Tuesday an&#39; Thursday nights by tradin&#39; its Saturday slot for 40 marshmallows. Owen tries to jump da line by printin&#39; a machine at a public library at 9:00 am on January 8, an&#39; da library&#39;s printer jams on da screw. Dat&#39;s da closest da war comes to a shot fired in December or January.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-saturday-december-18-2027-da-trace\">Saturday, December 18, 2027: da trace</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da forecast for Friday night says <em>flurries possible, no accumulation</em>. Nobody believes it, an&#39; both sides stage dere machines on da porches anyway: da North&#39;s 5, da South&#39;s 2.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>6:40 am.</strong> A trace, 0.02&quot; on car roofs an&#39; da tops of da mailboxes at 29°F, gone by 9:30.</p></li><li><p><strong>6:52 am.</strong> Tessa an&#39; Dev take N-1 an&#39; N-2 out at da 4-mm detent an&#39; try to gather da court&#39;s trace into a band. After 15 minutes it&#39;s a faint stripe on da #14 driveway dat only shows up in Lila&#39;s picture from down low.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:10 am.</strong> Owen an&#39; Marcus come out wit&#39; dere two machines an&#39; get even less on da Brandt side.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:15 am.</strong> Dev scoops a gloveful off da stripe to see what it does. It whitens da dark chopper for about two seconds an&#39; it&#39;s gone; close yer fist on it an&#39; it goes right back to da air.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> None. Nuttin&#39; packs, so nuttin&#39; gets t&#39;rown, an&#39; nobody argues about it. Lila logs it in da notebook as <strong>Can&#39;t Hardly Tell</strong>. It&#39;s da only snow on Kessler Court between March 3, 2027 an&#39; January 31, 2028.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-january-26-31-2028-da-week-before\">January 26 – 31, 2028: da week before</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> By January, Priya&#39;s printin&#39; has put <strong>14 licensed machines</strong> on t&#39;ree streets.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Wednesday, January 26.</strong> Da long-range models show a deep upper low formin&#39; over da Four Corners by da next Monday, wit&#39; arctic air already sittin&#39; over Kansas. Dev reads da forecast discussion out loud at da bus stop, an&#39; one phrase gets repeated all week: <em>&quot;potentially historic.&quot;</em> Dat night da North starts a list on da Oduya fridge: gloves, shovels, recyclin&#39; bins for block molds, sleds, marshmallows.</p></li><li><p><strong>Thursday, January 27.</strong> Tessa redraws da December lines on graph paper at four squares to da inch, wit&#39; every tent stake marked, &#39;cause dey&#39;re gonna be under 3 ft of snow. Da South buys 6 snow shovels at da hardware store at 4:30 pm, which leaves da store wit&#39; none.</p></li><li><p><strong>Friday, January 28.</strong> A Winter Storm Watch goes up. Da North dries 31 pairs of gloves in rotation t&#39;rough t&#39;ree dryers. Mr. Oduya brings 24 sled slats down from da attic &quot;just in case.&quot; Lila moves Owen&#39;s math worksheet from da kitchen drawer to da back of her own notebook, reads it, an&#39; puts it back.</p></li><li><p><strong>Saturday, January 29.</strong> Both sides hold a muster. Da North&#39;s in da Oduya garage counts 9 kids, 9 machines an&#39; 6 shovels. Da South&#39;s in da Brandt garage counts 10 kids, 5 machines an&#39; 10 shovels.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday, January 30.</strong> Da Watch turns into a Warning for 10–16&quot;. Parents on da court agree on da rules over coffee at #12: 45 minutes outside, 20 inside, an&#39; no tunnels any grown-up ain&#39;t seen.</p></li><li><p><strong>Monday, January 31, 3:00 pm.</strong> It gets bumped up to a <strong>Blizzard Warning</strong>, local amounts over 18&quot;, thundersnow possible. Schools close Tuesday, den Wednesday, before a flake falls. At 9:00 pm da North&#39;s 9 machines are folded an&#39; staged in da Oduya garage, wings in, gates at 12 mm.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> None. Both sides spend da week watchin&#39; da sky an&#39; each other&#39;s garages.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-da-storm\">Da storm</h3>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Time</th><th>Temp</th><th>What falls</th><th>Rate</th><th>Runnin&#39; total</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mon Jan 31, 10:15 pm</td><td>26°F</td><td>First flakes, snowball snow</td><td>½&quot;/hr</td><td>0&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Tue Feb 1, 2:00 pm</td><td>24°F</td><td>Snowball snow quits</td><td>—</td><td><strong>6.0&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Tue 2:40 pm</td><td>22°F</td><td><strong>First thunder</strong>; thundersnow in da deformation band</td><td>1–2&quot;/hr</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Tue 11 pm – Wed 1 am</td><td>19°F</td><td>Worst of da thundersnow, 47 strikes counted from da Oduya window</td><td>4&quot;/hr</td><td>17.5&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Wed Feb 2</td><td>18–15°F</td><td>Thundersnow in bursts, gusts to 45 mph</td><td>½–2&quot;/hr</td><td>35.0&quot;</td></tr><tr><td>Thu Feb 3, 3:20 am</td><td>14°F</td><td>Last thunder, den da snow quits</td><td>—</td><td><strong>40.0&quot;</strong></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Packable depth:</strong> 6.0&quot; × 1.0 + 34.0&quot; thundersnow × 2.0 = <strong>74.0&quot;</strong>, War Across Da Whole Continent.</p></li><li><p><strong>Drifts:</strong> 11 ft against da north side of every house on Kessler Court.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da slow melt:</strong> an arctic high parks over da Plains, an&#39; a second arctic front backs it up on February 27. Lows run −8 to 19°F an&#39; no day hits 32°F till Monday, March 6, t&#39;ree days after da peak. Da pack settles from 39&quot; to 24&quot; but it don&#39;t leave. Rain on March 7–8 floods every street da plows narrowed, an&#39; da district gives up on da week: Tulsa schools stay closed t&#39;rough spring break an&#39; open back up <strong>Monday, March 20</strong>, after 28 school days closed.</p></li><li><p><strong>Realism:</strong> nuttin&#39; like dis is in Oklahoma&#39;s records. It sits right where da ladder says: in da Upper Age, past anyting even da snowbelt&#39;s seen. Not even Houghton, eh.</p></li></ul>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-da-climb\">Da climb</h3>\n<p>Da North gets to every tier first. Da South follows a few hours or a day behind, an&#39; copies most of what it sees.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-1-thursday-february-3-low-9f-high-14f-wind-chill-10f\">Day 1 — Thursday, February 3 (low 9°F, high 14°F, wind chill −10°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> At 6:30 am, t&#39;ree hours after da last thunder, da North musters in da Oduya garage. Da parents set one rule before anybody goes out: 45 minutes outside, 20 minutes in, no exceptions, checked against da microwave clock. Lila reads da inventory out loud: 9 kids, 6 snow shovels, 4 recyclin&#39; bins to use as block molds, 2 sleds, 9 machines wit&#39; da wings folded, 31 pairs of dry gloves goin&#39; &#39;round t&#39;rough t&#39;ree dryers. Da plan got drawn on graph paper Wednesday, four squares to da inch: build back on da December tent stakes, which are under 3 ft of snow now an&#39; have to be found wit&#39; a broom handle. Da South musters at 8:15 in da Brandt garage wit&#39; 10 kids, 4 shovels an&#39; 1 licensed machine, workin&#39; from no plan at all.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Battlements (105).</strong> Da North cuts blocks wit&#39; da recyclin&#39; bins, packs &#39;em 2 ft high in two staggered courses, an&#39; has walls 4–5 ft high an&#39; 20&quot; thick by 9:40 am. Dev cuts four firin&#39; notches a side, 10&quot; wide, wit&#39; a dustpan. A tower goes up at da northeast corner, 2 ft over da wall, an&#39; da first lookout sees da South&#39;s door open from 100 ft. Da South copies it by 4:30 pm.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 3, 9:40 am · da South got dere Thu Feb 3, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreadin&#39; After Dark (106).</strong> At 6:00 pm da North&#39;s 9 machines go out under da porch lights. Da reflective strips on da wings, added for road safety an&#39; grumbled about since September, are da only way da crews find each other. Da South&#39;s one machine goes out at 8:00.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 3, 6:00 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 3, 8:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Standin&#39; Watch (107).</strong> Lila draws a shift chart on da back of da Wednesday plan: four to a fort, 2-hour shifts, first light to full dark. It starts at 7:10 am da next day; da South&#39;s starts Saturday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 4, 7:10 am · da South got dere Sat Feb 5, 7:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Quinzhee (108).</strong> At 10:30 da North shovels a heap 6 ft high an&#39; 11 ft across next to da fort. Before lunch, 12 sticks a foot long, cut off a trimmed redbud, get pushed in from outside to show how deep to dig. Da heap sits 2 hours to set up while everybody eats. Hollowin&#39; runs 1:15–2:30, an&#39; da diggers stop at every stick. It seats two, t&#39;ree if dey&#39;re friends. Da South&#39;s heap gets shoveled at dusk an&#39; hollowed Friday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 3, 2:30 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 4, 10:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader da Depots (109).</strong> Da Oduya garage becomes Depot 1: da 9 machines get numbered N-1 to N-9 in marker, wit&#39; 30 spares off Priya&#39;s printer sorted into labeled shoeboxes (wings, tread bands, clutch springs).</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 3, 3:45 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 4, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Whole Buncha Forts (110).</strong> At 1:00 pm Beacon Street&#39;s 11 kids walk out of da South&#39;s fort over who gets da tower an&#39; build dere own at da end of da court. Dat makes t&#39;ree strongholds, 400 ft of frontage an&#39; 30 kids. Da North signs Beacon Street into an alliance at 3:10 for 40 marshmallows an&#39; da use of N-7. Da South&#39;s got no ally till Saturday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 3, 3:10 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 5, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Igloo (111), started.</strong> Dev, da one person who&#39;s read about it, starts cuttin&#39; blocks out of da 10-ft drift on da north side of #14 wit&#39; Mrs. Rao&#39;s bread knife: 18&quot; long, 10&quot; high, 6&quot; thick. Forty blocks get cut before dark.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da first exchange of da Great Storm is at 10:05 am, before either side&#39;s got a roof. Owen leads 10 South kids across da court at da North&#39;s half-built wall, each one carryin&#39; 8 rounds in a grocery bag. Da North t&#39;rows from da new notches, 9 t&#39;rowers at about 18 rounds a minute, an&#39; da South&#39;s line stops 25 ft short. By da 10:50 break for da 45/20 rule da hits are 140 to 96. Da afternoon&#39;s a standoff across 60 ft of 3-ft snow nobody can run t&#39;rough. When Beacon Street walks out at 1:00, its 11 kids take dere rounds wit&#39; &#39;em, an&#39; da South&#39;s fire drops by half. Da parents call everybody in at 6:15, 17 minutes after sundown.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-2-friday-february-4-low-6f-high-12f\">Day 2 — Friday, February 4 (low −6°F, high 12°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da cold&#39;s da story. Da parents add a second rule: no tunnelin&#39; unless a grown-up&#39;s seen da tunnel. Mr. Oduya takes da day off work an&#39; brings 24 sled slats down from da attic. Da North&#39;s plan for da day is da trench line, drawn da night before wit&#39; rangin&#39; marks every 25 ft.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Igloo (111), finished.</strong> Da last of 140 blocks closes da spiral at 11:05 am. Inside: 7 ft across, 4½ ft at da crown, an entrance tunnel below floor level. Wit&#39; t&#39;ree inside it reads 30°F against 19°F outside. Da South&#39;s igloo falls in twice an&#39; stands on Sunday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 4, 11:05 am · da South got dere Sun Feb 6, 1:20 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Field Fix-It Kits (112).</strong> Two clutch springs, a spare pinion, four tread bands an&#39; a knife get rolled into a dish towel for every crew. Nine kits by 9:00 am.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 4, 9:00 am · da South got dere Fri Feb 4, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Fieldworks (113).</strong> Eleven North kids cut firin&#39; steps, banked approaches an&#39; two sling lanes, 300 ft in all, from 9:00 to 3:30, workin&#39; in shifts to da 45/20 rule.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 4, 3:30 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 5, noon</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreadin&#39; Da Trench Floor (114) an&#39; Trenches (115).</strong> Da trench goes in 2 ft deep an&#39; 80 ft long along da rangin&#39; marks, an&#39; two narrow machines lay fresh footin&#39; on its churned-up floor. Da South starts its trench at 2:00 an&#39; finishes it Saturday mornin&#39;.</p><ul><li><p>Spreadin' Da Trench Floor (114): da North Fri Feb 4, 1:30 pm · da South Sat Feb 5, 9:15 am</p></li><li><p>Trenches (115): da North Fri Feb 4, 1:45 pm · da South Sat Feb 5, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Supply Trench (116) an&#39; Spreader Sappers (117).</strong> A back trench, 120 ft long an&#39; 3 ft deep, runs from da fort to da Depot 1 garage door. Crews lay cover over da spoil so da South can&#39;t see what&#39;s new.</p><ul><li><p>Supply Trench (116): da North Fri Feb 4, 2:30 pm · da South Sat Feb 5, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Sappers (117): da North Fri Feb 4, 2:45 pm · da South Sat Feb 5, 1:20 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Trench Warfare (118) an&#39; Camo Layer (119).</strong> By 3:00 nobody on da North stands up in da open. Lobbin&#39; planks, cut from a busted bookcase, go into service at 60 degrees.</p><ul><li><p>Trench Warfare (118): da North Fri Feb 4, 2:00 pm · da South Fri Feb 4, 2:20 pm</p></li><li><p>Camo Layer (119): da North Fri Feb 4, 3:00 pm · da South Sat Feb 5, 1:50 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Tunnels (120) an&#39; Tunnel Spreaders (121).</strong> T&#39;ree tunnels get cut into da 10-ft drift, 150 ft in all. Mr. Oduya crawls every foot of &#39;em before anybody else goes in, sets a sled slat every 6 ft, an&#39; marks four entrances, two of &#39;em hidden. N-3 an&#39; N-4 go underground wit&#39; da wings folded an&#39; da gate at 4 mm.</p><ul><li><p>Tunnels (120): da North Fri Feb 4, 4:20 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 9:00 am</p></li><li><p>Tunnel Spreaders (121): da North Fri Feb 4, 4:45 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 9:45 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> At noon da South sends 4 kids to knock in da unfinished igloo. Dey get within 15 ft before Dev, inside it wit&#39; 30 rounds, drives &#39;em off all by himself, an&#39; da igloo gets closed 65 minutes later. From 2:00 pm on it&#39;s trench warfare: lobbin&#39; planks on both sides at 60 degrees, hit rates down from one in four to one in eleven, an&#39; da front moves about 6 ft all afternoon. Hits for da day: 380 to 310.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-3-saturday-february-5-low-1f-high-18f\">Day 3 — Saturday, February 5 (low 1°F, high 18°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> At breakfast in da Brandt kitchen, Lila finds Owen&#39;s math worksheet from last February on da table. One line on it&#39;s circled: <em>&quot;pour water on da wall?&quot;</em> She takes a picture, puts it back exactly where it was, an&#39; walks it across da street. Da North spends da mornin&#39; findin&#39; containers: 2 waterin&#39; cans, 4 milk jugs an&#39; a mop bucket, 7 gallons a trip.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Staff Sling (122) an&#39; Diggin&#39; Under (123).</strong> Two staff slings get built from broom handles an&#39; dish towels. At 10:00 da North drives a 22-ft tunnel under da South&#39;s front wall in four hours, wit&#39; two diggers in shifts an&#39; Mr. Oduya checkin&#39; it twice. At 2:10 pm, 8 ft of da South&#39;s wall drops in a second an&#39; a half.</p><ul><li><p>Staff Sling (122): da North Sat Feb 5, 9:30 am · da South Sat Feb 5, 3:40 pm</p></li><li><p>Diggin' Under (123): da North Sat Feb 5, 2:10 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Spreader Guild (124).</strong> Da 9 North operators organize. To get in ya need an even 5-mm run, da 9-second clutch check from memory, an&#39; da surface rule said word for word.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 5, 11:00 am · da South got dere Sun Feb 6, 10:15 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Keep (125) t&#39;rough Fort Inside a Fort (129).</strong> Da keep&#39;s a walled dome, 7 × 6 × 4 ft inside, shared by da North an&#39; Beacon Street. Da inner bailey goes up behind it, 60 ft of circuit, 5 ft high an&#39; 30&quot; thick, an&#39; da second ring 30 ft out by dark. Da South builds its keep Sunday.</p><ul><li><p>Da Keep (125): da North Sat Feb 5, 3:00 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>Touchin' Up Da Keep (126): da North Sat Feb 5, 3:20 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 11:45 am</p></li><li><p>Inner Bailey (127): da North Sat Feb 5, 3:40 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Registry (128): da North Sat Feb 5, 3:55 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 1:45 pm</p></li><li><p>Fort Inside a Fort (129): da North Sat Feb 5, 4:00 pm · da South Sun Feb 6, 4:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Iced-Up Walls (130).</strong> At 4:10 pm, at 3°F, da North pours 7 gallons a side down da outside faces. It freezes inside da hour, an&#39; da first South round to hit it shatters. Owen sees da glaze at 8:30, figures out where da idea went, an&#39; has da South&#39;s walls poured by 8:50 pm. Dat worksheet never gets left on da kitchen table again.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 5, 4:10 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 5, 8:50 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> When da South&#39;s wall drops at 2:10 pm, 14 North kids go t&#39;rough da 8-ft gap wit&#39; 20 rounds each. Dey hold da inside of da South&#39;s fort for 25 minutes, till Marcus brings da South&#39;s reserve of 9 up t&#39;rough its own trench an&#39; pushes &#39;em back out. Da gap&#39;s filled back in by 3:30. At 4:10 da first South round to hit da new glaze shatters in front of 20 witnesses on both sides. Hits for da day: 410 to 260, da North&#39;s biggest margin yet.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-4-sunday-february-6-low-4f-high-20f\">Day 4 — Sunday, February 6 (low 4°F, high 20°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Juniper Lane, Anjali&#39;s street, walks over at 8:00 am, 23 kids strong, an&#39; joins da North. Dat makes 43 on da North wit&#39; Beacon Street, enough to staff jobs &#39;stead of forts. Lila writes da first job board on da Oduya garage door in dry-erase marker: glazin&#39;, slings, engines, depot, cocoa.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Armoured Spreaders (131).</strong> Curved shields cut outta a plastic kiddie pool go over da troughs of N-1 to N-4, addin&#39; 4 kg to each.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 9:00 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Glazin&#39; Crews (132).</strong> T&#39;ree kids per fort haul 40 gallons a night from da Oduya basement tap, 60 ft in relays, 11 trips. Unglazed sections get marked on da map in red.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 9:15 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 9:40 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Doctrine (133).</strong> Tessa writes da t&#39;ree rules on an index card: resurface before da packers run dry, never while somebody&#39;s watchin&#39;, never da same ground twice in a day.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 9:45 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 10:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Barbican (134), started.</strong> Eleven kids push da main gate out into a 20-ft passage bent twice at right angles, 6 ft high, wit&#39; a drop slot over da second bend. It takes till Monday afternoon.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 10:30 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, noon</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Sling Corps (135).</strong> T&#39;irty slingers drill in two ranks of 15, t&#39;rowin&#39; on Lila&#39;s count. It takes 9 sessions over 3 days to get a decent volley, an&#39; a t&#39;ird of &#39;em never get it.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 11:00 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 1:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Corps (136).</strong> Nine operators run a rollin&#39; line off da plow berm at da end of da court, 40 ft of fresh cover a pass.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 12:30 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 2:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Siege Engines (137).</strong> Da parts get gathered da night before: a plastic sled from da Rao garage, a fence post from da Nelsons&#39; blown-down section, an&#39; four bungee cords dat Lila takes outta her dad&#39;s truck, on da South&#39;s side of da street, wit&#39;out askin&#39;. Five kids put it togedder in 2 hours. At 2:40 pm it t&#39;rows a 4&quot; round a measured 110 ft an&#39; falls apart on da second shot. Mr. Brandt misses da cords Monday mornin&#39;, which is also when Owen figures out how a catapult gets made. Da South&#39;s first engine flies Monday at 3:15.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 2:40 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 3:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreader Go-Between (138).</strong> Dev becomes da runner between da machines an&#39; da engine, an&#39; da only one allowed to stop a launch mid-load.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 3:00 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 3:40 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ballista (139).</strong> Dev&#39;s bike gives up its inner tube; two fence uprights from da same blown-down section frame it. It puts one round t&#39;rough a South firin&#39; notch at 130 ft at 4:50 pm, wit&#39; a 50-second reload.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 4:50 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Block Tower (140), started.</strong> Six hundred blocks are needed. Da first tower, built straight up, falls at 9 ft on Monday. Da second, tapered from 9 ft square to 5, gets finished Thursday an&#39; stands 19 days.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 6, 5:10 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da catapult&#39;s first an&#39; only shot, at 2:40 pm, lands on da South&#39;s tower an&#39; takes 8&quot; off da top. At 3:30 da South raids Depot 1 to get at da machines, 12 kids along da fence line. Da sling corps&#39; first live volley, 30 rounds in 2 seconds, meets &#39;em at 40 ft, an&#39; da raid turns back wit&#39; no machine taken. At 4:50 da ballista puts a round t&#39;rough a South firin&#39; notch at 130 ft; da South boards up t&#39;ree of its notches wit&#39; a cut-up sled. Hits for da day: 690 to 300.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-5-monday-february-7-low-8f-high-22f\">Day 5 — Monday, February 7 (low 8°F, high 22°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Schools are still closed. Anjali spends Sunday night turnin&#39; da Rao garage into a production line: 3 foldin&#39; tables, 6 ice-cream scoops, a 3&quot; ring cut off a coffee can for a size gauge, an&#39; a tally sheet. Priya, who still don&#39;t care, agrees to keep da printer runnin&#39; if somebody brings her cocoa every 2 hours.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Counter-Battery (141) an&#39; Spread-an&#39;-Pack Cycle (142).</strong> Da North&#39;s second engine gets built to hit da South&#39;s first; it knocks it out at 11:20 am. Packers start workin&#39; machine-laid beds inside da hour while dey still bind.</p><ul><li><p>Counter-Battery (141): da North Mon Feb 7, 11:20 am · da South Tue Feb 8, 9:00 am</p></li><li><p>Spread-an'-Pack Cycle (142): da North Mon Feb 7, 11:45 am · da South Tue Feb 8, 9:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Trebuchet (143).</strong> A pit 2 ft deep gets dug to seat da frame, an&#39; a laundry basket of packed snow is da counterweight. Nine hours of buildin&#39;, 7:00 am to 4:00 pm. Its first shot goes 260 ft, past anyting da South&#39;s got.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 7, 4:15 pm · da South got dere Tue Feb 8, 11:30 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ammo Factory (144).</strong> Anjali&#39;s foreman, wit&#39; 6 workers. Output runs 60–90 rounds an hour; every round gets checked against da coffee-can ring an&#39; da engine cup before it&#39;s stacked. One in eight gets t&#39;rown out. Inventory passes 2,000 by Tuesday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 7, 8:00 am · da South got dere Mon Feb 7, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Resurfacin&#39; Line, Engine Shops, Spreader Foundry (145–147).</strong> A second line lays snow back on stripped ground so da packers never run dry. Spare frames an&#39; arms get kept in da Rao shed. Priya tests two plastics in da family freezer overnight an&#39; picks da one dat don&#39;t shatter; nobody ever second-guesses it.</p><ul><li><p>Resurfacin' Line (145): da North Mon Feb 7, 1:00 pm · da South Tue Feb 8, 1:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Engine Shops (146): da North Mon Feb 7, 2:00 pm · da South Tue Feb 8, 2:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Foundry (147): da North Tue Feb 8, 6:50 am · da South Tue Feb 8, 3:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Snow Quarries (148).</strong> Four cuttin&#39; sites open away from da fightin&#39;, each givin&#39; about 600 rounds a day. Da best is da 9-ft plow pile at da church lot on Beacon Street, which changes hands t&#39;ree times by Friday.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 7, 2:30 pm · da South got dere Tue Feb 8, 3:45 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Casemates, Rangin&#39; Stakes, Same Screw for Everybody (149–151).</strong> Da main front gets t&#39;ickened to 4 ft so firin&#39; rooms can be cut into it, 6 ft wide wit&#39; an 18&quot; opening. Stakes go in every 25 ft out to 200 ft. Every screw on da North gets brought to da 11-cm pattern.</p><ul><li><p>Casemates (149): da North Mon Feb 7, 3:00 pm · da South Tue Feb 8, 4:30 pm</p></li><li><p>Rangin' Stakes (150): da North Mon Feb 7, 3:30 pm · da South Tue Feb 8, 5:15 pm</p></li><li><p>Same Screw for Everybody (151): da North Mon Feb 7, 5:00 pm · da South Tue Feb 8, 7:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Da South spends Monday copyin&#39; Sunday: its first engine, its first glazin&#39; crew, an&#39; a factory in da Brandt kitchen dat Mrs. Brandt shuts down at 4:00 pm.</p>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da engine duel opens at 11:20 am, when da North&#39;s new counter-battery engine knocks out da South&#39;s first catapult on its t&#39;ird shot. Da South answers at 3:15 wit&#39; its own catapult, built from da bungee cords Mr. Brandt finally noticed were missin&#39; dat mornin&#39;. Da trebuchet&#39;s first shot, at 4:15, goes 260 ft over everyting an&#39; lands in da Brandt back yard behind da South&#39;s last wall, where nobody was defendin&#39;. Hits for da day: 1,120 to 640, wit&#39; da factory&#39;s rounds doin&#39; most of da work now.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-6-tuesday-february-8-low-10f-high-25f\">Day 6 — Tuesday, February 8 (low 10°F, high 25°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da war&#39;s outgrown da court. At 7:30 am Tessa, Lila, Dev an&#39; da Beacon an&#39; Juniper captains walk four blocks wit&#39; a 100-ft tape measure an&#39; a clipboard, markin&#39; da line wit&#39; orange spray paint on da snow.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Front Lines (152).</strong> Four blocks, 2,000 ft of frontage. Every two-fort alliance gets one main wall, <strong>6 ft high, 3 ft thick an&#39; 220 ft long</strong>; t&#39;ree alliances wall 660 ft of it. Sixty-four kids, 5 engines, one per 400 ft.</p><ul><li><p>Front Lines (152): da North Tue Feb 8, 8:00 am · da South Wed Feb 9, 8:00 am</p></li><li><p>Spreader Scoutin' (153): da North Tue Feb 8, 9:00 am · da South Wed Feb 9, 8:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Ravelins an&#39; Salients (154–155).</strong> Four wedges go up in front of da wall, each 30 ft along its faces an&#39; 5 ft high, wit&#39; a crew of 5 an&#39; 200 rounds.</p><ul><li><p>Ravelins (154): da North Tue Feb 8, 10:30 am · da South Wed Feb 9, 10:00 am</p></li><li><p>Salients (155): da North Tue Feb 8, 11:15 am · da South Wed Feb 9, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>Shootin' at Da Spreaders (156): da North Tue Feb 8, noon · da South Wed Feb 9, 12:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Bombardment (157).</strong> Nine engines t&#39;row 40 rounds an hour each at da South&#39;s walls. Glazed frontage dat lasted a week now lasts two days.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Tue Feb 8, 8:30 am · da South got dere Wed Feb 9, 1:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Supply Lines t&#39;rough da Depots (158–162).</strong> Sleds get requisitioned from four households dat don&#39;t get asked. A sled tramway runs to within 400 ft of da line. Twenty-two roofed blockhouses go up along da routes over da next four days. Six depots of 1,500 rounds each sit behind da sectors.</p><ul><li><p>Supply Lines (158): da North Tue Feb 8, 1:30 pm · da South Wed Feb 9, 2:30 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Convoys (159): da North Tue Feb 8, 2:00 pm · da South Wed Feb 9, 3:15 pm</p></li><li><p>Spreader Rail Head (160): da North Tue Feb 8, 3:00 pm · da South Wed Feb 9, 4:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Chain of Blockhouses (161): da North Tue Feb 8, 4:00 pm · da South Wed Feb 9, 5:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Da Depots (162): da North Tue Feb 8, 5:00 pm · da South Wed Feb 9, 6:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Spreaders Dat Push Demselves, da Batteries on Runners, da Forward Works (163–165).</strong> Machines get towed on sled frames. Engines move every night so no spot gets used twice. Da works in front of da bailey get rebuilt to da new scale over Wednesday.</p><ul><li><p>Spreaders Dat Push Demselves (163): da North Tue Feb 8, 6:30 pm · da South Thu Feb 10, 7:30 am</p></li><li><p>Da Batteries on Runners (164): da North Tue Feb 8, 9:00 pm · da South Thu Feb 10, 8:30 am</p></li><li><p>Da Forward Works (165): da North Wed Feb 9, 6:45 am · da South Thu Feb 10, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p>Da South gets to Front Lines Wednesday mornin&#39;, wit&#39; its line marked in Owen&#39;s mom&#39;s ketchup &#39;cause da spray paint was on da North&#39;s side of da street.</p>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Front Lines gets fought, for da first time, from a distance. Nine North engines bombard da South&#39;s walls from 8:30 am to 4:00 pm at 40 rounds an hour each. Da South hits da two eastern ravelins at 1:00 wit&#39; 22 kids an&#39; takes one, crew of 5 an&#39; 200 rounds included. Da ravelin gets taken back at 2:45 when a North supply sled, comin&#39; down da new tramway, turns out to be carryin&#39; 6 slingers &#39;stead of rounds. Hits for da day: 2,900 to 1,700.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-7-wednesday-february-9-low-12f-high-27f\">Day 7 — Wednesday, February 9 (low 12°F, high 27°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Tessa spends Tuesday night drawin&#39; da citadel at four squares to da inch: five rooms on a footprint of 34 by 22 ft, 40 ft on da longest side, 6 ft of headroom in da middle, walls 3 ft thick. Da spot is da side yard of #12, da only flat 40 ft on da court. At 7:00 am two parents walk da site wit&#39; a yardstick an&#39; set da one rule dat never gets broken: <strong>nobody inside while anybody&#39;s on top.</strong></p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Snow Citadel (166).</strong> Buildin&#39; runs 7:30 am to 3:00 pm in crews of 8: 60 person-hours for da rooms an&#39; roof. Da inner bailey gets made bigger around it da same day, 160 ft of circuit at 7 ft high an&#39; 36&quot; thick, for anudder 90 person-hours. At 3:40 pm Tessa names it <strong>Nine-Forty</strong>, after da first flake, an&#39; paints da name over da entrance in food colorin&#39;. Garrison: 28 of da North&#39;s 88.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 3:40 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 10, 3:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Tuckin&#39; In Da Citadel (167).</strong> Eleven crews resurface every walked-on spot inside 72 hours. From da South&#39;s line da citadel looks permanently, impossibly fresh, an&#39; everybody inside knows it&#39;s a political statement.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 3:55 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 10, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Two-Story Fort, Eh (168).</strong> At 4:00 pm Dev an&#39; Tessa start a second floor over Nine-Forty&#39;s middle room, da only one wit&#39; walls under it on all four sides: 14 by 10 ft inside, 6 ft of headroom, walls 3 ft t&#39;ick, 20 by 16 ft on da outside. Eleven kids haul 2-inch sled slats an&#39; fence boards outta da #14 garage, lay &#39;em across da walls at 6 ft, an&#39; pack 8 inches of snow over &#39;em for a floor 10 inches deep. Da drawin&#39; says leave it a night; at 12°F it gets t&#39;ree hours, t&#39;rough supper. At 7:30 pm two parents stand on da slab togedder, 360 lb between &#39;em, an&#39; da citadel rule gets changed to: nobody inside under a roof nobody&#39;s stood on yet. Da upstairs room gets finished by porch light at 9:10 pm: 14 by 10 ft an&#39; 5 ft high inside walls 2 ft t&#39;ick, under an 8-inch roof, so Nine-Forty now stands 12 ft 6 in at da middle. Four notches, 10 inches wide an&#39; 8 high, sit 3½ ft over da upstairs floor an&#39; clear da downstairs wall by a whole story. Ten packed steps 3 ft wide, each about 8 inches high an&#39; 22 deep, climb 18 ft up da back to a hatch 2 by 3 ft. Nine kids hold it, an&#39; da 6 in da room underneat&#39; get told to quit t&#39;rowin&#39; up. On Saturday a crack opens over da downstairs doorway, an&#39; da floor gets re-slatted dat night. Da South follows on <strong>Friday, February 11</strong> at 2:15 pm, wit&#39; a second floor over da middle room of its 3-room citadel in da Brandt backyard, 12 by 9 ft instead of 14 by 10 &#39;cause da room underneat&#39; is smaller, eh.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 9:10 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 11, 2:15 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Draft (169).</strong> Lila draws up a roll across four blocks: one body per two kids in every house, wit&#39; exemptions for injury, for anybody wit&#39; a workin&#39; stove, an&#39; for da engine crews. It comes to 140 names. About 70% show up.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 5:00 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 10, 7:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Big Ol&#39; Trebuchets (170).</strong> Two engines wit&#39; 400-lb counterweights of packed snow in a lawn cart, crews of 9, range 400 ft. One shot takes a foot of glazed wall off da face.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 5:30 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 11, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Standin&#39; Army (171).</strong> Ninety of da 140 report every day for 11 days straight. Four ranks, scarves for uniforms, an&#39; every order goes t&#39;rough Lila.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 9, 6:00 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 11, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> At 10:15 am da South tries to stop da citadel before it&#39;s got a roof: 31 kids come across da court behind two sleds stacked wit&#39; rounds. Da North&#39;s sling corps puts 30 rounds into a 20-ft square in 2 seconds, t&#39;ree times. Da attack stalls at da barbican&#39;s second bend, where 6 South kids stand under da drop slot for 40 seconds. Hits dat hour run 214 to 77. Da South gets to da Snow Citadel on Thursday, wit&#39; a 3-room citadel in da Brandt back yard.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-8-thursday-february-10-low-15f-high-29f\">Day 8 — Thursday, February 10 (low 15°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da treaties get drafted overnight on flattened appliance boxes, one delegation of six from each side, around da Rao kitchen table. Lila writes for da North an&#39; Marcus for da South. Dey agree on boundaries to da foot, a limit of 300 rounds a side per engagement, an&#39; a ceilin&#39; of 4 engines each, a number both sides are already over. At 11:50 pm Tessa insists on one last article, word for word off da envelope: <em>da matter of November 24 is reserved.</em> Marcus agrees right away. Everybody at da table knows why.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Officers an&#39; da Spreader Clauses (174–175).</strong> Eleven officers across both sides, none of &#39;em has t&#39;rown in 2 days. Da clauses cap machines an&#39; ban resurfacin&#39; where da other side can see. Both sides stick to da count; neither one sticks to da sight line.</p><ul><li><p>Drafted onto Da Spreaders (172): da North Thu Feb 10, 7:00 am · da South Fri Feb 11, 3:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Pillboxes (173): da North Thu Feb 10, 7:30 am · da South Fri Feb 11, 4:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Da Officers (174): da North Thu Feb 10, 8:30 am · da South Fri Feb 11, 5:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Da Spreader Clauses (175): da North Thu Feb 10, 11:00 am · da South Thu Feb 10, 11:05 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Treaties (176).</strong> Four documents are in force by noon, signed on cardboard. Two of &#39;em disagree on where da line crosses da #12 driveway.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, noon · da South got dere Thu Feb 10, 12:05 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>T’ree-Story Fort (177).</strong> Da North wants a spotter higher dan da Block Tower&#39;s gonna be. A fort built just for it goes up on da Juniper Lane corner from 6:00 am to 12:30 pm: six crews of 11, about 430 person-hours, da tier&#39;s 11 people for 5 days done in one mornin&#39;. It stands 20 by 18 ft on da outside. Da downstairs room is 12 by 10 ft an&#39; 5 ft 2 in high inside walls 4 ft t&#39;ick; da second room, inside walls 3 ft t&#39;ick, is 14 by 12 ft an&#39; 4 ft 2 in high; da floors over &#39;em are 10 inches of slat an&#39; packed snow, toppin&#39; out at 6 ft an&#39; 11 ft. Da t&#39;ird story sits set back on da second one&#39;s roof: 8 ft square an&#39; 4 ft high inside walls 2 ft t&#39;ick, 12 ft square on da outside, under an 8-inch roof at 15 ft 8 in, 3 ft 8 in taller dan da Block Tower. Its two notches, a foot wide an&#39; 10 inches high, take one spotter an&#39; one sling. T&#39;irty-one steps 2 ft wide, each 4¼ inches high an&#39; a foot deep, get cut into da outside wall an&#39; climb 31 ft along da long side an&#39; around one corner; Anjali strings a rope beside &#39;em at 11:40 after Ada slips on step 19. Da South builds its own on <strong>Saturday, February 12</strong>, done at 8:45 am, before it goes past All-Out Snow War dat day, ya know.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, 12:30 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 12, 8:45 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Shootin&#39; Blind (178).</strong> Da Block Tower gets finished at 1:00 pm, an&#39; its spotter calls corrections down to da batteries. Da first round on a target nobody can see lands at 2:20; hit rate settles near one in seven.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, 2:20 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 12, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Border Commission (179).</strong> Priya chairs 9 members, 4 an&#39; 4 an&#39; herself. It hears its first dispute at 3:30, da #12 driveway, an&#39; splits it down da middle wit&#39; a garden hose laid on da snow.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, 3:30 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 10, 3:35 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Fakin&#39; Da Weather (180).</strong> One suburban district has plowed its bus lots clean an&#39; says it&#39;s openin&#39; Friday. Dat night 6 North crews, towin&#39; machines on sleds, lay a fresh 4&quot; coat across 9 square miles of its lots an&#39; routes. Da district closes Friday. Da instrument record says nuttin&#39; fell. Every local story says 4&quot;.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, 9:30 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 11, 9:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Fortified Line (181), started.</strong> Da North&#39;s casemates, pillboxes an&#39; tunnels start gettin&#39; joined into one long work. It hits 2.5 miles by da end of February an&#39; gets walked around at da east end, t&#39;rough a neighborhood whose parents said no diggin&#39;.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 10, 4:45 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 12, 7:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da treaty&#39;s 300-round limit lasts 4 hours. At 4:10 pm a South engine crew t&#39;rows its 301st round at da Juniper Lane ravelin, da North answers wit&#39; all 9 engines, an&#39; da commission&#39;s first full session is about dat.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-9-friday-february-11-low-11f-high-31f-da-north-passes-total-snow-war\">Day 9 — Friday, February 11 (low 11°F, high 31°F): da North passes All-Out Snow War</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> At 8:30 am da shared timetable goes up on Anjali&#39;s factory whiteboard: every North neighborhood&#39;s offensive scheduled to da quarter hour, all seven of &#39;em. Kessler Court, Beacon Street, Juniper Lane, Halvorsen Place, Cherry Hill Circle, Pinecrest North an&#39; da Southwind apartments. Each one gets a runner, a copy of da timetable an&#39; a cocoa ration.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>All-Out Snow War (182).</strong> At 9:00 da North attacks on t&#39;ree fronts at once. Forty engines are goin&#39;, 21 trebuchets an&#39; 19 torsion onagers wound from bungee cord an&#39; rope; a t&#39;ird of all rounds get t&#39;rown by engine. By 6:10 pm, when da parents call everybody in, da North&#39;s t&#39;rown 12,400 rounds, 4,100 of &#39;em by engine. Hits are 3,870 to 3,654, wit&#39; 1,102 disputed.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 11, 9:00 am · da South got dere Sat Feb 12, 9:30 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> At 1:15 pm da South breaks t&#39;rough Nine-Forty&#39;s outer bailey at da southeast corner an&#39; holds da gap 2 hours. At 3:30 da North comes up t&#39;rough da Juniper-side tunnel behind &#39;em an&#39; takes it back. Lunch is a truce nobody writes down an&#39; everybody keeps: 41 thermoses at 12:15. On da North&#39;s side it&#39;s just a Friday on da way up. Da South gets to All-Out Snow War da next day, February 12, nine days after da snow quit, an&#39; dat&#39;s da day its chronicler writes da war&#39;s most famous wrong sentence.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-10-saturday-february-12-low-8f-high-26f\">Day 10 — Saturday, February 12 (low 8°F, high 26°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da engines are a mess of mismatched parts, so Dev an&#39; two shop-class eighth-graders spend Friday night drawin&#39; one engine: one frame, one arm length (8 ft), one cup (a cut-down mop bucket). Copies go out to every neighborhood by bike at 7:00 am. At da same hour Lila starts callin&#39; every parent on da roll.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Engines Off Da Same Pattern (183).</strong> Ninety engines get built to da one drawin&#39; over da next t&#39;ree weeks, against 40 of mixed design still in service. A busted engine&#39;s back on da line in 4 hours &#39;stead of 2 days.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 12, 7:00 am · da South got dere Sun Feb 13, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Everybody Called Up (184).</strong> By Sunday night 1,100 kids have reported across da North&#39;s seven neighborhoods an&#39; t&#39;ree more dat ask to join. Anjali&#39;s factory an&#39; its 9 copies switch entirely to ammunition at 4,000 rounds a day.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 12, 11:00 am · da South got dere Sun Feb 13, 11:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Spreader Divisions (185).</strong> Da machines get dere own command: four divisions, each one 900 machines on da books an&#39; about 140 for real. Spreader officers get saluted from dis day on.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 12, 2:00 pm · da South got dere Mon Feb 14, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South finally gets to All-Out Snow War: 7 neighborhoods of its own, 40 engines, an&#39; a shared timetable copied off da North&#39;s whiteboard t&#39;rough da Rao kitchen window. Its chronicler, a Pinecrest sixth-grader, writes da day up dat night. Da North spends da day holdin&#39;, not attackin&#39;, an&#39; loses 60 ft of da Juniper ravelin.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-11-sunday-february-13-low-6f-high-24f\">Day 11 — Sunday, February 13 (low 6°F, high 24°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Losin&#39; da ravelin shows da forts are too slow. Tessa, Lila an&#39; da Beacon captain spend da mornin&#39; in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room wit&#39; a map on a cereal box, plannin&#39; columns &#39;stead of walls: 11 kids each, 400 rounds, one light launcher on a sled.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Blitz (186).</strong> At 1:30 pm six columns leave da North&#39;s forts all at once. Each one covers a quarter mile in under 6 minutes. Fights dat ran 50 minutes now run 9. Da deepest column gets 600 ft past da South&#39;s line an&#39; da front moves a block.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 13, 1:30 pm · da South got dere Tue Feb 15, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Blitz Spreadin&#39; (187).</strong> Machines run ahead of da columns layin&#39; supply on ground nobody&#39;s taken yet. Over da next week da fronts move 11 miles in all, mostly into parks an&#39; school fields. More dan half da forward machines get lost an&#39; dey keep da doctrine anyway.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sun Feb 13, 1:35 pm · da South got dere Tue Feb 15, 10:05 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South&#39;s line breaks at 2:20 at da church lot on Beacon Street an&#39; gets pushed back to da crick culvert by dark. Hits dat afternoon run 2,140 to 610. Da South copies da columns on Tuesday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-12-monday-february-14-low-10f-high-25f\">Day 12 — Monday, February 14 (low 10°F, high 25°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Four suburban districts try a half-day openin&#39; for Valentine&#39;s Day; Tulsa stays closed. Da North writes a sleepover permission slip, 1 page, wit&#39; t&#39;ree conditions: sleepin&#39; bags rated to −20°F, a parent within 30 ft all night, an&#39; everybody inside by 10:00 pm. Fifty-two families sign it.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Raidin&#39; Way Behind Da Line (188).</strong> Parties of four wit&#39; 60 rounds each go out up to 2 miles behind da South&#39;s line. Of 9 targets in da next month, 4 get wrecked: two depots, a quarry an&#39; an engine park.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 14, 9:00 am · da South got dere Tue Feb 15, 1:00 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Batteries Dat Hit Right On Da Nose an&#39; da Deep Bunker (189–190).</strong> A battery clerk works out range, arm an&#39; counterweight on paper before da first shot. Four engines put rounds inside a 9-ft circle at 300 ft. Command moves into a bunker under da Oduya back yard, 20 ft of snow an&#39; tunnel over it; nobody on its staff can draw a plan of it from memory.</p><ul><li><p>Batteries Dat Hit Right On Da Nose (189): da North Mon Feb 14, 10:30 am · da South Tue Feb 15, 3:00 pm</p></li><li><p>Da Deep Bunker (190): da North Mon Feb 14, 2:00 pm · da South Tue Feb 15, 4:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Fortress Cities (191).</strong> Da citadel gets made big enough to house its garrison overnight: 41 rooms on a footprint of 120 by 90 ft, 8 ft of headroom behind 4-ft walls, 900 ft of mapped tunnel. Fifty-two kids sleep in it dat night wit&#39; 6 parents in da Oduya garage next door. Inside it reads 28°F all night against −2°F outside.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 14, 7:30 pm · da South got dere Wed Feb 16, 7:30 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Attendance in da four suburban districts drops under half, dey close again at noon, an&#39; nobody writes it down. Valentines get swapped across da line at da treaty gate at 4:00 pm, 340 of &#39;em, 22 wit&#39; a round inside.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-13-tuesday-february-15-low-12f-high-28f\">Day 13 — Tuesday, February 15 (low 12°F, high 28°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da North&#39;s got more kids now dan it can train on da job. Lila opens t&#39;ree schools in da Oduya basement an&#39; da Rao livin&#39; room, each wit&#39; a sign-up sheet an&#39; a whistle.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Spreaders Big as a Town (192).</strong> Two machines get scaled up to 4-m troughs on wagon wheels an&#39; towed by a lawn tractor. Each covers 11 blocks a shift.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Tue Feb 15, 9:00 am · da South got dere Wed Feb 16, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Staff College, da War Academy, Spreader Academy (193–195).</strong> Da staff college takes 22 candidates an&#39; teaches supply, frontage an&#39; da war&#39;s 9 campaigns so far. Da war academy teaches packin&#39; (four squeezes, t&#39;ree seconds) to 40 students a class, 80% graduatin&#39;. Da spreader academy&#39;s entrance exam is one unbroken 100-ft run judged by eye, an&#39; its first lesson is da graph-paper sketch, one wheel bigger dan da other.</p><ul><li><p>Da Staff College (193): da North Tue Feb 15, 10:00 am · da South Wed Feb 16, 10:00 am</p></li><li><p>Da War Academy (194): da North Tue Feb 15, 11:00 am · da South Wed Feb 16, 11:00 am</p></li><li><p>Da Spreader Academy (195): da North Tue Feb 15, 1:00 pm · da South Wed Feb 16, 1:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South&#39;s first blitz columns go out at 10:00 am. Two of six get lost in Pinecrest an&#39; one gets captured whole by a North deep-raidin&#39; party, sled launcher an&#39; all. Da South gets to da Fortress Cities on Wednesday, an&#39; its sleepover permission slip is copied off da North&#39;s word for word.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-14-wednesday-february-16-low-14f-high-29f\">Day 14 — Wednesday, February 16 (low 14°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da North&#39;s too big now to run out of one garage. Tessa cuts a city map into 11 pieces wit&#39; a ruler on da Oduya dinin&#39; table, one per region, each wit&#39; at least one quarry site, one depot an&#39; one engine park. Da 22 staff-college candidates get paired up an&#39; sent out by bike to run &#39;em.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Provinces (196).</strong> Eleven provinces wit&#39; a roll of about 200 kids each. An order from Nine-Forty gets to da farthest province in a day an&#39; a half, by bike an&#39; walkie-talkie relay. T&#39;ree provinces make more dan dey t&#39;row an&#39; get taxed a fift&#39; of output, about 900 rounds a week each.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 16, 9:30 am · da South got dere Thu Feb 17, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South hits da Halvorsen province at dawn wit&#39; 4 blitz columns to test da new borders. Da Battle of da Culvert runs 7:10–10:40 am at da crick crossin&#39;. Da North holds da culvert mouth an&#39; loses da playground behind it. Hits are 1,880 to 1,640, da closest day of da war so far. Da South splits into provinces on Thursday, nine of &#39;em.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-15-thursday-february-17-low-15f-high-30f\">Day 15 — Thursday, February 17 (low 15°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Tessa an&#39; da staff college spend Wednesday night writin&#39; one decree for every work in da North. Da numbers get set above da biggest fort anybody&#39;s got: main wall 550 ft long, 14 ft high an&#39; 8 ft thick, notches 14&quot; at da t&#39;roat, six to a section; trench 6 ft deep; tunnel 14 ft down; keep 200 by 150 ft. Surveyors go out at 8:00 am wit&#39; a broom handle painted in 1-ft bands an&#39; a composition notebook.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Spreader Works, Coast to Coast (197).</strong> Forty garages an&#39; two school libraries print machines in da cold-tough plastic Priya picked. Da standin&#39; park passes 1,100 machines.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 17, 8:00 am · da South got dere Fri Feb 18, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Da Imperial Gauge (198).</strong> By 5:00 pm da surveyors report dat not one work in da North meets da gauge, which is da whole point.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 17, 5:00 pm · da South got dere Fri Feb 18, 5:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> A South party of 12 ambushes da survey team at da Cherry Hill ravelin at 11:30 an&#39; grabs da broom handle. Da North makes a second one by 1:00 pm. Dat night da two sides sign da <strong>Recut Truce</strong>: no t&#39;rowin&#39; while da walls are bein&#39; rebuilt, t&#39;rough Monday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-16-friday-february-18-low-13f-high-30f\">Day 16 — Friday, February 18 (low 13°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Every one of da North&#39;s 22 settlements gets a work order: one 550-ft wall, 14 ft high an&#39; 8 ft thick, which takes about 1.3 acres of stripped yard. Every family whose yard gets stripped signs a permission line in da province notebook. Da 12 dat don&#39;t get mapped an&#39; routed around.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Great Recut (199), started.</strong> Sixty t&#39;ousand hands start at 8:00 am: walls raised an&#39; t&#39;ickened, every keep made bigger, trenches dug wider an&#39; deeper. Rounds packed in one province now fit da notches in every other one.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 18, 8:00 am · da South got dere Sat Feb 19, 8:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> None. Da truce holds all day, da longest da war&#39;s gone wit&#39;out a t&#39;row since da snow fell.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-17-saturday-february-19-low-12f-high-29f\">Day 17 — Saturday, February 19 (low 12°F, high 29°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da capital&#39;s wall gets laid out wit&#39; string an&#39; stakes around da whole of Kessler Court an&#39; halfway down da street: 1,650 ft, 21 ft high an&#39; 12 ft thick, da gauge an&#39; half again. Its crews come from all 11 provinces in rotation, 600 at a time.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Snow Empire (200).</strong> By dusk da North&#39;s works run 2.5 miles across its settlements: 21 walls of 550 ft an&#39; da capital&#39;s 1,650. Twenty-two settlements are signed up. Two are behind on dere tribute of 2,000 rounds a week an&#39; keep dere garrison support anyway. Four more are still negotiatin&#39;.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Sat Feb 19, 6:00 pm · da South got dere Sun Feb 20, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da truce holds, barely. A South engine crew &quot;tests&quot; its trebuchet into an empty field at 3:00 pm, 6 shots, an&#39; da Border Commission rules it legal 5 votes to 4.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-18-sunday-february-20-low-14f-high-31f\">Day 18 — Sunday, February 20 (low 14°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da South, a day behind on its own recut, declares its own empire anyway, wit&#39; Kessler Court as its capital too: da Brandt side, 60 ft across da street from da North&#39;s.</p>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da Recut Truce breaks at 2:00 pm, a day early, when a North spreader crew lays fresh cover where da South&#39;s capital wall can see it. Da South says it breaks da spreader clauses; da North says it&#39;s maintenance. In da 3 hours after dat, da two capitals trade 6,200 rounds across a street 40 ft wide.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-19-monday-february-21-presidents-day\">Day 19 — Monday, February 21: Presidents&#39; Day</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Saturday&#39;s survey teams walked a line across da city from da river on da west to da crick on da east: 19 miles. One milecastle per mile wit&#39; a garrison of 32, two turrets between each pair, 9 gates. Da line&#39;s drawn to run right down da middle of Kessler Court, between #14 an&#39; #9, &#39;cause dat&#39;s where da war is.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Frontier Wall (201).</strong> Forty t&#39;ousand hands build it to da gauge, 14 ft high an&#39; 8 ft thick. Da North builds its half Monday. Da South builds da other face Tuesday, an&#39; da two halves meet at da mailboxes, where a gate gets cut 6 ft wide for da mail carrier.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 21, 5:30 pm · da South got dere Tue Feb 22, 4:00 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Trade crosses at da 9 gates an&#39; nowhere else, which is true about half da time. Da first fight at da Kessler gate is at 4:15 pm, over whose side da gate&#39;s latch is on.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-20-tuesday-february-22-low-16f-high-31f\">Day 20 — Tuesday, February 22 (low 16°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da city wants sidewalks, bus stops an&#39; driveways cleared before schools open back up, an&#39; da war&#39;s machines are da only snow movers on most of da streets. Somebody&#39;s gotta decide which ground gets resurfaced an&#39; which gets cleared. Dev drafts a schedule Monday night, an&#39; Priya, who still don&#39;t care, agrees to run da office if it&#39;s got a printer.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Spreader Ministry (202).</strong> Redistribution turns into a department wit&#39; 4,000 staff: every licensed operator, dere crews an&#39; dere record-keepers. It answers to nobody who&#39;s pushed a machine since February 3. Its first published act puts fortifications below sidewalks, bus stops an&#39; driveways on da resurfacin&#39; list. Dat causes a constitutional crisis at Nine-Forty, an&#39; it stands, &#39;cause every parent in da empire agrees wit&#39; it.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Tue Feb 22, 8:00 am · da South got dere Wed Feb 23, 9:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da day&#39;s fight runs 9:00 am–4:30 pm along da Frontier Wall. Both sides lose more ground to da ministry&#39;s list dan to each other: 3 forts on driveways get taken down by dere own crews.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-21-wednesday-february-23-low-18f-high-30f\">Day 21 — Wednesday, February 23 (low 18°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Decisions quit gettin&#39; made on da wall an&#39; start gettin&#39; made in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room, by flashlight, in da evenin&#39;s. Nine advisors sit around Tessa dere; not one of &#39;em holds a spot on da line.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Politics at Court (203).</strong> Four of da last six senior appointments go to kids who never built a wall, includin&#39; two who only ever ran da cocoa line. Two province commands change hands in a week for reasons dat never get written in da notebook.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Wed Feb 23, 7:30 pm · da South got dere Thu Feb 24, 7:30 pm</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South raids da Pinecrest North province while its commander&#39;s at Nine-Forty for a meetin&#39; about da meetin&#39; schedule. Dey take 3 engines an&#39; da province&#39;s cocoa urn. Da North gets da engines back Thursday an&#39; never da urn.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-22-thursday-february-24-low-17f-high-30f\">Day 22 — Thursday, February 24 (low 17°F, high 30°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da court&#39;s outgrown da citadel. Since Tuesday, 900 kids in shifts have been buildin&#39; on da elementary school field. Lila spends Thursday night at da Oduya dinin&#39; table drawin&#39; up da <strong>lines of succession</strong> for da North&#39;s 11 foundin&#39; families, by birth order, youngest last.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Da Palace-Fortress (204).</strong> Da palace is built mostly to keep da North&#39;s own court in line: 7 wards, one for each rank, each walled off from da next an&#39; entered only t&#39;rough da ward below it. Da throne hall sits in da middle under a dome 60 ft across. Nine hundred people live an&#39; work inside by day, an&#39; every one knows how far in dey&#39;re allowed. Da outer wall&#39;s da weakest in da empire, an&#39; nobody ever tests it.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Thu Feb 24, 2:00 pm · da South got dere Sat Feb 26, 8:00 am</p></li></ul></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> At 4:30 pm da South&#39;s whole engine park, 61 engines, fires on da palace&#39;s outer wall for an hour as a test. Nuttin&#39; hits it. Dey were aimed at da citadel on Kessler Court, which is still da North&#39;s capital on every map da South&#39;s got. Da South starts its own palace Friday.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-23-friday-february-25-low-18f-high-30f-da-court-crowns-itself\">Day 23 — Friday, February 25 (low 18°F, high 30°F): da court crowns itself</h4>\n<p>Packable depth&#39;s 74.0&quot;. Da North&#39;s got about 17,400 kids on its rolls an&#39; 214 engines; da South about 12,600 an&#39; 161. Schools are closed for da 18th school day in a row.</p>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Lila&#39;s lines of succession, finished at 11:40 pm Thursday, run 3 pages: 11 foundin&#39; families, every kid listed by birth order, youngest last. Her own name&#39;s on da Brandt line, on da South&#39;s side of da street, an&#39; it&#39;s crossed out in her own hand. At 6:45 am da palace&#39;s throne hall gets swept, an&#39; a dinin&#39; chair from #14 gets carried in for da throne.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>7:15 am, da Hereditary Aristocracy (205).</strong> Command of da North passes by birth order to da youngest kid of each of da <strong>11 foundin&#39; lines</strong>. Tessa, 11, hands da North to her sister <strong>Ada, 6</strong>, in front of 140 kids in da throne hall. Da older ones get skipped altogedder. Ada&#39;s first two orders, told to Tessa to write down: cocoa for everybody, an&#39; nobody t&#39;rows at da dog.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 25, 7:15 am · da South got dere Sat Feb 26, 9:15 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>7:31 am.</strong> Da school bus don&#39;t come, da 18th school day runnin&#39;. Da aristocracy goes to war.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:00 am, da Client Kingdoms (206).</strong> Nine of da 12 yards dat wouldn&#39;t do da Great Recut sign on as client kingdoms of da North. Dey sit right inside da North&#39;s own territory, too little to conquer an&#39; too proud to join up, an&#39; da empire&#39;s been flowin&#39; around &#39;em for a week: da Nelson side yard behind da blown-down fence (3 kids), da gap between da two pines at #3 (2), da Halvorsen culvert mouth (4), da corner of da Cherry Hill mailbox bank (2), a willow on Pinecrest North (5), two duplex porches at Southwind (3 an&#39; 4), da back corner of da church lot (6), an&#39; a sandbox on Juniper Lane (2). Each one&#39;s got one fort wit&#39; a wall ya can see over, an&#39; nuttin&#39; bigger dan a garage bat. Each keeps its own flag, its own calendar an&#39; its own rules, an&#39; hands every dealin&#39; wit&#39; da South to Nine-Forty. In return each one gets protection, 4,000 rounds a season an&#39; 6 pattern engines it can&#39;t export, an&#39; each owes da North 300 bodies whenever asked. Da nine togedder got 31 kids an&#39; nobody who can crew an engine. Ada signs all nine treaties in purple marker by 11:40. Da udder 3 yards stay outta everyt&#39;ing.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Feb 25, 11:00 am · da South got dere Sat Feb 26, 1:30 pm</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>3:40 pm.</strong> Ada holds court for 20 minutes in da throne hall an&#39; confirms all 9 advisors, 4 of &#39;em she&#39;s never met.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da South, still run on merit, makes its biggest attack of da war so far while da North&#39;s busy wit&#39; a coronation. At 3:50 pm, 61 engines open up on da Frontier Wall&#39;s Kessler gate an&#39; 9 blitz columns go for milecastles 6, 7 an&#39; 8. Column 4, led by Owen himself, breaks t&#39;rough at milecastle 7 at 4:35 an&#39; holds da gap 40 minutes. Ada&#39;s only battlefield order of da day, passed along by Tessa from da palace, is &quot;make &#39;em go back.&quot; Da North&#39;s sling corps an&#39; 30 engines from da Halvorsen province close da gap at 5:15. At 6:05 da parents call everybody in. Rounds t&#39;rown dat afternoon: about 48,000. Hits: 11,240 to 10,980, wit&#39; 3,400 disputed. <strong>Tiers reached: Da Hereditary Aristocracy (#205) an&#39; da Client Kingdoms (#206).</strong></p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-24-saturday-february-26-low-15f-high-31f\">Day 24 — Saturday, February 26 (low 15°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Lila&#39;s copy of da succession list goes across da street overnight; Marcus finds it under da Brandt door at 7:00 am an&#39; uses it as da pattern for da South&#39;s 9 lines. Down in da Rao basement, Priya spends da mornin&#39; on a problem nobody else noticed: da Continental Spreader Works&#39; 40 garages an&#39; 2 school libraries are all printin&#39; off her files, wit&#39; 40 different tolerances, an&#39; spare parts from one garage don&#39;t fit machines from anudder no more.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:15 am.</strong> Da South gets to da Hereditary Aristocracy. Owen hands command to his brother <strong>Sam, 8</strong>; Marcus, bein&#39; an older one, gets skipped.</p></li><li><p><strong>1:30 pm.</strong> Da South gets to da Client Kingdoms. Sam signs nine yards along Beacon Street an&#39; da crick in pencil, on da back of Lila&#39;s succession list. Two of &#39;em signed wit&#39; da North da day before, an&#39; from den on dey fly bot&#39; flags.</p></li><li><p><strong>2:15 pm.</strong> T&#39;irty-one hours after takin&#39; da throne, Ada hands da North back to <strong>Tessa</strong> an&#39; goes to a birthday party. Ada&#39;s da youngest Oduya, so dere&#39;s nobody further down da line to pass it to, an&#39; command goes back up to da founder who gave it away. Dat&#39;s how da succession works from now on: da youngest rules about 31 hours, den da founder takes it back.</p></li><li><p><strong>6:30 pm.</strong> Priya sends one text to da owners of da 4 biggest print shops in da empire: &quot;Basement. 10 am tomorrow. Bring your moulds.&quot;</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Sam&#39;s first order is da siege of da Juniper ravelin, run from 8:30 am to 4:30 pm by da South&#39;s province captains, who are older ones an&#39; so actually in charge. Da ravelin falls at 4:00 an&#39; da North takes it back by 5:10 wit&#39; 12 engines an&#39; da sling corps. Hits: 6,310 to 6,220.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-25-sunday-february-27-low-9f-high-22f-gusts-to-38-mph-da-second-front\">Day 25 — Sunday, February 27 (low 9°F, high 22°F, gusts to 38 mph): da second front</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> A second arctic front comes t&#39;rough at 3:10 am behind a line of snow showers dat leave nuttin&#39; ya could measure. Da temperature drops 14 degrees in two hours, an&#39; every forecast dat showed a thaw dis week now shows highs in da low 20s t&#39;rough Thursday. Da glazin&#39; crews, who were plannin&#39; to quit, get told to keep pourin&#39;.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>10:00 am, da Rao basement.</strong> Four print shops meet around Priya&#39;s printer: <strong>House Rao</strong> (Kessler Court, da original files, 1 printer), <strong>House Whitaker</strong> (a two-car garage on Cherry Hill, 9 printers), <strong>House Delgado</strong> (two Southwind duplex basements, 6 printers) an&#39; <strong>House Kim</strong> (da Beacon Street church basement, 11 printers). Between &#39;em dey hold every screw mould an&#39; trough mould in da North. Da meetin&#39; runs 2 hours an&#39; 40 minutes, wit&#39; cocoa at 11:00 an&#39; 12:00, an&#39; ends wit&#39; one price list an&#39; one pattern book, bot&#39; to come out Monday.</p></li><li><p><strong>4:15 pm.</strong> Sam&#39;s 31 hours are up. Sam&#39;s da youngest Brandt, wit&#39; nobody below him in da line, so command of da South goes back to <strong>Owen</strong> right in da middle of da afternoon&#39;s fightin&#39;.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da Gust Battle at da Kessler gate runs 12:30–4:30 pm. At 38 mph a round t&#39;rown by hand drifts 15 ft over 60 ft of flight, an&#39; slings are no good, so da day belongs to da engines: 26 on da North, 19 on da South, all firin&#39; downwind or not at all. Da North holds da upwind side of da court &#39;cause da Frontier Wall runs nort&#39;–sout&#39;, an&#39; da South&#39;s rounds land short all afternoon. 4,100 rounds, 1,020 hits, eh.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-26-monday-february-28-low-2f-high-17f\">Day 26 — Monday, February 28 (low −2°F, high 17°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da coldest mornin&#39; since February 4. Schools are closed for da 20th school day. Da four houses&#39; price list gets printed on House Rao&#39;s printer overnight, 400 copies, an&#39; taped up at all 9 Frontier Wall gates an&#39; every depot by 8:00 am.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>11:00 am, da Spreader Cartels (207).</strong> Makin&#39; every machine in da North now goes t&#39;rough <strong>four houses holdin&#39; da moulds between &#39;em</strong>: Rao, Whitaker, Delgado an&#39; Kim. Dey set da price, da pattern an&#39; da delivery day for every machine in da alliance. A screw costs <strong>44 marshmallows</strong>, eleven times da 4 it cost at da Spreader Works, Coast to Coast in February. Deliveries go out Thursdays only. Da pattern book fixes one screw, one trough, one wing an&#39; one clutch spring, an&#39; nuttin&#39; outside it can be ordered. Da new screws fail at exactly da old rate: about 1 in 9 cracks its first week.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Mon Feb 28, 11:00 am · da South got dere Tue Feb 29, 10:00 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>2:00 pm.</strong> Two of da North&#39;s client kingdoms, da sandbox on Juniper Lane an&#39; da Nelson side yard, try mouldin&#39; dere own screws instead: one in a Jell-O mould outta a kitchen drawer, da udder by packin&#39; modelin&#39; clay around a cartel screw. Da cartels cut &#39;em bot&#39; off from spares at 2:40. Da sandbox&#39;s only machine is down by dark wit&#39; a cracked clutch spring it can&#39;t replace no more.</p></li><li><p><strong>7:30 pm.</strong> Owen, back in charge for 27 hours, finds out every South machine got printed off da same files an&#39; needs da same parts. Da South&#39;s 3 machine shops an&#39; da Brandt garage spend da evenin&#39; countin&#39; what dey got left.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> At 3:10 pm a South deep-raidin&#39; party of 4 gets into House Kim&#39;s church basement t&#39;rough da coat room an&#39; comes out wit&#39; 2 trough moulds an&#39; a bag of clutch springs. Da moulds turn out to be from a rejected batch wit&#39; da wrong pitch, which is why dey were in da coat room. Along da Frontier Wall da cold keeps da day short: 1:00 to 4:00 pm, wit&#39; 20-minute indoor breaks every 30 minutes under a new parents&#39; rule. 2,800 rounds, 640 hits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-27-tuesday-february-29-low-3f-high-21f\">Day 27 — Tuesday, February 29 (low 3°F, high 21°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da South&#39;s Pinecrest commander, an older brother wit&#39; no line of his own, spends Monday night writin&#39; a two-page claim on notebook paper: command can only pass down a line, never back up it, so Owen comin&#39; back on Sunday don&#39;t count.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>10:00 am.</strong> Da South gets to da Spreader Cartels. Its 3 machine shops an&#39; da Brandt garage turn into 4 houses of dere own an&#39; put out a price list, 52 marshmallows a screw, on da back of da North&#39;s.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da first contested claim, four days in.</strong> Da Border Commission hears da Pinecrest claim at lunch an&#39; rules 5–4 dat command can go back to da founder. Da Pinecrest commander appeals &#39;cause da rulin&#39; was made on February 29, a day dat don&#39;t exist t&#39;ree years outta four. Da appeal&#39;s still pendin&#39; in May.</p></li><li><p><strong>3:30 pm.</strong> Da sandbox an&#39; da Nelson side yard give in to da cartels, t&#39;ree days after tryin&#39; to leave &#39;em. Dey hand over da Jell-O mould an&#39; da clay, an&#39; get dere spares back at da old price plus 6 marshmallows for da trouble.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Dat afternoon da South splits over da rulin&#39;. Two Pinecrest blitz columns attack da South&#39;s own Kessler capital wall for 40 minutes before anybody stops &#39;em. Da North watches from da Frontier Wall an&#39; don&#39;t step in, which da treaty don&#39;t technically require. 3,900 rounds get t&#39;rown on da court dat day, 1,700 of &#39;em by South kids at udder South kids.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-28-wednesday-march-1-low-8f-high-26f-contact\">Day 28 — Wednesday, March 1 (low 8°F, high 26°F): contact</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> By March 1 every heir in all 11 North lines an&#39; 9 South lines has had dere 31 hours an&#39;, bein&#39; da youngest in dere line wit&#39; nobody below &#39;em, handed command back up. Da founders run da war again. Da youngest of each line keeps da title, a chair in da throne hall, an&#39; da right to give one order a week, which is usually about cocoa.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:20 am.</strong> Da Frontier Wall&#39;s east end, milecastle 19, gets to da crick. On da far bank, behind a wall of dere own 9 ft high, are 40 kids from Broken Arrow who&#39;ve been fightin&#39; dere own Great Storm war since February 3. Dey call demselves da <strong>Lynn Lane Confederacy</strong>, dey got to da Fortress Cities on dere own, an&#39; dey never heard of Kessler Court.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:00 am.</strong> Lila an&#39; Priya cross da footbridge under a white pillowcase wit&#39; a copy of da four treaties. Da Confederacy reads &#39;em at a picnic table an&#39; asks one question: what&#39;s da matter of November 24? Nobody answers.</p></li><li><p><strong>By 8:00 pm</strong> da North&#39;s made contact wit&#39; 4 more wars on its edges: Sand Springs across da river bridge, Owasso at da turnpike overpass, Jenks–Bixby at da levee, an&#39; Bartlesville, t&#39;rough a cousin. Each one&#39;s got its own storm war, its own forts an&#39; its own count of tiers, all lower dan Kessler&#39;s.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da first fight between two cities&#39; wars is at da crick footbridge at 2:15 pm, over whose side da bridge belongs to. Da Confederacy holds it till 3:40, when 9 North engines get walked down Milecastle Road an&#39; set up on da bank. Rounds: 3,300. Hits: 880 to 610. Da footbridge gets declared neutral at 4:30 by a Border Commission sittin&#39; right on da bridge, wit&#39; 2 Confederacy members added to make 11.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-day-29-thursday-march-2-low-12f-high-28f\">Day 29 — Thursday, March 2 (low 12°F, high 28°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Contact spreads faster now dan anybody can walk. It runs t&#39;rough t&#39;ree channels at once: da cartels&#39; Thursday deliveries, which go out in parents&#39; trucks to relatives in udder towns; a group chat Dev starts at 7:00 am called <strong>Turnpike Accord</strong>, which has 212 members by noon; an&#39; cousins.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:00 am.</strong> A cartel delivery of 12 machines gets to a cousin in Edmond, 105 miles off, whose own storm war&#39;s been stuck at Trench Warfare wit&#39; nuttin&#39; to resurface wit&#39;. By noon Edmond&#39;s allied wit&#39; da North.</p></li><li><p><strong>11:30 am.</strong> Muskogee&#39;s war joins da Turnpike Accord; Stillwater&#39;s joins at 1:10. Oklahoma City&#39;s an&#39; Norman&#39;s wars sign wit&#39; da South t&#39;rough Marcus&#39;s soccer teammates.</p></li><li><p><strong>3:00 pm.</strong> Da chat gets to Wichita, 175 miles nort&#39;west, an&#39; Joplin, 110 miles nort&#39;east. Wichita&#39;s war, which has its own frontier wall along a drainage canal, declares on Edmond by mistake an&#39; den on Oklahoma City on purpose.</p></li><li><p><strong>9:40 pm.</strong> Priya counts <strong>14 belligerents</strong> in da chat an&#39; on da map taped to da Rao basement wall, an&#39; draws da front in red marker from Wichita to Muskogee: 220 miles.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Most of it&#39;s still local: da crick footbridge (neutral, fought over anyways at 2:00), da river bridge to Sand Springs, an&#39; da Frontier Wall, where da North an&#39; South trade about 6,000 rounds between 10:00 am an&#39; 4:00 pm. Da war&#39;s first long-range action is a cartel embargo: House Whitaker won&#39;t ship spares to any war allied wit&#39; da South, an&#39; Norman&#39;s machines start failin&#39; by Friday.</p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-day-30-friday-march-3-da-peak\">Day 30 — Friday, March 3: da peak</h3>\n<p>Packable depth&#39;s 74.0&quot; an&#39; da pack on da ground is still 24&quot; settled. Da North&#39;s got about 19,800 kids on its rolls an&#39; 240 engines; da South about 14,100 an&#39; 175. Schools are closed for da 23rd school day. It&#39;s 10°F at dawn an&#39; 29°F by afternoon, da 30th day in a row below freezin&#39;.</p>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Priya&#39;s up at 5:30 tapin&#39; 9 printed sheets of road map togedder on da Rao basement wall: Kansas, Oklahoma, Missouri an&#39; Arkansas, wit&#39; every belligerent in da Turnpike Accord marked in red. At 7:00 am Ada uses her one order of da week from da throne hall: cocoa at every milecastle at 10:00. Tessa an&#39; Lila spend da mornin&#39; at da Oduya dinin&#39; table writin&#39; letters of alliance to wars dey&#39;ve never seen, copied out by hand 6 times each, Rule 4 at da bottom of every one.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>8:15 am.</strong> Fort Smith&#39;s war, allied to da South t&#39;rough Owen&#39;s cousin, attacks Fayetteville&#39;s across da Arkansas line over a plow pile at a church kids from bot&#39; towns use.</p></li><li><p><strong>9:40 am.</strong> Fayetteville asks da Turnpike Accord for help. Da North answers in 11 minutes. Springfield, Missouri, joins up wit&#39; Fayetteville at 10:05.</p></li><li><p><strong>10:30 am, War Across Da Whole Continent (208).</strong> Little Rock&#39;s war, da <strong>Arkansas River League</strong>, comes in on Fayetteville&#39;s side an&#39; da North&#39;s at 10:30, an&#39; da chain closes: one war from Wichita, Kansas to Little Rock, Arkansas. Priya runs a string along da red line t&#39;rough every belligerent an&#39; measures it against da map&#39;s scale bar: <strong>400 miles</strong>. Holy wah.</p><ul><li><p>Da North got dere Fri Mar 3, 10:30 am · da South got dere Sat Mar 4, 9:40 am</p></li></ul></li><li><p><strong>Noon.</strong> Priya&#39;s count of belligerents hits <strong>19</strong>: Kessler North; da Kessler South coalition; da Pinecrest columns, counted as dere own belligerent since Tuesday an&#39; still counted wit&#39; da South; da Lynn Lane Confederacy; Sand Springs; Owasso; Jenks–Bixby; Bartlesville; Edmond; Muskogee; Stillwater; Oklahoma City; Norman; Wichita; Joplin; Fort Smith; Fayetteville; Springfield; an&#39; Little Rock. Nobody can name all 19 from memory, Priya neider, an&#39; da count&#39;s still goin&#39; up.</p></li><li><p><strong>4:00 pm.</strong> Every belligerent&#39;s chronicler calls or texts in a head count for da day. Da total, countin&#39; only kids who t&#39;rew at least once, is <strong>about 400,000</strong>. Kessler&#39;s own rolls only make up 33,900 of &#39;em. Oklahoma City by itself reports 96,000, a number no udder belligerent believes an&#39; nobody in Oklahoma City can explain.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da war gets fought on four fronts at once. On da Frontier Wall, da North an&#39; South trade about 52,000 rounds between 9:00 am an&#39; 6:00 pm, da most of any day of da war; da South&#39;s engines open up on da Kessler gate at 9:00 an&#39; its blitz columns go for milecastles 3 an&#39; 4 at 1:15, an&#39; get held at bot&#39;. At da crick footbridge da Confederacy, allied wit&#39; da North now, holds da bridge against Sand Springs, which joined da South, for 4 hours. At da Arkansas line, Fort Smith takes da church plow pile at 11:20 an&#39; loses it at 2:50. An&#39; by cartel embargo: House Whitaker won&#39;t ship to da South&#39;s allies, which leaves Norman wit&#39; 3 workin&#39; machines outta 19. Hits on da Frontier Wall: 13,050 to 12,480, wit&#39; 4,100 disputed. <strong>Peak tier: War Across Da Whole Continent (#208).</strong></p>\n<h3 id=\"ks-yp-da-slow-melt-an-what-came-after\">Da slow melt an&#39; what came after</h3>\n<p>No more snow falls. Da Great Storm&#39;s da last event of da winter, an&#39; everyting after March 3 gets fought on snow dat&#39;s already on da ground an&#39; slowly leavin&#39;: 24&quot; settled on March 3, 9&quot; in open yards by March 17, bare yards on March 24, an&#39; da last plow pile gone on April 14. Da war comes down tier by tier as da pack shrinks, startin&#39; at its edges: da udder cities&#39; wars, on t&#39;inner snow, melt out first.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-saturday-march-4-low-14f-high-31f\">Saturday, March 4 (low 14°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Fort Smith signs a real alliance wit&#39; da South at 8:00 am, by text, copied onto cardboard by Marcus. Two of da North&#39;s clients try to renegotiate while da North&#39;s busy wit&#39; t&#39;ree udder states: da Halvorsen culvert an&#39; da church-lot corner.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>9:40 am.</strong> Da South gets to War Across Da Whole Continent, a day behind da North, da minute Fort Smith&#39;s signature gets taped to da Brandt garage door.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da renegotiations.</strong> Da culvert gets it done. Its 6 engines, sittin&#39; in a row wit&#39; nobody to crew &#39;em since February 25, get traded for cocoa delivered every afternoon at 3:00. Da church lot asks for a sevent&#39; engine an&#39; gets told no.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da North&#39;s deep raiders take da South&#39;s main depot at da Brandt garage, 1,100 rounds, while da South&#39;s attackin&#39; da Halvorsen province. Along da Wall, about 21,000 rounds. In Wichita, da canal wall gets overrun by Edmond&#39;s ally from da nort&#39; side of town, an&#39; Wichita leaves da war at 5:00 pm, da first belligerent to go.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-sunday-march-5-low-19f-high-31f\">Sunday, March 5 (low 19°F, high 31°F)</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da forecast finally shows a high above freezin&#39; for Monday, an&#39; 45°F rain for Tuesday an&#39; Wednesday. Da glazin&#39; crews pour a last coat on every south-facin&#39; wall Saturday night, 40 gallons a fort. Da imperial walls stand at da full gauge, 14 ft, on every face, an&#39; da Frontier Wall runs unbroken all 19 miles.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>Noon.</strong> Priya&#39;s map shows 17 belligerents: Wichita an&#39; Joplin are out. Two new ones, Claremore an&#39; Sapulpa, joined up, bot&#39; on da North&#39;s side, so da count&#39;s still goin&#39; up in da middle an&#39; down at da ends.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> All day, mostly raids on depots an&#39; quarries, which are gonna melt faster dan da walls &#39;cause dey&#39;re piles. Da Beacon church plow pile, 9 ft now, changes hands twice. About 14,000 rounds.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-monday-march-6-low-24f-high-38f-da-first-thaw\">Monday, March 6 (low 24°F, high 38°F): da first thaw</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> For da first time in 32 days a day hits 32°F, at 11:40 am. At 6:00 am da district says schools stay closed dis week &#39;cause of da rain comin&#39; Tuesday, an&#39; wit&#39; spring break da week after, classes start back up Monday, March 20.</p>\n<ul><li><p>Da walls sweat by noon but stand. Rounds packed off da sweatin&#39; faces come out half slush an&#39; twice as heavy. Both sides call it cheatin&#39;, an&#39; both do it.</p></li><li><p>Oklahoma City&#39;s an&#39; Norman&#39;s wars, on 14&quot; of pack, are gone by dark. Priya takes &#39;em off da map wit&#39; a pencil eraser.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da Slush Battle at da Kessler gate runs 1:00–4:30 pm. Slush rounds don&#39;t shatter on glaze, dey stick, an&#39; da South uses &#39;em to plug up 4 of da North&#39;s firin&#39; notches. Da North answers by movin&#39; its slingers into da casemates. 3,400 rounds, 960 hits.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-tuesday-march-7-friday-march-10-da-rain\">Tuesday, March 7 – Friday, March 10: da rain</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Da Spreader Ministry calls an emergency meetin&#39; in Nine-Forty&#39;s back room Monday night: da machines can&#39;t lay snow dat ain&#39;t dere, an&#39; rain&#39;s gonna strip da glaze in a day.</p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>March 7–8.</strong> 0.9&quot; of rain over two days. Da glaze goes first, den da outer foot of every wall. Da palace dome on da school field slumps 8 ft at da crown an&#39; gets closed at 4:00 pm Wednesday wit&#39; nobody inside, followin&#39; da citadel rule. Every street da plows narrowed floods curb to curb.</p></li><li><p><strong>March 9.</strong> Little Rock, Fayetteville, Fort Smith an&#39; Springfield melt out da same day. Da Arkansas River League sends one last text to da Turnpike Accord: a picture of a bare yard.</p></li><li><p><strong>March 10.</strong> Da ministry declares da resurfacin&#39; season over. Da 4,000 staff go back to bein&#39; kids wit&#39; machines, an&#39; da machines go back into garages, numbered. Priya&#39;s map is down to 6 belligerents, all inside 30 miles of Kessler Court.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Hardly any on da rain days: da Border Commission rules dat rain is a truce. On Thursday an&#39; Friday da fightin&#39; is in da snow dat&#39;s left: da shady nort&#39; faces of da walls, where it&#39;s still 5 ft.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-monday-march-13-friday-march-17-spring-break\">Monday, March 13 – Friday, March 17: spring break</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Both sides know dis is da last all-day fightin&#39; da winter&#39;s gonna give &#39;em. Da North spends Sunday pullin&#39; everyting dat&#39;s left into one fortress on da shady side of Kessler Court, rebuildin&#39; da Frontier Wall&#39;s last 300 ft outta da slumped sections. Da South does da same 60 ft away.</p>\n<ul><li><p>Da last engines get moved into da court: 14 on da North, 11 on da South.</p></li><li><p>Da Lynn Lane Confederacy, da last outside belligerent, melts out Wednesday, March 15, an&#39; sends 6 kids to fight for da North on Thursday.</p></li></ul>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> Da Siege of Kessler Court runs all five days, 9:00 am to 5:30 pm, wit&#39; a cocoa truce at noon. It&#39;s da war like it started: two forts, one street, da same December lines, but wit&#39; 25 engines an&#39; 400 kids. Da last engine shot of da war gets fired on <strong>Thursday, March 16, at 4:44 pm</strong>: a North trebuchet at da South&#39;s capital wall, 260 ft, a hit. Friday gets fought by hand.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-monday-march-20-school\">Monday, March 20: school</h4>\n<p><strong>Gettin&#39; ready.</strong> Schools open back up after 28 school days closed: February 1 t&#39;rough March 10, den spring break. Da bus comes at 7:31 for da first time since January 31, an&#39; 6 kids on Kessler Court are standin&#39; on da snow wall stub by da mailboxes to catch it.</p>\n<p><strong>Da fightin&#39;.</strong> After school only, 3:25 to 5:00 pm, on 9&quot; of gray snow in da shade. About 600 rounds. Nobody keeps count of hits, for da first time since February 3, eh.</p>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-march-24-april-14-da-last-of-it\">March 24 – April 14: da last of it</h4>\n<ul><li><p><strong>March 24.</strong> Da yards are bare. Da last 9 ft of da Frontier Wall stands in da shade between #14 an&#39; #9, an&#39; both sides still claim it.</p></li><li><p><strong>April 1.</strong> Da wall stub&#39;s down to 3 ft. Owen an&#39; Tessa, each one alone, get seen checkin&#39; on it from dere own porches at 7:00 am.</p></li><li><p><strong>April 14, 3:20 pm.</strong> Da last plow pile in da empire, at da Kessler gate, is gone. <strong>(No Ammo, Eh)</strong></p></li></ul>\n<h4 id=\"ks-yp-what-came-after\">What came after</h4>\n<ul><li><p><strong>April – May.</strong> Da Border Commission keeps meetin&#39; at a lunch table till da last week of May. It hears 140 disputes over da winter an&#39; gets 113 of its rulin&#39;s accepted. Da 27 dat get rejected are all about February 25.</p></li><li><p><strong>May 19.</strong> Da South&#39;s chronicle gets finished: 41 pages, 3 maps, an&#39; its famous first line about da storm dat started it.</p></li><li><p><strong>Summer 2028.</strong> Lila copies all four treaties, da Imperial Gauge an&#39; da lines of succession into a t&#39;ree-ring binder. Rule 4&#39;s da last article of every treaty in it, in da same words. Priya keeps da printer runnin&#39; all summer an&#39; prints nuttin&#39; but spare clutch springs.</p></li><li><p><strong>Sunday, December 10, 2028, 9:40 pm.</strong> Da second anniversary, <strong>2 A.S.</strong> Two hundred an&#39; twelve kids stand for one minute at da Kessler mailboxes, where da gate was. Ada&#39;s 7 an&#39; don&#39;t remember any of it. Sam&#39;s 9 an&#39; remembers all of it, different.</p></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-how-da-record-goes-wrong\">How da record goes wrong</h2>\n<p>By February 25, roughly 30,000 kids are in da war, an&#39; fewer dan 20 of &#39;em were on Kessler Court on November 24, 2026. To everybody else, <strong>da storm</strong> means da Great Storm.</p>\n<p>Da South coalition&#39;s chronicler, a Pinecrest sixth-grader, opens da spring write-up wit&#39; a line from Saturday, February 12, da day da South got to All-Out Snow War, nine days after da snow quit:</p>\n<blockquote><p><em>The war has outlasted the storm that started it by nine days.</em></p></blockquote>\n<p>Dat sentence is da All-Out Snow War tier. It got written by somebody who wasn&#39;t dere, about da wrong storm, at a step da North passed da day before. From den on da record&#39;s got two origins: Dev&#39;s calendar counts from December 10, 2026, an&#39; da chronicle counts from February 2028. Neither one mentions November 24.</p>\n<p><strong>Where da ladder picks it up:</strong></p>\n<ul><li><p><strong>No Ammo, Eh:</strong> da older ones&#39; stories &quot;place it in a different year.&quot; Marcus says da t&#39;row hit; Lila says it never left Owen&#39;s hand.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da Founding Myths</strong> an&#39; <strong>da Stuff of Legend:</strong> eight versions of da first t&#39;row, an&#39; &quot;no record of the first throw survives,&quot; &#39;cause da real one&#39;s dated before da calendar even starts.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da Hereditary Aristocracy:</strong> command passes down by birth order, so for 31 hours da war gets run by da ones least able to remember November 24. Ada was 4 at da time.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da Settlement Law:</strong> Rule 4 gets copied forward into 412 articles an&#39; ends up da last one. Da law gets bound later as volume nine, an&#39; volume nine gets lost.</p></li><li><p><strong>Da Grudge Dat Got Handed Down:</strong> da cause survives only as &quot;volume nine, article four hundred and twelve,&quot; an&#39; four reconstructions can&#39;t agree which side got wronged. Da original question was never who got wronged. It was <strong>whether da snowball happened, an&#39; whose snow it was.</strong></p></li><li><p><strong>Da Spreader Schism:</strong> da war splits over whether laid snow is real snow or a forgery. Dat&#39;s Tessa&#39;s objection on November 24, argued by folks who never heard of November 24, ya know.</p></li></ul>\n<h2 id=\"ks-yp-ledger\">Ledger</h2>\n<div class=\"ks-table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>Year Zero (2026–27)</th><th>Year One (2027–28)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Key dates</td><td>Sketch Sep 12 · disputed t&#39;row Nov 24 · first flake Dec 10, 9:40 pm</td><td>Storm Jan 31 – Feb 3 · court crowned Fri Feb 25 · peak Fri Mar 3 · schools open back up Mon Mar 20 · last snow Apr 14</td></tr><tr><td>Storms</td><td>Dec 10–11 (2.5&quot;) · Jan 21–22 (4.5&quot;) · ice Feb 9 (0.1&quot;) · trace Mar 3, 2027</td><td><strong>40.0&quot; (6.0&quot; snowball + 34.0&quot; thundersnow)</strong>, after a trace on Dec 18, 2027; da last snow of da winter</td></tr><tr><td>Packable depth at peak</td><td>2.5&quot; · 4.5&quot;</td><td><strong>74.0&quot;</strong></td></tr><tr><td>Peak tiers</td><td>Buildin&#39; Forts (#84, Fri Dec 11) · Fortress an&#39; Siege (#102, Fri Jan 22)</td><td><strong>War Across Da Whole Continent (#208, Fri Mar 3)</strong>, after da Hereditary Aristocracy (#205) an&#39; da Client Kingdoms (#206) on Fri Feb 25 an&#39; da Spreader Cartels (#207) on Mon Feb 28</td></tr><tr><td>All-Out Snow War (#182)</td><td>—</td><td>Passed: North Fri Feb 11, South Sat Feb 12</td></tr><tr><td>Participants</td><td>11 → 18</td><td>30 → 300+ → 1,100 → about 2,200 in provinces → 60,000 hands at da Great Recut → 19 belligerents an&#39; about 400,000 counted t&#39;rowers on Mar 3</td></tr><tr><td>Main wall</td><td>30&quot; × 12&quot; × 16 ft → 40&quot; × 18&quot; × 20 ft</td><td>220 ft per alliance → <strong>550 × 14 × 8 ft</strong> per settlement → 1,650 ft at da capital</td></tr><tr><td>Longest work</td><td>—</td><td>Da Frontier Wall, 19 miles, right t&#39;rough da middle of Kessler Court; da front, 400 miles from Wichita to Little Rock</td></tr><tr><td>Machines</td><td>1 → 3 licensed + 4 knockoffs</td><td>14 licensed by January, all printed in da Rao basement → 1,100 at da Spreader Works, Coast to Coast → 4 cartel houses at 44 marshmallows a screw</td></tr><tr><td>Documents</td><td>Envelope (4 rules) → Manual (22 pp) → Rules of Kessler Court (9 rules)</td><td>4 treaties · Da Imperial Gauge · cartel price list · letters of alliance to 17 wars · South chronicle</td></tr><tr><td>Rule 4</td><td>Reserved</td><td>Last article of every treaty an&#39; every letter of alliance; still reserved</td></tr><tr><td>Who&#39;s in charge</td><td>Tessa · Owen</td><td>Ada, 6 an&#39; Sam, 8, for 31 hours each, den Tessa an&#39; Owen again</td></tr><tr><td>School days closed</td><td>—</td><td>28 (Feb 1 – Mar 10), den spring break</td></tr><tr><td>First to every tier</td><td>North</td><td>North</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n"};
